<p id="mwGw"><b id="mwHA">Donald John Trump</b> (born June 14, 1946) is an American politician, media personality, and businessman who is the 47th <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/President_of_the_United_States" title="President of the United States" id="mwHQ">president of the United States</a>.<!-- DO NOT CHANGE preceding sentence (including linking) without prior consensus; see [[Talk:Donald Trump#C70|consensus 70]]. --> A member of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Republican_Party_(United_States)" title="Republican Party (United States)" id="mwHg">Republican Party</a>, he served as the 45th president from 2017 to 2021.</p><!-- DO NOT CHANGE preceding sentence (including linking) without prior consensus; see [[Talk:Donald Trump#C70|consensus 70]]. -->

<p id="mwHw">Born into a wealthy New York City family, Trump graduated from the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/University_of_Pennsylvania" title="University of Pennsylvania" id="mwIA">University of Pennsylvania</a> in 1968 with a bachelor's degree in economics. He became the president of his family's real estate business in 1971, renamed it <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Trump_Organization" title="The Trump Organization" id="mwIQ">the Trump Organization</a>, and began acquiring and building skyscrapers, hotels, casinos, and golf courses. He launched side ventures, many licensing the Trump name, and filed for six business bankruptcies in the 1990s and 2000s. From 2004 to 2015, he hosted the reality television show <i id="mwIg"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Apprentice_(American_TV_series)" title="The Apprentice (American TV series)" id="mwIw">The Apprentice</a></i>, bolstering his reputation as a wealthy tycoon. Presenting himself as a political outsider, Trump won the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2016_United_States_presidential_election" title="2016 United States presidential election" id="mwJA">2016 presidential election</a> against <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Democratic_Party_(United_States)" title="Democratic Party (United States)" id="mwJQ">Democratic Party</a> nominee <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hillary_Clinton" title="Hillary Clinton" id="mwJg">Hillary Clinton</a>.</p>

<p id="mwJw">During <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_presidency_of_Donald_Trump" title="First presidency of Donald Trump" id="mwKA">his first presidency</a>, Trump imposed <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Executive_Order_13769" title="Executive Order 13769" id="mwKQ">a travel ban</a> on seven <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Islam_by_country#Countries" title="Islam by country" id="mwKg">Muslim-majority countries</a>, expanded the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mexico–United_States_border_wall" title="Mexico–United States border wall" id="mwKw">Mexico–United States border wall</a>, and enforced <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trump_administration_family_separation_policy" title="Trump administration family separation policy" id="mwLA">a family separation policy</a> on the border.<!-- This sentence must contain a summary of Trump's actions on immigration, including the Muslim travel ban, the wall, and the family separation policy; see [[Talk:Donald Trump#C52|consensus 52]]. --> He rolled back environmental and business regulations, signed the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tax_Cuts_and_Jobs_Act" title="Tax Cuts and Jobs Act" id="mwLQ">Tax Cuts and Jobs Act</a>, and appointed three Supreme Court justices. He withdrew the U.S. from agreements on climate and trade and started <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China–United_States_trade_war" title="China–United States trade war" id="mwLg">a trade war with China</a>. In response to the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/COVID-19_pandemic_in_the_United_States" title="COVID-19 pandemic in the United States" id="mwLw">COVID-19 pandemic</a> in 2020, he downplayed its severity, contradicted health officials, and signed the bipartisan <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CARES_Act" title="CARES Act" id="mwMA">CARES Act</a>. After losing the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2020_United_States_presidential_election" title="2020 United States presidential election" id="mwMQ">2020 presidential election</a> to <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joe_Biden" title="Joe Biden" id="mwMg">Joe Biden</a>, Trump refused to concede defeat and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Attempts_to_overturn_the_2020_United_States_presidential_election" title="Attempts to overturn the 2020 United States presidential election" id="mwMw">attempted to overturn the result</a>, resulting in the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/January_6_United_States_Capitol_attack" title="January 6 United States Capitol attack" id="mwNA">January<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwNQ"> </span>6 Capitol attack</a> in 2021. He was impeached twice<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwNg">—</span><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_impeachment_of_Donald_Trump" title="First impeachment of Donald Trump" id="mwNw">in 2019</a> for abuse of power and obstruction of Congress and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second_impeachment_of_Donald_Trump" title="Second impeachment of Donald Trump" id="mwOA">in 2021</a> for incitement of insurrection<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwOQ">—</span>and acquitted by the Senate both times.</p>

<p id="mwOg">In 2023, Trump was found liable in New York state civil cases <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/E._Jean_Carroll_v._Donald_J._Trump" title="E. Jean Carroll v. Donald J. Trump" id="mwOw">for sexual abuse and defamation</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_York_business_fraud_lawsuit_against_the_Trump_Organization" title="New York business fraud lawsuit against the Trump Organization" id="mwPA">for business fraud</a>. In May 2024, he was found guilty in a New York state court on 34 counts <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prosecution_of_Donald_Trump_in_New_York" title="Prosecution of Donald Trump in New York" id="mwPQ">of falsifying business records</a>, making him the first U.S. president convicted of a felony. After winning the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2024_United_States_presidential_election" title="2024 United States presidential election" id="mwPg">2024 presidential election</a> against Vice President <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kamala_Harris" title="Kamala Harris" id="mwPw">Kamala Harris</a>, he was given a no-penalty sentence, and two federal felony indictments for <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Federal_prosecution_of_Donald_Trump_(classified_documents_case)" title="Federal prosecution of Donald Trump (classified documents case)" id="mwQA">retention of classified documents</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Federal_prosecution_of_Donald_Trump_(election_obstruction_case)" title="Federal prosecution of Donald Trump (election obstruction case)" id="mwQQ">obstruction of the 2020 election</a> were dismissed <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prejudice_(legal_term)" title="Prejudice (legal term)" id="mwQg">without prejudice</a>.</p>

<p id="mwQw">Trump began <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second_presidency_of_Donald_Trump" title="Second presidency of Donald Trump" id="mwRA">his second presidency</a> by initiating <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025_United_States_federal_mass_layoffs" title="2025 United States federal mass layoffs" id="mwRQ">mass layoffs of federal workers</a>. He <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tariffs_in_the_second_Trump_administration" title="Tariffs in the second Trump administration" id="mwRg">imposed tariffs</a> on nearly all countries at the highest level since the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_Depression" title="Great Depression" id="mwRw">Great Depression</a> and signed the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/One_Big_Beautiful_Bill_Act" title="One Big Beautiful Bill Act" id="mwSA">One Big Beautiful Bill Act</a>. His administration's actions<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwSQ">—</span>including the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Targeting_of_political_opponents_and_civil_society_under_the_second_Trump_administration" title="Targeting of political opponents and civil society under the second Trump administration" id="mwSg">targeting of political opponents and civil society</a>, restriction of transgender rights, mass <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deportation_in_the_second_Trump_administration" title="Deportation in the second Trump administration" id="mwSw">deportation of undocumented immigrants</a>, and extensive use of executive orders<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwTA">—</span>have drawn over 550 <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Legal_affairs_of_the_second_Trump_presidency" title="Legal affairs of the second Trump presidency" id="mwTQ">lawsuits challenging their legality</a>. In Latin America, he pursued a campaign to <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_strikes_on_alleged_drug_traffickers_during_Operation_Southern_Spear" title="United States strikes on alleged drug traffickers during Operation Southern Spear" id="mwTg">attack alleged drug traffickers</a> and ordered <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_United_States_intervention_in_Venezuela" title="2026 United States intervention in Venezuela" id="mwTw">a military raid into Venezuela</a> to capture the country's president. In the Middle East, he authorized joint U.S.<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwUA">–</span>Israeli strikes on Iran that began the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_Iran_war" title="2026 Iran war" id="mwUQ">2026 Iran war</a>.</p>

<p id="mwUg">Since 2015, Trump's leadership style and political agenda<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwUw">—</span>often referred to as <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trumpism" title="Trumpism" id="mwVA">Trumpism</a><span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwVQ">—</span>have reshaped the Republican Party's identity. Many of his comments and actions have been <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Racial_views_of_Donald_Trump" title="Racial views of Donald Trump" id="mwVg">characterized as racist</a> or misogynistic.<!-- DO NOT CHANGE preceding sentence without prior consensus, see [[Talk:Donald Trump#C30|consensus 30]] and [[Talk:Donald Trump#C51|consensus 51]]. --> He has <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/False_or_misleading_statements_by_Donald_Trump" title="False or misleading statements by Donald Trump" id="mwVw">made many false or misleading statements</a> during his campaigns and presidency, to a degree unprecedented in American politics,<!-- DO NOT CHANGE preceding sentence without prior consensus, see [[Talk:Donald Trump#C49|consensus 49]]. --> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_conspiracy_theories_promoted_by_Donald_Trump" title="List of conspiracy theories promoted by Donald Trump" id="mwWA">has promoted conspiracy theories</a>.<!-- DO NOT CHANGE preceding sentence without prior consensus; see [[Talk:Donald Trump#C53|consensus 53]]. --> Trump's actions have been described by researchers as <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Authoritarianism_during_the_Trump_presidencies" title="Authoritarianism during the Trump presidencies" id="mwWQ">authoritarian</a> and contributing to <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Democratic_backsliding_in_the_United_States" title="Democratic backsliding in the United States" id="mwWg">democratic backsliding</a>.<!-- DO NOT REMOVE mention of authoritarianism and democratic backsliding; see [[Talk:Donald Trump/Archive 206#RfC: lead sentence on "authoritarian" actions and "contributing to democratic backsliding"]]. Per close, the info should remain and be attributed, not given undue prominence, and succinct. --> After his first term, scholars and historians <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="#Scholarly_rankings" class="mw-selflink-fragment" id="mwWw">ranked him</a> as one of the worst presidents in American history.</p><!-- DO NOT CHANGE preceding sentence without prior consensus; see [[Talk:Donald Trump#C54|consensus 54]]. -->

<meta property="mw:PageProp/toc" id="mwXA" data-mw='{"autoGenerated":true}'/></section><section data-mw-section-id="1" id="mwXQ" aria-labelledby="Early_life_and_education"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id="Early_life_and_education">Early life and education</h2></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt63" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwXg" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Early life and education of Donald Trump"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt64" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt63" id="mwXw">Main article: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Early_life_and_education_of_Donald_Trump" title="Early life and education of Donald Trump">Early life and education of Donald Trump</a></div>

<figure class="mw-default-size mw-halign-left" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwYA"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Donald_Trump_NYMA.jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwYQ"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Donald_Trump_NYMA.jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/Donald_Trump_NYMA.jpg/250px-Donald_Trump_NYMA.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/Donald_Trump_NYMA.jpg/330px-Donald_Trump_NYMA.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" alt="A black-and-white photograph of Trump as a teenager, smiling, wearing a dark pseudo-military uniform with three ribbons and a white shoulder strap" data-file-width="780" data-file-height="1024" data-file-type="bitmap" height="197" width="150" class="mw-file-element mw-file-upright" style="--mw-file-upright: 0.6" id="mwYg"/></a><figcaption id="mwYw">1964 yearbook photo</figcaption></figure><!-- DO NOT MOVE this image to the right. Left alignment is required for correct vertical alignment in some user configurations. -->

<p id="mwZA">Donald John Trump was born on June<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwZQ"> </span>14, 1946, at <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jamaica_Hospital" title="Jamaica Hospital" class="mw-redirect" id="mwZg">Jamaica Hospital</a> in the New York City borough of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Queens" title="Queens" id="mwZw">Queens</a>, the fourth child of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fred_Trump" title="Fred Trump" id="mwaA">Fred Trump</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mary_Anne_MacLeod_Trump" title="Mary Anne MacLeod Trump" id="mwaQ">Mary Anne MacLeod Trump</a>.<sup about="#mwt65" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher201730,_37_1-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEKranishFisher201730, 37"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher201730,_37-1"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Kranish"},"2":{"wt":"Fisher"},"3":{"wt":"2017"},"pp":{"wt":"30, 37"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher201730,_37-1" id="mwag"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwaw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwbA">[</span>1<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwbQ">]</span></span></a></sup> He is of German and Scottish descent.<sup about="#mwt68" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher2017v_2-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEKranishFisher2017v"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher2017v-2"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Kranish"},"2":{"wt":"Fisher"},"3":{"wt":"2017"},"p":{"wt":"v"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher2017v-2" id="mwbg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwbw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwcA">[</span>2<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwcQ">]</span></span></a></sup> He grew up with his older siblings, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maryanne_Trump_Barry" title="Maryanne Trump Barry" id="mwcg">Maryanne</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fred_Trump_Jr." title="Fred Trump Jr." id="mwcw">Fred Jr</a>, and Elizabeth, and his younger brother, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_Trump" title="Robert Trump" id="mwdA">Robert</a>, in a 23-room mansion in the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jamaica_Estates" title="Jamaica Estates" class="mw-redirect" id="mwdQ">Jamaica Estates</a> neighborhood of Queens.<sup about="#mwt73" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-3" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-3"}}'><a href="#cite_note-3" id="mwdg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwdw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mweA">[</span>3<span class="cite-bracket" id="mweQ">]</span></span></a></sup></p> 

<p id="mweg">As favorite son,<sup about="#mwt74" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBuettnerCraig2024302_4-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBuettnerCraig2024302"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBuettnerCraig2024302-4"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Buettner"},"2":{"wt":"Craig"},"3":{"wt":"2024"},"p":{"wt":"302"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBuettnerCraig2024302-4" id="mwew"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwfA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwfQ">[</span>4<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwfg">]</span></span></a></sup> Donald competed<sup about="#mwt77" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEO'Brien2005a49_5-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEO&apos;Brien2005a49"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEO&apos;Brien2005a49-5"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"O&apos;Brien"},"2":{"wt":"2005a"},"p":{"wt":"49"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEO'Brien2005a49-5" id="mwfw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwgA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwgQ">[</span>5<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwgg">]</span></span></a></sup> with his taskmaster father yet considered him to be his best friend.<sup about="#mwt80" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher2017272_6-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEKranishFisher2017272"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher2017272-6"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Kranish"},"2":{"wt":"Fisher"},"3":{"wt":"2017"},"p":{"wt":"272"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher2017272-6" id="mwgw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwhA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwhQ">[</span>6<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwhg">]</span></span></a></sup> Fred passed along to Donald his work ethic<sup about="#mwt83" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher2017272_6-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEKranishFisher2017272"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher2017272-6"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Kranish"},"2":{"wt":"Fisher"},"3":{"wt":"2017"},"p":{"wt":"272"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher2017272-6" id="mwhw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwiA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwiQ">[</span>6<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwig">]</span></span></a></sup> and the axiom to bring in projects "on time and under budget".<sup about="#mwt86" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEO'Brien2005a47_7-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEO&apos;Brien2005a47"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEO&apos;Brien2005a47-7"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"O&apos;Brien"},"2":{"wt":"2005a"},"p":{"wt":"47"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEO'Brien2005a47-7" id="mwiw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwjA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwjQ">[</span>7<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwjg">]</span></span></a></sup> He told him repeatedly to be "a killer" and that he was "a king".<sup about="#mwt89" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEHurt_III199313_8-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEHurt III199313"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEHurt_III199313-8"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Hurt III"},"2":{"wt":"1993"},"p":{"wt":"13"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEHurt_III199313-8" id="mwjw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwkA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwkQ">[</span>8<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwkg">]</span></span></a></sup> Although Trump never chose to acknowledge his debt, he would rely on his father's business sense<sup about="#mwt92" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBarrett201613_9-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBarrett201613"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBarrett201613-9"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Barrett"},"2":{"wt":"2016"},"p":{"wt":"13"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBarrett201613-9" id="mwkw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwlA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwlQ">[</span>9<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwlg">]</span></span></a></sup> and financial backing for years to come.<sup about="#mwt95" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEO'Brien2005a51_10-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEO&apos;Brien2005a51"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEO&apos;Brien2005a51-10"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"O&apos;Brien"},"2":{"wt":"2005a"},"p":{"wt":"51"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEO'Brien2005a51-10" id="mwlw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwmA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwmQ">[</span>10<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwmg">]</span></span></a></sup> To sidestep taxes, Fred paid his children each about $20,000 a year, equivalent to $265,000 a year in 2024. Trump was a millionaire in inflation-adjusted dollars by age eight.<sup about="#mwt98" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBuettnerCraig202430–31_11-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBuettnerCraig202430–31"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBuettnerCraig202430–31-11"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Buettner"},"2":{"wt":"Craig"},"3":{"wt":"2024"},"pp":{"wt":"30–31"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBuettnerCraig202430–31-11" id="mwmw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwnA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwnQ">[</span>11<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwng">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwnw">Trump attended the private <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kew-Forest_School" title="Kew-Forest School" id="mwoA">Kew-Forest School</a> through seventh grade. His father enrolled him in the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_York_Military_Academy" title="New York Military Academy" id="mwoQ">New York Military Academy</a>, a private boarding school, from eighth to twelfth grade.<sup about="#mwt101" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher201733,_38,_45_12-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEKranishFisher201733, 38, 45"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher201733,_38,_45-12"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Kranish"},"2":{"wt":"Fisher"},"3":{"wt":"2017"},"pp":{"wt":"33, 38, 45"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher201733,_38,_45-12" id="mwog"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwow"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwpA">[</span>12<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwpQ">]</span></span></a></sup> The academy pushed students into sports<sup about="#mwt104" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBuettnerCraig202466_13-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBuettnerCraig202466"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBuettnerCraig202466-13"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Buettner"},"2":{"wt":"Craig"},"3":{"wt":"2024"},"p":{"wt":"66"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBuettnerCraig202466-13" id="mwpg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwpw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwqA">[</span>13<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwqQ">]</span></span></a></sup> and taught the imperative of winning.<sup about="#mwt107" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher201742_14-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEKranishFisher201742"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher201742-14"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Kranish"},"2":{"wt":"Fisher"},"3":{"wt":"2017"},"p":{"wt":"42"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher201742-14" id="mwqg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwqw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwrA">[</span>14<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwrQ">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwrg">Trump considered attending film school in California but, to be closer to home, enrolled at <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fordham_University" title="Fordham University" id="mwrw">Fordham University</a> in 1964.<sup about="#mwt110" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher201745_15-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEKranishFisher201745"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher201745-15"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Kranish"},"2":{"wt":"Fisher"},"3":{"wt":"2017"},"pp":{"wt":"45"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher201745-15" id="mwsA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwsQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwsg">[</span>15<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwsw">]</span></span></a></sup> He participated in the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reserve_Officers'_Training_Corps" title="Reserve Officers' Training Corps" id="mwtA">Reserve Officers' Training Corps</a> during his first year, attending classes in a military uniform every Wednesday, but dropped it in his second year.<sup about="#mwt113" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBuettnerCraig202499_16-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBuettnerCraig202499"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBuettnerCraig202499-16"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Buettner"},"2":{"wt":"Craig"},"3":{"wt":"2024"},"p":{"wt":"99"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBuettnerCraig202499-16" id="mwtQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwtg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwtw">[</span>16<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwuA">]</span></span></a></sup> In his junior year, he transferred to the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wharton_School" title="Wharton School" id="mwuQ">Wharton School</a> of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/University_of_Pennsylvania" title="University of Pennsylvania" id="mwug">University of Pennsylvania</a>, most often commuting to his father's office on weekends, and graduated in May 1968 with a Bachelor of Science in economics.<sup about="#mwt116" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher201747–50_17-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEKranishFisher201747–50"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher201747–50-17"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Kranish"},"2":{"wt":"Fisher"},"3":{"wt":"2017"},"pp":{"wt":"47–50"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher201747–50-17" id="mwuw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwvA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwvQ">[</span>17<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwvg">]</span></span></a></sup> Contrary to his statements that he was top of his class with the highest grades possible, Wharton's published academic honors and dean's list do not include his name.<sup about="#mwt119" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher201747_18-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEKranishFisher201747"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher201747-18"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Kranish"},"2":{"wt":"Fisher"},"3":{"wt":"2017"},"p":{"wt":"47"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher201747-18" id="mwvw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwwA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwwQ">[</span>18<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwwg">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt130" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-19" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-19"}}'><a href="#cite_note-19" id="mwww"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwxA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwxQ">[</span>19<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwxg">]</span></span></a></sup> By the time he went to Wharton, he was considering a career in real estate.<sup about="#mwt122" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBarrett201675_20-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBarrett201675"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBarrett201675-20"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Barrett"},"2":{"wt":"2016"},"p":{"wt":"75"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBarrett201675-20" id="mwxw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwyA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwyQ">[</span>20<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwyg">]</span></span></a></sup> He was exempted from the draft during the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vietnam_War" title="Vietnam War" id="mwyw">Vietnam War</a> due to a claim of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Calcaneal_spur" title="Calcaneal spur" id="mwzA">bone spurs</a> in his heels.<sup about="#mwt125" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher201748_21-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEKranishFisher201748"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher201748-21"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Kranish"},"2":{"wt":"Fisher"},"3":{"wt":"2017"},"p":{"wt":"48"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher201748-21" id="mwzQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwzg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwzw">[</span>21<span class="cite-bracket" id="mw0A">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="2" id="mw0Q" aria-labelledby="Business_career"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id="Business_career">Business career</h2></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt131" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mw0g" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Business career of Donald Trump"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt132" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt131" id="mw0w">Main article: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Business_career_of_Donald_Trump" title="Business career of Donald Trump">Business career of Donald Trump</a></div>

<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt133" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mw1A" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"further","href":"./Template:Further"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Business projects of Donald Trump in Russia"},"2":{"wt":"Tax returns of Donald Trump"},"3":{"wt":"The Trump Organization"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt134" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt133" id="mw1Q">Further information: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Business_projects_of_Donald_Trump_in_Russia" title="Business projects of Donald Trump in Russia">Business projects of Donald Trump in Russia</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tax_returns_of_Donald_Trump" title="Tax returns of Donald Trump">Tax returns of Donald Trump</a>, and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Trump_Organization" title="The Trump Organization">The Trump Organization</a></div>

<section data-mw-section-id="3" id="mw1g" aria-labelledby="Real_estate"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Real_estate">Real estate</h3></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt135" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mw1w" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"see also","href":"./Template:See_also"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"The Trump Organization#Real estate"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt136" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt135" id="mw2A">See also: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Trump_Organization#Real_estate" title="The Trump Organization">The Trump Organization §<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>Real estate</a></div>
<p id="mw2Q">Starting in 1968, Trump was employed at Trump Management, his father's real estate company, which oversaw more than fourteen thousand middle-class apartments in complexes Fred had built in Queens, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Staten_Island" title="Staten Island" id="mw2g">Staten Island</a>, and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brooklyn" title="Brooklyn" id="mw2w">Brooklyn</a>.<sup about="#mwt137" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher201751–53_22-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEKranishFisher201751–53"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher201751–53-22"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Kranish"},"2":{"wt":"Fisher"},"3":{"wt":"2017"},"pp":{"wt":"51–53"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher201751–53-22" id="mw3A"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mw3Q"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mw3g">[</span>22<span class="cite-bracket" id="mw3w">]</span></span></a></sup> For about five years,<sup about="#mwt140" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEO'Brien2005a51_10-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEO&apos;Brien2005a51"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEO&apos;Brien2005a51-10"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"O&apos;Brien"},"2":{"wt":"2005a"},"p":{"wt":"51"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEO'Brien2005a51-10" id="mw4A"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mw4Q"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mw4g">[</span>10<span class="cite-bracket" id="mw4w">]</span></span></a></sup> he managed apartments, made repairs, and collected rent.<sup about="#mwt143" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBarrett201676_23-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBarrett201676"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBarrett201676-23"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Barrett"},"2":{"wt":"2016"},"p":{"wt":"76"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBarrett201676-23" id="mw5A"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mw5Q"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mw5g">[</span>23<span class="cite-bracket" id="mw5w">]</span></span></a></sup> Trump asked his father to expand to <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan" title="Manhattan" id="mw6A">Manhattan</a>, where prices were higher, but his father was content in the outer boroughs.<sup about="#mwt146" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEO'Brien2005a51_10-2" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEO&apos;Brien2005a51"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEO&apos;Brien2005a51-10"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"O&apos;Brien"},"2":{"wt":"2005a"},"p":{"wt":"51"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEO'Brien2005a51-10" id="mw6Q"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mw6g"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mw6w">[</span>10<span class="cite-bracket" id="mw7A">]</span></span></a></sup> In 1971, he moved to Manhattan, where he planned to relocate the business<sup about="#mwt149" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBarrett201679_24-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBarrett201679"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBarrett201679-24"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Barrett"},"2":{"wt":"2016"},"p":{"wt":"79"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBarrett201679-24" id="mw7Q"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mw7g"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mw7w">[</span>24<span class="cite-bracket" id="mw8A">]</span></span></a></sup> and commuted to his father's office.<sup about="#mwt152" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher201759_25-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEKranishFisher201759"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher201759-25"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Kranish"},"2":{"wt":"Fisher"},"3":{"wt":"2017"},"p":{"wt":"59"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher201759-25" id="mw8Q"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mw8g"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mw8w">[</span>25<span class="cite-bracket" id="mw9A">]</span></span></a></sup> That year, his father made himself chairman and Trump president.<sup about="#mwt155" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBarrett201675_20-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBarrett201675"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBarrett201675-20"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Barrett"},"2":{"wt":"2016"},"p":{"wt":"75"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBarrett201675-20" id="mw9Q"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mw9g"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mw9w">[</span>20<span class="cite-bracket" id="mw-A">]</span></span></a></sup> Trump began using the Trump Organization as an umbrella for the corporate names of his father's businesses.<sup about="#mwt158" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBlair2015250_26-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBlair2015250"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBlair2015250-26"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Blair"},"2":{"wt":"2015"},"p":{"wt":"250"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBlair2015250-26" id="mw-Q"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mw-g"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mw-w">[</span>26<span class="cite-bracket" id="mw_A">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mw_Q"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roy_Cohn" title="Roy Cohn" id="mw_g">Roy Cohn</a>, Trump's most important early influence after his father,<sup about="#mwt161" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBarrett201681_27-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBarrett201681"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBarrett201681-27"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Barrett"},"2":{"wt":"2016"},"p":{"wt":"81"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBarrett201681-27" id="mw_w"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAQA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAQE">[</span>27<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAQI">]</span></span></a></sup> was his <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fixer_(person)" title="Fixer (person)" id="mwAQM">fixer</a>, lawyer, and mentor<sup about="#mwt164" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBarrett2016126_28-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBarrett2016126"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBarrett2016126-28"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Barrett"},"2":{"wt":"2016"},"p":{"wt":"126"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBarrett2016126-28" id="mwAQQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAQU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAQY">[</span>28<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAQc">]</span></span></a></sup> for 13 years in the 1970s and 1980s.<sup about="#mwt207" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Mahler2016Cohn_29-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Mahler2016Cohn"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Mahler2016Cohn-29"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Mahler2016Cohn-29" id="mwAQg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAQk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAQo">[</span>29<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAQs">]</span></span></a></sup> Cohn taught Trump that life is transactional,<sup about="#mwt167" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEHaberman202233_30-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEHaberman202233"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEHaberman202233-30"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Haberman"},"2":{"wt":"2022"},"p":{"wt":"33"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEHaberman202233-30" id="mwAQw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAQ0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAQ4">[</span>30<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAQ8">]</span></span></a></sup> that popular opinion is more important than a court of law,<sup about="#mwt170" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBirdGoldmark2026265_31-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBirdGoldmark2026265"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBirdGoldmark2026265-31"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Bird"},"2":{"wt":"Goldmark"},"3":{"wt":"2026"},"p":{"wt":"265"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBirdGoldmark2026265-31" id="mwARA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwARE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwARI">[</span>31<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwARM">]</span></span></a></sup> that fabrication can drown out facts,<sup about="#mwt173" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBirdGoldmark2026266_32-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBirdGoldmark2026266"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBirdGoldmark2026266-32"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Bird"},"2":{"wt":"Goldmark"},"3":{"wt":"2026"},"p":{"wt":"266"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBirdGoldmark2026266-32" id="mwARQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwARU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwARY">[</span>32<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwARc">]</span></span></a></sup> to leave no paper trail,<sup about="#mwt176" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBirdGoldmark2026272_33-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBirdGoldmark2026272"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBirdGoldmark2026272-33"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Bird"},"2":{"wt":"Goldmark"},"3":{"wt":"2026"},"p":{"wt":"272"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBirdGoldmark2026272-33" id="mwARg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwARk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwARo">[</span>33<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwARs">]</span></span></a></sup> and to evade paying bills and taxes.<sup about="#mwt179" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBirdGoldmark2026273–274_34-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBirdGoldmark2026273–274"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBirdGoldmark2026273–274-34"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Bird"},"2":{"wt":"Goldmark"},"3":{"wt":"2026"},"pp":{"wt":"273–274"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBirdGoldmark2026273–274-34" id="mwARw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAR0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAR4">[</span>34<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAR8">]</span></span></a></sup> Trump showed a propensity for litigation, no matter the outcome and cost; even when he lost, he described the case as a win.<sup about="#mwt182" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBuettnerCraig2024126_35-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBuettnerCraig2024126"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBuettnerCraig2024126-35"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Buettner"},"2":{"wt":"Craig"},"3":{"wt":"2024"},"p":{"wt":"126"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBuettnerCraig2024126-35" id="mwASA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwASE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwASI">[</span>35<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwASM">]</span></span></a></sup>In 1973, Cohn helped Trump countersue the U.S. government for $100<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwASQ"> </span>million (equivalent to $<span about="#mwt185" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwASU" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"inflation","href":"./Template:Inflation"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"US"},"2":{"wt":"100"},"3":{"wt":"1973"}},"i":0}}]}'>725</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwASY"> </span>million in <span about="#mwt186" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwASc" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"inflation/year","href":"./Template:Inflation/year"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"US"}},"i":0}}]}'>2025</span><sup about="#mwt187" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-inflation-US_36-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"inflation-US","group":""},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-inflation-US-36"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"inflation/fn","href":"./Template:Inflation/fn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"US"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-inflation-US-36" id="mwASg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwASk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwASo">[</span>36<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwASs">]</span></span></a></sup>) over its charges that Trump's properties had discriminated against Black applicants and tenants. The case was settled in a consent decree agreeing to desegregate, which the Trumps ended up in court four years later for violating.<sup about="#mwt196" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBarrett201682–84_37-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBarrett201682–84"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBarrett201682–84-37"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Barrett"},"2":{"wt":"2016"},"p":{"wt":"82–84"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBarrett201682–84-37" id="mwASw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAS0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAS4">[</span>37<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAS8">]</span></span></a></sup> Cohn was a <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Consigliere" title="Consigliere" id="mwATA">consigliere</a> and intermediary with whom Trump continued his father's connection to the New York Mafia,<sup about="#mwt199" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBirdGoldmark2026263,_270_38-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBirdGoldmark2026263, 270"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBirdGoldmark2026263,_270-38"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Bird"},"2":{"wt":"Goldmark"},"3":{"wt":"2026"},"pp":{"wt":"263, 270"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBirdGoldmark2026263,_270-38" id="mwATE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwATI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwATM">[</span>38<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwATQ">]</span></span></a></sup> benefiting from its control of the building trade unions.<sup about="#mwt202" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEJohnston201645–46_39-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEJohnston201645–46"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEJohnston201645–46-39"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Johnston"},"2":{"wt":"2016"},"pp":{"wt":"45–46"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEJohnston201645–46-39" id="mwATU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwATY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwATc">[</span>39<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwATg">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwATk">Between 1991 and 2009, Trump filed for <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chapter_11" title="Chapter 11" class="mw-redirect" id="mwATo">Chapter 11</a> bankruptcy protection for six of his businesses: the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plaza_Hotel" title="Plaza Hotel" id="mwATs">Plaza Hotel</a> in Manhattan, the casinos in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atlantic_City,_New_Jersey" title="Atlantic City, New Jersey" id="mwATw">Atlantic City, New Jersey</a>, and the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trump_Hotels_&amp;_Casino_Resorts" title="Trump Hotels &amp; Casino Resorts" class="mw-redirect" id="mwAT0">Trump Hotels &amp; Casino Resorts</a> company.<sup about="#mwt210" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-40" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-40"}}'><a href="#cite_note-40" id="mwAT4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAT8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAUA">[</span>40<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAUE">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt211" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-TW_41-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"TW"}}'><a href="#cite_note-TW-41" id="mwAUI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAUM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAUQ">[</span>41<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAUU">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwAUY">In 1992 and 1994, Trump, working with several relatives, formed a shell company for paying the vendors providing services and supplies for Trump's rental units, then billing those services and supplies to Trump Management with significant markups; the increased costs were used to get state approval for increasing the rents of his rent-stabilized units. Besides inflating rents, the schemes served to transfer assets from Fred to his children and nephew and lower their tax burden.<sup about="#mwt214" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Tax_Schemes_42-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Tax_Schemes"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Tax_Schemes-42"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Tax_Schemes-42" id="mwAUc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAUg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAUk">[</span>42<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAUo">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<section data-mw-section-id="4" id="mwAUs" aria-labelledby="Manhattan_and_Chicago_developments"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading4"><h4 id="Manhattan_and_Chicago_developments">Manhattan and Chicago developments</h4></div>
<figure class="mw-default-size mw-halign-left" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwAUw"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Donald_Trump_with_model_of_Television_City.jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwAU0"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Donald_Trump_with_model_of_Television_City.jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Donald_Trump_with_model_of_Television_City.jpg/250px-Donald_Trump_with_model_of_Television_City.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Donald_Trump_with_model_of_Television_City.jpg/500px-Donald_Trump_with_model_of_Television_City.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" alt="Donald Trump, in a black suit and red tie, standing in from of a model of Manhattan development projects" data-file-width="3135" data-file-height="4559" data-file-type="bitmap" height="276" width="190" class="mw-file-element mw-file-upright" style="--mw-file-upright: 0.75" id="mwAU4"/></a><figcaption id="mwAU8">In 1985 with a model of one of his aborted Manhattan development projects<sup about="#mwt217" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-43" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-43"}}'><a href="#cite_note-43" id="mwAVA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAVE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAVI">[</span>43<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAVM">]</span></span></a></sup></figcaption></figure>
<p id="mwAVQ">Trump gained public attention in 1978 with the launch of his family's first Manhattan venture: the renovation of the derelict <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hyatt_Grand_Central_New_York#Commodore_Hotel" title="Hyatt Grand Central New York" id="mwAVU">Commodore Hotel</a>, adjacent to <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grand_Central_Terminal" title="Grand Central Terminal" id="mwAVY">Grand Central Terminal</a>.<sup about="#mwt226" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-44" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-44"}}'><a href="#cite_note-44" id="mwAVc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAVg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAVk">[</span>44<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAVo">]</span></span></a></sup> Lacking the wherewithal to finance the venture, Trump was able to call on his father and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hyatt" title="Hyatt" id="mwAVs">Hyatt</a> to guarantee the $70<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwAVw"> </span>million construction loan.<sup about="#mwt229" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Rich_NYMag_45-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Rich NYMag"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Rich_NYMag-45"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Rich_NYMag-45" id="mwAV0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAV4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAV8">[</span>45<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAWA">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt232" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-46" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-46"}}'><a href="#cite_note-46" id="mwAWE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAWI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAWM">[</span>46<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAWQ">]</span></span></a></sup> With Cohn's help and their political connections, he received a 40-year tax break<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwAWU">—</span>which is still, as of 2016, New York City's longest tax abatement<sup about="#mwt235" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-47" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-47"}}'><a href="#cite_note-47" id="mwAWY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAWc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAWg">[</span>47<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAWk">]</span></span></a></sup><span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwAWo">—</span>a prize worth over $410<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwAWs"> </span>million.<sup about="#mwt238" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-48" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-48"}}'><a href="#cite_note-48" id="mwAWw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAW0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAW4">[</span>48<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAW8">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt218" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBirdGoldmark2026269–271_49-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBirdGoldmark2026269–271"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBirdGoldmark2026269–271-49"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Bird"},"2":{"wt":"Goldmark"},"3":{"wt":"2026"},"pp":{"wt":"269–271"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBirdGoldmark2026269–271-49" id="mwAXA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAXE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAXI">[</span>49<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAXM">]</span></span></a></sup> The hotel reopened in 1980 as the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hyatt_Grand_Central_New_York" title="Hyatt Grand Central New York" id="mwAXQ">Grand Hyatt Hotel</a>.<sup about="#mwt221" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher2017[httpsbooksgooglecombooksidx2jUDQAAQBAJpgPA84_84]_50-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEKranishFisher2017[httpsbooksgooglecombooksidx2jUDQAAQBAJpgPA84 84]"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher2017[httpsbooksgooglecombooksidx2jUDQAAQBAJpgPA84_84]-50"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Kranish"},"2":{"wt":"Fisher"},"3":{"wt":"2017"},"p":{"wt":"[https://books.google.com/books?id=x2jUDQAAQBAJ&amp;pg=PA84 84]"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher2017[httpsbooksgooglecombooksidx2jUDQAAQBAJpgPA84_84]-50" id="mwAXU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAXY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAXc">[</span>50<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAXg">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwAXk">That same year, he obtained rights to develop <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trump_Tower" title="Trump Tower" id="mwAXo">Trump Tower</a>, a mixed-use skyscraper in Midtown Manhattan.<sup about="#mwt247" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-51" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-51"}}'><a href="#cite_note-51" id="mwAXs"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAXw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAX0">[</span>51<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAX4">]</span></span></a></sup> The building houses the headquarters of the Trump Corporation and Trump's <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Political_action_committee" title="Political action committee" id="mwAX8">political action committee</a> (PAC) and was his primary residence until 2019.<sup about="#mwt250" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-moved_52-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"moved"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-moved-52"}}'><a href="#cite_note-moved-52" id="mwAYA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAYE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAYI">[</span>52<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAYM">]</span></span></a></sup> Cohn obtained the zoning and building permits, brought in his mob partners to pour concrete, and sold apartments, creating a market for luxury Trump spaces.<sup about="#mwt239" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBirdGoldmark2026280_53-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBirdGoldmark2026280"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBirdGoldmark2026280-53"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Bird"},"2":{"wt":"Goldmark"},"3":{"wt":"2026"},"p":{"wt":"280"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBirdGoldmark2026280-53" id="mwAYQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAYU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAYY">[</span>53<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAYc">]</span></span></a></sup> He delivered oral arguments to appellate court, securing a $20<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwAYg"> </span>million <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tax_abatement" title="Tax abatement" class="mw-redirect" id="mwAYk">tax abatement</a>.<sup about="#mwt242" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBirdGoldmark2026281_54-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBirdGoldmark2026281"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBirdGoldmark2026281-54"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Bird"},"2":{"wt":"Goldmark"},"3":{"wt":"2026"},"p":{"wt":"281"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBirdGoldmark2026281-54" id="mwAYo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAYs"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAYw">[</span>54<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAY0">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwAY4">In 1988, Trump acquired the Plaza Hotel with a loan from a consortium  of 16 banks.<sup about="#mwt253" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-55" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-55"}}'><a href="#cite_note-55" id="mwAY8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAZA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAZE">[</span>55<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAZI">]</span></span></a></sup> The hotel filed for bankruptcy protection in 1992, and a reorganization plan was approved a month later, with the banks taking control of the property.<sup about="#mwt256" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-56" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-56"}}'><a href="#cite_note-56" id="mwAZM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAZQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAZU">[</span>56<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAZY">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwAZc">In 1995, Trump defaulted on over $3<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwAZg"> </span>billion of bank loans, and the lenders seized the Plaza Hotel along with most of his other properties in a "vast and humiliating restructuring" that allowed him to avoid personal bankruptcy.<sup about="#mwt259" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-plaza_57-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"plaza"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-plaza-57"}}'><a href="#cite_note-plaza-57" id="mwAZk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAZo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAZs">[</span>57<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAZw">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt262" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-58" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-58"}}'><a href="#cite_note-58" id="mwAZ0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAZ4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAZ8">[</span>58<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAaA">]</span></span></a></sup> Trump's last major construction project was the 92-story mixed-use <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trump_International_Hotel_and_Tower_(Chicago)" title="Trump International Hotel and Tower (Chicago)" id="mwAaE">Trump International Hotel and Tower</a> in Chicago, which opened in 2008. In 2024, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trump_International_Hotel_and_Tower_(Chicago)#Tax_deductions" title="Trump International Hotel and Tower (Chicago)" id="mwAaI"><i id="mwAaM">The New York Times</i> and ProPublica reported</a> that the Internal Revenue Service was investigating whether he had twice written off losses incurred through construction cost overruns and lagging sales of residential units in the building he had declared to be worthless on his 2008 tax return.<sup about="#mwt265" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-59" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-59"}}'><a href="#cite_note-59" id="mwAaQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAaU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAaY">[</span>59<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAac">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="5" id="mwAag" aria-labelledby="Atlantic_City_casinos"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading4"><h4 id="Atlantic_City_casinos">Atlantic City casinos</h4></div>
<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwAak"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Trump_Taj_Mahal,_2007.jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwAao"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Trump_Taj_Mahal,_2007.jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/Trump_Taj_Mahal%2C_2007.jpg/250px-Trump_Taj_Mahal%2C_2007.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/Trump_Taj_Mahal%2C_2007.jpg/500px-Trump_Taj_Mahal%2C_2007.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" alt="The entrance of the Trump Taj Mahal, a casino in Atlantic City. It has motifs evocative of the Taj Mahal in India." data-file-width="2592" data-file-height="1944" data-file-type="bitmap" height="188" width="250" class="mw-file-element" id="mwAas"/></a><figcaption id="mwAaw">Entrance of the Trump Taj Mahal (now the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hard_Rock_Hotel_&amp;_Casino_Atlantic_City" title="Hard Rock Hotel &amp; Casino Atlantic City" id="mwAa0">Hard Rock Hotel &amp; Casino</a>) in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atlantic_City" title="Atlantic City" class="mw-redirect" id="mwAa4">Atlantic City</a>, New Jersey</figcaption></figure>
<p id="mwAa8">In 1984, Trump opened <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harrah's_at_Trump_Plaza" title="Harrah's at Trump Plaza" class="mw-redirect" id="mwAbA">Harrah's at Trump Plaza</a>, a hotel and casino, with financing and management help from the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Holiday_Corporation" title="Holiday Corporation" class="mw-redirect" id="mwAbE">Holiday Corporation</a>.<sup about="#mwt272" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-fall_60-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"fall"}}'><a href="#cite_note-fall-60" id="mwAbI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAbM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAbQ">[</span>60<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAbU">]</span></span></a></sup> It was unprofitable, and he paid Holiday $70<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwAbY"> </span>million in May 1986 to take sole control.<sup about="#mwt266" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher2017[httpsbooksgooglecombooksidx2jUDQAAQBAJpgPA128_128]_61-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEKranishFisher2017[httpsbooksgooglecombooksidx2jUDQAAQBAJpgPA128 128]"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher2017[httpsbooksgooglecombooksidx2jUDQAAQBAJpgPA128_128]-61"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Kranish"},"2":{"wt":"Fisher"},"3":{"wt":"2017"},"p":{"wt":"[https://books.google.com/books?id=x2jUDQAAQBAJ&amp;pg=PA128 128]"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher2017[httpsbooksgooglecombooksidx2jUDQAAQBAJpgPA128_128]-61" id="mwAbc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAbg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAbk">[</span>61<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAbo">]</span></span></a></sup> In 1985, he bought the unopened Atlantic City Hilton Hotel and renamed it <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trump's_Castle" title="Trump's Castle" class="mw-redirect" id="mwAbs">Trump's Castle</a>.<sup about="#mwt275" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-62" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-62"}}'><a href="#cite_note-62" id="mwAbw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAb0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAb4">[</span>62<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAb8">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt269" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBlair2015351_63-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBlair2015351"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBlair2015351-63"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Blair"},"2":{"wt":"2015"},"p":{"wt":"351"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBlair2015351-63" id="mwAcA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAcE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAcI">[</span>63<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAcM">]</span></span></a></sup> Both casinos filed for Chapter 11 bankruptcy protection in 1992.<sup about="#mwt278" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-64" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-64"}}'><a href="#cite_note-64" id="mwAcQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAcU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAcY">[</span>64<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAcc">]</span></span></a></sup> Trump bought a third Atlantic City venue in 1988, the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trump_Taj_Mahal" title="Trump Taj Mahal" class="mw-redirect" id="mwAcg">Trump Taj Mahal</a>. It was financed with $675<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwAck"> </span>million in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Junk_bonds" title="Junk bonds" class="mw-redirect" id="mwAco">junk bonds</a> and completed for $1.1<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwAcs"> </span>billion, opening in April 1990.<sup about="#mwt279" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-fall_60-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"fall"}}'><a href="#cite_note-fall-60" id="mwAcw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAc0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAc4">[</span>60<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAc8">]</span></span></a></sup> He filed for Chapter 11 bankruptcy protection in 1991. Under the provisions of the restructuring agreement, Trump gave up half his initial stake and personally guaranteed future performance.<sup about="#mwt282" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-65" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-65"}}'><a href="#cite_note-65" id="mwAdA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAdE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAdI">[</span>65<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAdM">]</span></span></a></sup> To reduce his $900<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwAdQ"> </span>million of personal debt, he sold the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trump_Shuttle" title="Trump Shuttle" id="mwAdU">Trump Shuttle</a> airline; his megayacht, the <i id="mwAdY"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trump_Princess" title="Trump Princess" class="mw-redirect" id="mwAdc">Trump Princess</a></i>, which had been leased to his casinos and kept docked; and other businesses.<sup about="#mwt285" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-66" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-66"}}'><a href="#cite_note-66" id="mwAdg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAdk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAdo">[</span>66<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAds">]</span></span></a></sup> In 1995, Trump founded Trump Hotels &amp; Casino Resorts (THCR), which assumed ownership of the Trump Plaza.<sup about="#mwt288" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-67" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-67"}}'><a href="#cite_note-67" id="mwAdw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAd0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAd4">[</span>67<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAd8">]</span></span></a></sup> THCR purchased the Taj Mahal and the Trump Castle in 1996 and went bankrupt in 2004 and 2009, leaving him with 10<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwAeA"> </span>percent ownership.<sup about="#mwt291" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-fall_60-2" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"fall"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-fall-60"}}'><a href="#cite_note-fall-60" id="mwAeE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAeI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAeM">[</span>60<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAeQ">]</span></span></a></sup> He remained chairman until 2009.<sup about="#mwt294" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-68" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-68"}}'><a href="#cite_note-68" id="mwAeU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAeY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAec">[</span>68<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAeg">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="6" id="mwAek" aria-labelledby="Golf_clubs"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading4"><h4 id="Golf_clubs">Golf clubs</h4></div>
<p id="mwAeo">In 1985, Trump acquired the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mar-a-Lago" title="Mar-a-Lago" id="mwAes">Mar-a-Lago</a> estate in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Palm_Beach,_Florida" title="Palm Beach, Florida" id="mwAew">Palm Beach, Florida</a>.<sup about="#mwt297" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-69" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-69"}}'><a href="#cite_note-69" id="mwAe0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAe4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAe8">[</span>69<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAfA">]</span></span></a></sup> In 1995, he converted the estate into a private club with an initiation fee and annual dues. Trump continued to use a wing of the house as a private residence.<sup about="#mwt300" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-70" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-70"}}'><a href="#cite_note-70" id="mwAfE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAfI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAfM">[</span>70<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAfQ">]</span></span></a></sup> He declared the club his primary residence in 2019.<sup about="#mwt301" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-moved_52-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"moved"}}'><a href="#cite_note-moved-52" id="mwAfU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAfY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAfc">[</span>52<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAfg">]</span></span></a></sup> Trump began <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump_and_golf" title="Donald Trump and golf" id="mwAfk">building and buying golf courses</a> in 1999, owning 17 golf courses globally by 2016.<sup about="#mwt304" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-71" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-71"}}'><a href="#cite_note-71" id="mwAfo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAfs"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAfw">[</span>71<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAf0">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section></section><section data-mw-section-id="7" id="mwAf4" aria-labelledby="Licensing_the_Trump_name"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Licensing_the_Trump_name">Licensing the Trump name</h3></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt305" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwAf8" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"see also","href":"./Template:See_also"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"List of things named after Donald Trump"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt306" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt305" id="mwAgA">See also: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_things_named_after_Donald_Trump" title="List of things named after Donald Trump">List of things named after Donald Trump</a></div>
<p id="mwAgE"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Trump_Organization" title="The Trump Organization" id="mwAgI">The Trump Organization</a> often licensed the Trump name for consumer products and services, including foodstuffs, apparel, learning courses, and home furnishings.<sup about="#mwt312" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-neckties_72-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"neckties"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-neckties-72"}}'><a href="#cite_note-neckties-72" id="mwAgM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAgQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAgU">[</span>72<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAgY">]</span></span></a></sup> Over 50 licensing or management deals involved his name, generating at least $59<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwAgc"> </span>million for his companies.<sup about="#mwt315" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-73" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-73"}}'><a href="#cite_note-73" id="mwAgg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAgk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAgo">[</span>73<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAgs">]</span></span></a></sup> By 2018, only two consumer goods companies continued to license Trump's name.<sup about="#mwt316" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-neckties_72-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"neckties"}}'><a href="#cite_note-neckties-72" id="mwAgw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAg0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAg4">[</span>72<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAg8">]</span></span></a></sup> During the 2000s, he licensed his name to real estate developments. Forty of the projects he announced were not completed.<sup about="#mwt307" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBuettnerCraig2024410_74-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBuettnerCraig2024410"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBuettnerCraig2024410-74"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Buettner"},"2":{"wt":"Craig"},"3":{"wt":"2024"},"p":{"wt":"410"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBuettnerCraig2024410-74" id="mwAhA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAhE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAhI">[</span>74<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAhM">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="8" id="mwAhQ" aria-labelledby="Side_ventures"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Side_ventures">Side ventures</h3></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt317" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwAhU" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"see also","href":"./Template:See_also"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Donald Trump and American football"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt318" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt317" id="mwAhY">See also: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump_and_American_football" title="Donald Trump and American football">Donald Trump and American football</a></div>
<figure class="mw-default-size mw-halign-left" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwAhc"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Donald_Trump_and_Doug_Flutie_at_a_press_conference_in_the_Trump_Tower.jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwAhg"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Donald_Trump_and_Doug_Flutie_at_a_press_conference_in_the_Trump_Tower.jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Donald_Trump_and_Doug_Flutie_at_a_press_conference_in_the_Trump_Tower.jpg/250px-Donald_Trump_and_Doug_Flutie_at_a_press_conference_in_the_Trump_Tower.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Donald_Trump_and_Doug_Flutie_at_a_press_conference_in_the_Trump_Tower.jpg/500px-Donald_Trump_and_Doug_Flutie_at_a_press_conference_in_the_Trump_Tower.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" alt="Trump, Doug Flutie, and New Jersey Generals head coach Walt Michaels standing behind a lectern with big, round New Jersey Generals sign, with members of the press seated in the background" data-file-width="4916" data-file-height="3344" data-file-type="bitmap" height="170" width="250" class="mw-file-element" id="mwAhk"/></a><figcaption id="mwAho">1985 New Jersey Generals press conference in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trump_Tower" title="Trump Tower" id="mwAhs">Trump Tower</a></figcaption></figure>

<p id="mwAhw">In 1970, Trump invested $70,000 of his father's wealth to receive billing as coproducer of a Broadway comedy<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwAh0">—</span>and lost the money.<sup about="#mwt319" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBuettnerCraig2024109_75-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBuettnerCraig2024109"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBuettnerCraig2024109-75"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Buettner"},"2":{"wt":"Craig"},"3":{"wt":"2024"},"p":{"wt":"109"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBuettnerCraig2024109-75" id="mwAh4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAh8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAiA">[</span>75<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAiE">]</span></span></a></sup> After making low-ball bids for the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_York_Mets" title="New York Mets" id="mwAiI">New York Mets</a> and the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cleveland_Indians" title="Cleveland Indians" class="mw-redirect" id="mwAiM">Cleveland Indians</a> baseball teams, in 1983 for about $6<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwAiQ"> </span>million, he purchased the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_Jersey_Generals" title="New Jersey Generals" id="mwAiU">New Jersey Generals</a>, a team in the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Football_League" title="United States Football League" id="mwAiY">United States Football League</a>.<sup about="#mwt322" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher2017175_76-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEKranishFisher2017175"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher2017175-76"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Kranish"},"2":{"wt":"Fisher"},"3":{"wt":"2017"},"p":{"wt":"175"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher2017175-76" id="mwAic"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAig"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAik">[</span>76<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAio">]</span></span></a></sup> The league folded after the 1985 season, largely due to his attempt to move to a fall schedule (when it would have competed with the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Football_League" title="National Football League" id="mwAis">National Football League</a> for audience) and his attempt to force a merger with the NFL by bringing an antitrust suit.<sup about="#mwt327" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-77" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-77"}}'><a href="#cite_note-77" id="mwAiw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAi0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAi4">[</span>77<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAi8">]</span></span></a></sup> In 1989 and 1990, he lent his name to the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tour_de_Trump" title="Tour de Trump" class="mw-redirect" id="mwAjA">Tour de Trump</a> cycling stage race, an attempt to create an American equivalent of European races such as the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tour_de_France" title="Tour de France" id="mwAjE">Tour de France</a> or the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Giro_d'Italia" title="Giro d'Italia" id="mwAjI">Giro d'Italia</a>.<sup about="#mwt330" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-78" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-78"}}'><a href="#cite_note-78" id="mwAjM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAjQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAjU">[</span>78<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAjY">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwAjc">From 1986 to 1988, he purchased significant blocks of shares in various public companies while suggesting that he intended to take over the company and then sold his shares for a profit,<sup about="#mwt333" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Buettner-190508_79-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Buettner-190508"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Buettner-190508-79"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Buettner-190508-79" id="mwAjg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAjk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAjo">[</span>79<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAjs">]</span></span></a></sup> leading some observers to think he was engaged in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greenmail" title="Greenmail" id="mwAjw">greenmail</a>.<sup about="#mwt336" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-80" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-80"}}'><a href="#cite_note-80" id="mwAj0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAj4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAj8">[</span>80<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAkA">]</span></span></a></sup> <i id="mwAkE">The New York Times</i> found that he initially made millions of dollars in such stock transactions but "lost most, if not all, of those gains after investors stopped taking his takeover talk seriously".<sup about="#mwt337" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Buettner-190508_79-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Buettner-190508"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Buettner-190508-79" id="mwAkI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAkM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAkQ">[</span>79<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAkU">]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwAkY"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Donald_Trump_star_Hollywood_Walk_of_Fame.JPG" class="mw-file-description" id="mwAkc"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Donald_Trump_star_Hollywood_Walk_of_Fame.JPG" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/Donald_Trump_star_Hollywood_Walk_of_Fame.JPG/250px-Donald_Trump_star_Hollywood_Walk_of_Fame.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/Donald_Trump_star_Hollywood_Walk_of_Fame.JPG/500px-Donald_Trump_star_Hollywood_Walk_of_Fame.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" alt='A red star with a bronze outline and "Donald Trump" and a TV icon written on it in bronze, embedded in a black terrazzo sidewalk' data-file-width="3072" data-file-height="2304" data-file-type="bitmap" height="135" width="180" class="mw-file-element mw-file-upright" style="--mw-file-upright: 0.7" id="mwAkg"/></a><figcaption id="mwAkk">Star on the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hollywood_Walk_of_Fame" title="Hollywood Walk of Fame" id="mwAko">Hollywood Walk of Fame</a></figcaption></figure>
<p id="mwAks">In 1988, Trump purchased the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eastern_Air_Lines_Shuttle" title="Eastern Air Lines Shuttle" id="mwAkw">Eastern Air Lines Shuttle</a>, financing the purchase with $380<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwAk0"> </span>million (equivalent to $<span about="#mwt338" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwAk4" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"inflation","href":"./Template:Inflation"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"US"},"2":{"wt":"0.380"},"3":{"wt":"1988"},"r":{"wt":"2"}},"i":0}}]}'>1.03</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwAk8"> </span>billion in <span about="#mwt339" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwAlA" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"inflation/year","href":"./Template:Inflation/year"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"US"}},"i":0}}]}'>2025</span><sup about="#mwt340" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-inflation-US_36-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"inflation-US","group":""},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-inflation-US-36"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"inflation/fn","href":"./Template:Inflation/fn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"US"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-inflation-US-36" id="mwAlE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAlI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAlM">[</span>36<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAlQ">]</span></span></a></sup>) in loans from a syndicate of 22 banks. He renamed the airline <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trump_Shuttle" title="Trump Shuttle" id="mwAlU">Trump Shuttle</a> and operated it until 1992.<sup about="#mwt354" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-TA_81-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"TA"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-TA-81"}}'><a href="#cite_note-TA-81" id="mwAlY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAlc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAlg">[</span>81<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAlk">]</span></span></a></sup> He defaulted on his loans in 1991, and ownership passed to the banks.<sup about="#mwt357" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-82" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-82"}}'><a href="#cite_note-82" id="mwAlo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAls"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAlw">[</span>82<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAl0">]</span></span></a></sup> In 1996, he purchased the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Miss_Universe" title="Miss Universe" id="mwAl4">Miss Universe</a> pageants, including <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Miss_USA" title="Miss USA" id="mwAl8">Miss USA</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Miss_Teen_USA" title="Miss Teen USA" id="mwAmA">Miss Teen USA</a>.<sup about="#mwt349" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEHaberman2022129–130_83-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEHaberman2022129–130"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEHaberman2022129–130-83"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Haberman"},"2":{"wt":"2022"},"pp":{"wt":"129–130"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEHaberman2022129–130-83" id="mwAmE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAmI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAmM">[</span>83<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAmQ">]</span></span></a></sup> Due to disagreements with <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CBS" title="CBS" id="mwAmU">CBS</a> about scheduling, he took both pageants to <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NBC" title="NBC" id="mwAmY">NBC</a> in 2002.<sup about="#mwt360" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-84" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-84"}}'><a href="#cite_note-84" id="mwAmc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAmg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAmk">[</span>84<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAmo">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt363" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-85" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-85"}}'><a href="#cite_note-85" id="mwAms"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAmw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAm0">[</span>85<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAm4">]</span></span></a></sup> In 2007, he received a star on the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hollywood_Walk_of_Fame" title="Hollywood Walk of Fame" id="mwAm8">Hollywood Walk of Fame</a> for his work as producer of Miss Universe.<sup about="#mwt366" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-86" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-86"}}'><a href="#cite_note-86" id="mwAnA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAnE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAnI">[</span>86<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAnM">]</span></span></a></sup> NBC and Univision dropped the pageants in June 2015 in reaction to his comments about Mexican immigrants.<sup about="#mwt369" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-87" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-87"}}'><a href="#cite_note-87" id="mwAnQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAnU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAnY">[</span>87<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAnc">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<section data-mw-section-id="9" id="mwAng" aria-labelledby="Trump_University"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading4"><h4 id="Trump_University">Trump University</h4></div>
<p id="mwAnk">In 2005, Trump cofounded <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trump_University" title="Trump University" id="mwAno">Trump University</a>, a company that sold real estate seminars for up to $35,000. After New York State authorities notified the company that its use of "university" violated state law because it was not an academic institution, its name was changed to the Trump Entrepreneur Initiative in 2010.<sup about="#mwt370" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTED'Antonio2015281–282_88-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTED&apos;Antonio2015281–282"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTED&apos;Antonio2015281–282-88"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"D&apos;Antonio"},"2":{"wt":"2015"},"pp":{"wt":"281–282"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTED'Antonio2015281–282-88" id="mwAns"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAnw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAn0">[</span>88<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAn4">]</span></span></a></sup> In 2013, the State of New York filed a $40<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwAn8"> </span>million civil suit against Trump University, alleging that the company made false statements and defrauded consumers. Additionally, two class actions were filed in federal court against him and his companies. Internal documents revealed that employees were instructed to use a hard-sell approach, and former employees testified that Trump University had defrauded or lied to its students.<sup about="#mwt373" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTED'Antonio2015282–283_89-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTED&apos;Antonio2015282–283"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTED&apos;Antonio2015282–283-89"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"D&apos;Antonio"},"2":{"wt":"2015"},"pp":{"wt":"282–283"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTED'Antonio2015282–283-89" id="mwAoA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAoE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAoI">[</span>89<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAoM">]</span></span></a></sup> Shortly after winning the 2016 presidential election, he agreed to pay a total of $25<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwAoQ"> </span>million to settle the three cases.<sup about="#mwt378" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-90" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-90"}}'><a href="#cite_note-90" id="mwAoU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAoY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAoc">[</span>90<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAog">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section></section><section data-mw-section-id="10" id="mwAok" aria-labelledby="Foundation"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Foundation">Foundation</h3></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt379" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwAoo" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Donald J. Trump Foundation"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt380" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt379" id="mwAos">Main article: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_J._Trump_Foundation" title="Donald J. Trump Foundation">Donald J. Trump Foundation</a></div>

<p id="mwAow">The Donald J. Trump Foundation was a <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Private_foundation_(United_States)" title="Private foundation (United States)" id="mwAo0">private foundation</a> established in 1988.<sup about="#mwt383" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-91" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-91"}}'><a href="#cite_note-91" id="mwAo4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAo8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwApA">[</span>91<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwApE">]</span></span></a></sup> From 1987 to 2006, Trump gave his foundation $5.4<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwApI"> </span>million, which had been spent by the end of 2006. After donating a total of $65,000 in 2007<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwApM">–</span>2008, he stopped donating any personal funds to the charity,<sup about="#mwt386" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-retool_92-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"retool"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-retool-92"}}'><a href="#cite_note-retool-92" id="mwApQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwApU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwApY">[</span>92<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwApc">]</span></span></a></sup> which received millions from other donors, including $5<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwApg"> </span>million from <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vince_McMahon" title="Vince McMahon" id="mwApk">Vince McMahon</a>.<sup about="#mwt389" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-93" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-93"}}'><a href="#cite_note-93" id="mwApo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAps"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwApw">[</span>93<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAp0">]</span></span></a></sup> The foundation gave to health and sports-related charities, conservative groups,<sup about="#mwt392" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-94" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-94"}}'><a href="#cite_note-94" id="mwAp4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAp8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAqA">[</span>94<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAqE">]</span></span></a></sup> and charities that held events at Trump properties.<sup about="#mwt393" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-retool_92-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"retool"}}'><a href="#cite_note-retool-92" id="mwAqI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAqM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAqQ">[</span>92<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAqU">]</span></span></a></sup> In 2016, <i id="mwAqY"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Washington_Post" title="The Washington Post" id="mwAqc">The Washington Post</a></i> reported that the charity had committed several potential legal and ethical violations, including self-dealing and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tax_evasion" title="Tax evasion" id="mwAqg">tax evasion</a>.<sup about="#mwt396" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-95" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-95"}}'><a href="#cite_note-95" id="mwAqk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAqo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAqs">[</span>95<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAqw">]</span></span></a></sup> Also in 2016, the New York attorney general stated the foundation had violated state law by soliciting donations without submitting to required annual external audits and ordered it to cease its fundraising activities in New York immediately.<sup about="#mwt399" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-96" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-96"}}'><a href="#cite_note-96" id="mwAq0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAq4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAq8">[</span>96<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwArA">]</span></span></a></sup> Trump said in December 2016 that the foundation would be dissolved but was prevented from doing so "amid concerns about the handling of the foundation's documents and assets".<sup about="#mwt402" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-block_97-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"block"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-block-97"}}'><a href="#cite_note-block-97" id="mwArE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwArI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwArM">[</span>97<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwArQ">]</span></span></a></sup></p> 

<p id="mwArU">In June 2018, the New York attorney general's office filed a civil suit against the foundation, Trump, and his adult children, seeking $2.8<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwArY"> </span>million in restitution and additional penalties.<sup about="#mwt405" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-98" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-98"}}'><a href="#cite_note-98" id="mwArc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwArg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwArk">[</span>98<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAro">]</span></span></a></sup> In December 2018, the foundation ceased operation and disbursed its assets to other charities.<sup about="#mwt406" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-block_97-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"block"}}'><a href="#cite_note-block-97" id="mwArs"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwArw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAr0">[</span>97<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAr4">]</span></span></a></sup> In November 2019, a New York state judge ordered Trump to pay $2<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwAr8"> </span>million to a group of charities for misusing the foundation's funds, in part to finance his presidential campaign.<sup about="#mwt409" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-99" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-99"}}'><a href="#cite_note-99" id="mwAsA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAsE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAsI">[</span>99<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAsM">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="11" id="mwAsQ" aria-labelledby="Legal_affairs_and_bankruptcies"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Legal_affairs_and_bankruptcies">Legal affairs and bankruptcies</h3></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt410" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwAsU" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Personal and business legal affairs of Donald Trump"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt411" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt410" id="mwAsY">Main article: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Personal_and_business_legal_affairs_of_Donald_Trump" title="Personal and business legal affairs of Donald Trump">Personal and business legal affairs of Donald Trump</a></div>

<p id="mwAsc">According to a review of state and federal court files conducted by <i id="mwAsg"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/USA_Today" title="USA Today" id="mwAsk">USA Today</a></i> in 2018, Trump and his businesses had been involved in more than 4,000 lawsuits,<sup about="#mwt414" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-usat-lawsuits_100-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"usat-lawsuits"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-usat-lawsuits-100"}}'><a href="#cite_note-usat-lawsuits-100" id="mwAso"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAss"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAsw">[</span>100<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAs0">]</span></span></a></sup> liens, and other filings, often filed for nonpayment against him by employees, contractors, real estate brokers, and his own attorneys.<sup about="#mwt417" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-101" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-101"}}'><a href="#cite_note-101" id="mwAs4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAs8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAtA">[</span>101<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAtE">]</span></span></a></sup> While Trump has not filed for <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Personal_bankruptcy" title="Personal bankruptcy" id="mwAtI">personal bankruptcy</a>, his over-leveraged hotel and casino businesses in Atlantic City and New York filed for Chapter 11 bankruptcy protection six times between 1991 and 2009.<sup about="#mwt420" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-TW_41-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"TW"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-TW-41"}}'><a href="#cite_note-TW-41" id="mwAtM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAtQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAtU">[</span>41<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAtY">]</span></span></a></sup> They continued to operate while the banks restructured debt and reduced his shares in the properties.<sup about="#mwt421" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-TW_41-2" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"TW"}}'><a href="#cite_note-TW-41" id="mwAtc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAtg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAtk">[</span>41<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAto">]</span></span></a></sup> During the 1980s, more than 70 banks had lent Trump $4<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwAts"> </span>billion.<sup about="#mwt424" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-102" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-102"}}'><a href="#cite_note-102" id="mwAtw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAt0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAt4">[</span>102<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAt8">]</span></span></a></sup> After his corporate bankruptcies of the early 1990s, most major banks, with the exception of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deutsche_Bank" title="Deutsche Bank" id="mwAuA">Deutsche Bank</a>, declined to lend to him.<sup about="#mwt427" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-103" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-103"}}'><a href="#cite_note-103" id="mwAuE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAuI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAuM">[</span>103<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAuQ">]</span></span></a></sup> After the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/January_6_United_States_Capitol_attack" title="January 6 United States Capitol attack" id="mwAuU">January<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwAuY"> </span>6 Capitol attack</a>, Deutsche Bank also decided not to do business with him or his affiliated company in the future.<sup about="#mwt430" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-104" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-104"}}'><a href="#cite_note-104" id="mwAuc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAug"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAuk">[</span>104<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAuo">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section></section><section data-mw-section-id="12" id="mwAus" aria-labelledby="Media_career"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id="Media_career">Media career</h2></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt431" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwAuw" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Media career of Donald Trump"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt432" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt431" id="mwAu0">Main article: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Media_career_of_Donald_Trump" title="Media career of Donald Trump">Media career of Donald Trump</a></div>

<p id="mwAu4">Trump has <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bibliography_of_Donald_Trump" title="Bibliography of Donald Trump" id="mwAu8">published 19 books under his name</a>, most written or cowritten by <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ghostwriters" title="Ghostwriters" class="mw-redirect" id="mwAvA">ghostwriters</a>.<sup about="#mwt444" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-105" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-105"}}'><a href="#cite_note-105" id="mwAvE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAvI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAvM">[</span>105<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAvQ">]</span></span></a></sup> His first book, the 1987 <i id="mwAvU"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Art_of_the_Deal" title="The Art of the Deal" id="mwAvY">Art of the Deal</a></i>, was ghostwritten by <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tony_Schwartz_(author)" title="Tony Schwartz (author)" class="mw-redirect" id="mwAvc">Tony Schwartz</a>, who is credited as coauthor. It was a <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_New_York_Times_Best_Seller_list" title="The New York Times Best Seller list" id="mwAvg"><i id="mwAvk">New York Times</i> Best Seller</a> and made Trump a celebrity beyond New York as a rich and successful entrepreneur.<sup about="#mwt447" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-106" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-106"}}'><a href="#cite_note-106" id="mwAvo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAvs"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAvw">[</span>106<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAv0">]</span></span></a></sup> Trump had cameos in many films and television shows from 1985 to 2001.<sup about="#mwt450" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-107" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-107"}}'><a href="#cite_note-107" id="mwAv4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAv8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAwA">[</span>107<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAwE">]</span></span></a></sup> Trump acquired his style of politics from <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Professional_wrestling" title="Professional wrestling" id="mwAwI">professional wrestling</a>. From the late 1980s, he sporadically played himself as a super-rich boss at events staged by professional wrestling promotion <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/WWE" title="WWE" id="mwAwM">WWE</a>, including <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/WrestleMania_23" title="WrestleMania 23" id="mwAwQ">WrestleMania 23</a> in 2007. and being inducted into the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/WWE_Hall_of_Fame" title="WWE Hall of Fame" id="mwAwU">WWE Hall of Fame</a> in 2013.<sup about="#mwt433" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEGaufmanGanesh202469–70_108-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEGaufmanGanesh202469–70"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEGaufmanGanesh202469–70-108"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Gaufman"},"2":{"wt":"Ganesh"},"3":{"wt":"2024"},"pp":{"wt":"69–70"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEGaufmanGanesh202469–70-108" id="mwAwY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAwc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAwg">[</span>108<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAwk">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt436" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEO'Brien202042_109-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEO&apos;Brien202042"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEO&apos;Brien202042-109"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"O&apos;Brien"},"2":{"wt":"2020"},"p":{"wt":"42"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEO'Brien202042-109" id="mwAwo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAws"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAww">[</span>109<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAw0">]</span></span></a></sup> Starting in the 1990s, Trump appeared 24 times as a guest on the nationally syndicated <i id="mwAw4"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Howard_Stern_Show" title="Howard Stern Show" class="mw-redirect" id="mwAw8">Howard Stern Show</a></i>.<sup about="#mwt439" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher2017[httpsbooksgooglecombooksidx2jUDQAAQBAJpgPA166_166]_110-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEKranishFisher2017[httpsbooksgooglecombooksidx2jUDQAAQBAJpgPA166 166]"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher2017[httpsbooksgooglecombooksidx2jUDQAAQBAJpgPA166_166]-110"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Kranish"},"2":{"wt":"Fisher"},"3":{"wt":"2017"},"p":{"wt":"[https://books.google.com/books?id=x2jUDQAAQBAJ&amp;pg=PA166 166]"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher2017[httpsbooksgooglecombooksidx2jUDQAAQBAJpgPA166_166]-110" id="mwAxA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAxE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAxI">[</span>110<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAxM">]</span></span></a></sup> He had his own short-form talk radio program, <i id="mwAxQ"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trumped!" title="Trumped!" id="mwAxU">Trumped!</a></i>, from 2004 to 2008.<sup about="#mwt453" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-111" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-111"}}'><a href="#cite_note-111" id="mwAxY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAxc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAxg">[</span>111<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAxk">]</span></span></a></sup> From 2011 until 2015, he was a guest commentator on <i id="mwAxo"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fox_&amp;_Friends" title="Fox &amp; Friends" id="mwAxs">Fox &amp; Friends</a></i>.<sup about="#mwt456" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-112" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-112"}}'><a href="#cite_note-112" id="mwAxw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAx0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAx4">[</span>112<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAx8">]</span></span></a></sup> In 2021, Trump, who had been a member since 1989, resigned from <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SAG-AFTRA" title="SAG-AFTRA" id="mwAyA">SAG-AFTRA</a> to avoid a disciplinary hearing regarding the January<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwAyE"> </span>6 attack; two days later, the union permanently barred him.<sup about="#mwt459" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-113" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-113"}}'><a href="#cite_note-113" id="mwAyI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAyM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAyQ">[</span>113<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAyU">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwAyY">Producer <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mark_Burnett" title="Mark Burnett" id="mwAyc">Mark Burnett</a> made Trump a television star<sup about="#mwt460" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBuettnerCraig20247_114-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBuettnerCraig20247"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBuettnerCraig20247-114"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Buettner"},"2":{"wt":"Craig"},"3":{"wt":"2024"},"p":{"wt":"7"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBuettnerCraig20247-114" id="mwAyg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAyk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAyo">[</span>114<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAys">]</span></span></a></sup> when he created the reality show <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Apprentice_(American_TV_series)" title="The Apprentice (American TV series)" id="mwAyw"><i id="mwAy0">The Apprentice</i></a>, which Trump hosted from 2004 to 2015 (including variant <i id="mwAy4"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Celebrity_Apprentice" title="The Celebrity Apprentice" id="mwAy8">The Celebrity Apprentice</a></i>). On the shows, he was a superrich chief executive who eliminated contestants with the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Catchphrase" title="Catchphrase" id="mwAzA">catchphrase</a> "you're fired". <i id="mwAzE">The New York Times</i> called his portrayal "a highly flattering, highly fictionalized version" of himself.<sup about="#mwt465" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-show_115-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"show"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-show-115"}}'><a href="#cite_note-show-115" id="mwAzI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAzM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAzQ">[</span>115<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAzU">]</span></span></a></sup> The shows remade Trump's image for millions of viewers nationwide.<sup about="#mwt466" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-show_115-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"show"}}'><a href="#cite_note-show-115" id="mwAzY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAzc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAzg">[</span>115<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAzk">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt469" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-116" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-116"}}'><a href="#cite_note-116" id="mwAzo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAzs"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAzw">[</span>116<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAz0">]</span></span></a></sup> He made more than $400<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwAz4"> </span>million from the shows and related licensing agreements.<sup about="#mwt472" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-117" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-117"}}'><a href="#cite_note-117" id="mwAz8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA0A"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA0E">[</span>117<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA0I">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="13" id="mwA0M" aria-labelledby="Early_political_aspirations"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id="Early_political_aspirations">Early political aspirations</h2></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt473" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwA0Q" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"further","href":"./Template:Further"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Political career of Donald Trump"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt474" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt473" id="mwA0U">Further information: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Political_career_of_Donald_Trump" title="Political career of Donald Trump">Political career of Donald Trump</a></div>
<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwA0Y"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Donald_Trump_speaking_at_CPAC_2011_by_Mark_Taylor.jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwA0c"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Donald_Trump_speaking_at_CPAC_2011_by_Mark_Taylor.jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/Donald_Trump_speaking_at_CPAC_2011_by_Mark_Taylor.jpg/250px-Donald_Trump_speaking_at_CPAC_2011_by_Mark_Taylor.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/Donald_Trump_speaking_at_CPAC_2011_by_Mark_Taylor.jpg/500px-Donald_Trump_speaking_at_CPAC_2011_by_Mark_Taylor.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" alt="Trump, leaning heavily onto a lectern, with his mouth open mid-speech and a woman clapping next to him" data-file-width="5184" data-file-height="3456" data-file-type="bitmap" height="167" width="250" class="mw-file-element" id="mwA0g"/></a><figcaption id="mwA0k">Speaking at <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conservative_Political_Action_Conference" title="Conservative Political Action Conference" id="mwA0o">CPAC</a>, February 2011</figcaption></figure>
<p id="mwA0s">Trump registered as a Republican in Queens in 1969 and in Manhattan in 1987;<sup about="#mwt475" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBarrett201675_20-2" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBarrett201675"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBarrett201675-20"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Barrett"},"2":{"wt":"2016"},"p":{"wt":"75"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBarrett201675-20" id="mwA0w"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA00"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA04">[</span>20<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA08">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt480" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-118" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-118"}}'><a href="#cite_note-118" id="mwA1A"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA1E"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA1I">[</span>118<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA1M">]</span></span></a></sup> a member of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Independence_Party_of_New_York" title="Independence Party of New York" id="mwA1Q">Independence Party</a>, the New York state affiliate of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reform_Party_of_the_United_States_of_America" title="Reform Party of the United States of America" id="mwA1U">Reform Party</a>, in 1999; a <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Democratic_Party_(United_States)" title="Democratic Party (United States)" id="mwA1Y">Democrat</a> in 2001; a Republican in 2009; <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Independent_politician" title="Independent politician" id="mwA1c">unaffiliated</a> in 2011; and a Republican in 2012.<sup about="#mwt483" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-119" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-119"}}'><a href="#cite_note-119" id="mwA1g"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA1k"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA1o">[</span>119<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA1s">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwA1w">In 1987, Trump placed full-page advertisements in major newspapers,<sup about="#mwt489" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-hint_120-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"hint"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-hint-120"}}'><a href="#cite_note-hint-120" id="mwA10"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA14"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA18">[</span>120<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA2A">]</span></span></a></sup> expressing his views on foreign policy and how to eliminate the federal budget deficit.<sup about="#mwt492" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-121" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-121"}}'><a href="#cite_note-121" id="mwA2E"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA2I"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA2M">[</span>121<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA2Q">]</span></span></a></sup> In 1988, he approached <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lee_Atwater" title="Lee Atwater" id="mwA2U">Lee Atwater</a>, asking to be put on the ticket as Republican nominee <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_H._W._Bush" title="George H. W. Bush" id="mwA2Y">George H. W. Bush</a>'s running mate. Bush found the request "strange and unbelievable".<sup about="#mwt484" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEMeacham2016326_122-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEMeacham2016326"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEMeacham2016326-122"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Meacham"},"2":{"wt":"2016"},"p":{"wt":"326"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEMeacham2016326-122" id="mwA2c"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA2g"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA2k">[</span>122<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA2o">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt495" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-123" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-123"}}'><a href="#cite_note-123" id="mwA2s"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA2w"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA20">[</span>123<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA24">]</span></span></a></sup> Trump <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump_2000_presidential_campaign" title="Donald Trump 2000 presidential campaign" id="mwA28">was a candidate</a> in the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2000_Reform_Party_presidential_primaries" title="2000 Reform Party presidential primaries" id="mwA3A">2000 Reform Party presidential primaries</a> for three months before he withdrew in February 2000.<sup about="#mwt498" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-124" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-124"}}'><a href="#cite_note-124" id="mwA3E"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA3I"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA3M">[</span>124<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA3Q">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt501" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-125" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-125"}}'><a href="#cite_note-125" id="mwA3U"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA3Y"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA3c">[</span>125<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA3g">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt504" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-126" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-126"}}'><a href="#cite_note-126" id="mwA3k"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA3o"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA3s">[</span>126<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA3w">]</span></span></a></sup> In 2011, Trump considered challenging President <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barack_Obama" title="Barack Obama" id="mwA30">Barack Obama</a> in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2012_United_States_presidential_election" title="2012 United States presidential election" id="mwA34">the 2012 election</a>. He spoke at the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conservative_Political_Action_Conference" title="Conservative Political Action Conference" id="mwA38">Conservative Political Action Conference</a> in February and gave speeches in states with early primaries.<sup about="#mwt507" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-McA_127-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"McA"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-McA-127"}}'><a href="#cite_note-McA-127" id="mwA4A"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA4E"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA4I">[</span>127<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA4M">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt510" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-128" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-128"}}'><a href="#cite_note-128" id="mwA4Q"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA4U"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA4Y">[</span>128<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA4c">]</span></span></a></sup> In May 2011, he said that he would not run.<sup about="#mwt511" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-McA_127-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"McA"}}'><a href="#cite_note-McA-127" id="mwA4g"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA4k"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA4o">[</span>127<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA4s">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="14" id="mwA4w" aria-labelledby="2016_presidential_election"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id="2016_presidential_election">2016 presidential election</h2></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt512" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwA40" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"2016 United States presidential election"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt513" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt512" id="mwA44">Main article: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2016_United_States_presidential_election" title="2016 United States presidential election">2016 United States presidential election</a></div>

<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt514" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwA48" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"further","href":"./Template:Further"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Donald Trump 2016 presidential campaign"},"2":{"wt":"2016 Republican Party presidential primaries"},"3":{"wt":"First presidential transition of Donald Trump"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt515" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt514" id="mwA5A">Further information: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump_2016_presidential_campaign" title="Donald Trump 2016 presidential campaign">Donald Trump 2016 presidential campaign</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2016_Republican_Party_presidential_primaries" title="2016 Republican Party presidential primaries">2016 Republican Party presidential primaries</a>, and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_presidential_transition_of_Donald_Trump" title="First presidential transition of Donald Trump">First presidential transition of Donald Trump</a></div>
<figure class="mw-default-size mw-halign-left" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwA5E"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Donald_Trump_by_Gage_Skidmore_5.jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwA5I"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Donald_Trump_by_Gage_Skidmore_5.jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Donald_Trump_by_Gage_Skidmore_5.jpg/250px-Donald_Trump_by_Gage_Skidmore_5.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Donald_Trump_by_Gage_Skidmore_5.jpg/500px-Donald_Trump_by_Gage_Skidmore_5.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" alt='Trump speaking in front of an American flag behind a lectern, wearing a black business suit and a bright red "MAKE AMERICA GREAT AGAIN" baseball cap. The lectern sports a blue "TRUMP" sign.' data-file-width="4272" data-file-height="2848" data-file-type="bitmap" height="167" width="250" class="mw-file-element" id="mwA5M"/></a><figcaption id="mwA5Q">Campaigning in Arizona, March 2016</figcaption></figure>
<p id="mwA5U">Trump announced his candidacy for the 2016 election in June 2015.<sup about="#mwt518" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-129" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-129"}}'><a href="#cite_note-129" id="mwA5Y"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA5c"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA5g">[</span>129<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA5k">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt521" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-130" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-130"}}'><a href="#cite_note-130" id="mwA5o"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA5s"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA5w">[</span>130<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA50">]</span></span></a></sup> Using the slogan "<a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Make_America_Great_Again" title="Make America Great Again" id="mwA54">Make America Great Again</a>" he had trademarked in 2012,<sup about="#mwt524" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-131" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-131"}}'><a href="#cite_note-131" id="mwA58"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA6A"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA6E">[</span>131<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA6I">]</span></span></a></sup> he campaigned as a rich, successful businessman and an outsider without political experience,<sup about="#mwt527" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-132" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-132"}}'><a href="#cite_note-132" id="mwA6M"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA6Q"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA6U">[</span>132<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA6Y">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt530" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-133" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-133"}}'><a href="#cite_note-133" id="mwA6c"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA6g"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA6k">[</span>133<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA6o">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt533" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-134" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-134"}}'><a href="#cite_note-134" id="mwA6s"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA6w"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA60">[</span>134<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA64">]</span></span></a></sup> and claimed <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Media_bias" title="Media bias" id="mwA68">media bias</a> against him.<sup about="#mwt536" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-135" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-135"}}'><a href="#cite_note-135" id="mwA7A"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA7E"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA7I">[</span>135<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA7M">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt539" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-136" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-136"}}'><a href="#cite_note-136" id="mwA7Q"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA7U"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA7Y">[</span>136<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA7c">]</span></span></a></sup> Trump's campaign statements were often opaque and suggestive;<sup about="#mwt542" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-137" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-137"}}'><a href="#cite_note-137" id="mwA7g"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA7k"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA7o">[</span>137<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA7s">]</span></span></a></sup> a record number were false.<sup about="#mwt545" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-whoppers_138-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"whoppers"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-whoppers-138"}}'><a href="#cite_note-whoppers-138" id="mwA7w"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA70"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA74">[</span>138<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA78">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt548" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-139" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-139"}}'><a href="#cite_note-139" id="mwA8A"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA8E"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA8I">[</span>139<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA8M">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt551" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-140" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-140"}}'><a href="#cite_note-140" id="mwA8Q"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA8U"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA8Y">[</span>140<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA8c">]</span></span></a></sup> He became the Republican front-runner in March 2016<sup about="#mwt554" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-141" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-141"}}'><a href="#cite_note-141" id="mwA8g"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA8k"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA8o">[</span>141<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA8s">]</span></span></a></sup> and was declared the presumptive Republican nominee in May.<sup about="#mwt557" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-142" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-142"}}'><a href="#cite_note-142" id="mwA8w"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA80"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA84">[</span>142<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA88">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwA9A">Trump described <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NATO" title="NATO" id="mwA9E">NATO</a> as "obsolete"<sup about="#mwt566" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-143" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-143"}}'><a href="#cite_note-143" id="mwA9I"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA9M"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA9Q">[</span>143<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA9U">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt558" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEEdwards2018&quot;On_the_campaign_trail,_Trump_repeatedly_called_North_Atlantic_Treaty_Organization_(NATO)_'obsolete'&quot;_144-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEEdwards2018\"On the campaign trail, Trump repeatedly called North Atlantic Treaty Organization (NATO) &apos;obsolete&apos;\""},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEEdwards2018\"On_the_campaign_trail,_Trump_repeatedly_called_North_Atlantic_Treaty_Organization_(NATO)_&apos;obsolete&apos;\"-144"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Edwards"},"2":{"wt":"2018"},"loc":{"wt":"\"On the campaign trail, Trump repeatedly called North Atlantic Treaty Organization (NATO) &apos;obsolete&apos;\""}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEEdwards2018&quot;On_the_campaign_trail,_Trump_repeatedly_called_North_Atlantic_Treaty_Organization_(NATO)_'obsolete'&quot;-144" id="mwA9Y"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA9c"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA9g">[</span>144<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA9k">]</span></span></a></sup> and espoused views described by <i id="mwA9o">The Washington Post</i> as <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Noninterventionist" title="Noninterventionist" class="mw-redirect" id="mwA9s">noninterventionist</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Protectionist" title="Protectionist" class="mw-redirect" id="mwA9w">protectionist</a>.<sup about="#mwt569" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-145" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-145"}}'><a href="#cite_note-145" id="mwA90"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA94"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA98">[</span>145<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA-A">]</span></span></a></sup> His campaign platform emphasized renegotiating <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/U.S.–China_relations" title="U.S.–China relations" class="mw-redirect" id="mwA-E">U.S.–China relations</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Free_trade_agreements" title="Free trade agreements" class="mw-redirect" id="mwA-I">free trade agreements</a> such as <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NAFTA" title="NAFTA" class="mw-redirect" id="mwA-M">NAFTA</a> and strongly enforcing immigration laws. Other campaign positions included pursuing <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Energy_independence" title="Energy independence" id="mwA-Q">energy independence</a> while opposing climate change regulations, modernizing <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Department_of_Veterans_Affairs#Veterans_Benefits_Administration" title="United States Department of Veterans Affairs" id="mwA-U">services for veterans</a>, repealing and replacing the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Affordable_Care_Act" title="Affordable Care Act" id="mwA-Y">Affordable Care Act</a>, abolishing <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Common_Core" title="Common Core" id="mwA-c">Common Core</a> education standards, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Infrastructure-based_development" title="Infrastructure-based development" id="mwA-g">investing in infrastructure</a>, simplifying the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Internal_Revenue_Code" title="Internal Revenue Code" id="mwA-k">tax code</a> while reducing taxes, and imposing <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tariff" title="Tariff" id="mwA-o">tariffs</a> on imports by companies that offshore jobs. He advocated increasing military spending and extreme vetting or banning of immigrants from Muslim-majority countries.<sup about="#mwt572" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-146" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-146"}}'><a href="#cite_note-146" id="mwA-s"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA-w"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA-0">[</span>146<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA-4">]</span></span></a></sup> He promised to build a <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mexico–United_States_border_wall" title="Mexico–United States border wall" id="mwA-8">wall on the Mexico<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwA_A">–</span>U.S. border</a> and vowed that Mexico would pay for it.<sup about="#mwt575" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-147" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-147"}}'><a href="#cite_note-147" id="mwA_E"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA_I"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA_M">[</span>147<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA_Q">]</span></span></a></sup> He pledged to deport millions of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Undocumented_immigrant_population_of_the_United_States" title="Undocumented immigrant population of the United States" id="mwA_U">illegal immigrants residing in the U.S.</a>,<sup about="#mwt578" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-148" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-148"}}'><a href="#cite_note-148" id="mwA_Y"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA_c"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA_g">[</span>148<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA_k">]</span></span></a></sup> and criticized <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Birthright_citizenship_in_the_United_States" title="Birthright citizenship in the United States" id="mwA_o">birthright citizenship</a> for incentivizing "<a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anchor_babies" title="Anchor babies" class="mw-redirect" id="mwA_s">anchor babies</a>".<sup about="#mwt581" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-149" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-149"}}'><a href="#cite_note-149" id="mwA_w"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA_0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA_4">[</span>149<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA_8">]</span></span></a></sup> According to an analysis in <i id="mwBAA"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Political_Science_Quarterly" title="Political Science Quarterly" id="mwBAE">Political Science Quarterly</a></i>, Trump made "explicitly racist and sexist appeals to win over white voters" during his 2016 presidential campaign.<sup about="#mwt561" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTESchaffnerMacwilliamsNteta201831_150-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTESchaffnerMacwilliamsNteta201831"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTESchaffnerMacwilliamsNteta201831-150"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Schaffner"},"2":{"wt":"Macwilliams"},"3":{"wt":"Nteta"},"4":{"wt":"2018"},"p":{"wt":"31"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTESchaffnerMacwilliamsNteta201831-150" id="mwBAI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBAM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBAQ">[</span>150<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBAU">]</span></span></a></sup> In particular, his campaign launch speech drew criticism for claiming Mexican immigrants were rapists and bringing drugs and crime;<sup about="#mwt584" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-151" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-151"}}'><a href="#cite_note-151" id="mwBAY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBAc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBAg">[</span>151<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBAk">]</span></span></a></sup> in response, NBC fired him from <i id="mwBAo">Celebrity Apprentice</i>.<sup about="#mwt587" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-152" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-152"}}'><a href="#cite_note-152" id="mwBAs"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBAw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBA0">[</span>152<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBA4">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwBA8">Trump's FEC-required reports listed assets above $1.4<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwBBA"> </span>billion and outstanding debts of at least $265<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwBBE"> </span>million.<sup about="#mwt590" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-disclosure_153-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"disclosure"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-disclosure-153"}}'><a href="#cite_note-disclosure-153" id="mwBBI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBBM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBBQ">[</span>153<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBBU">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt593" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-154" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-154"}}'><a href="#cite_note-154" id="mwBBY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBBc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBBg">[</span>154<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBBk">]</span></span></a></sup> He did not release <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tax_returns_of_Donald_Trump" title="Tax returns of Donald Trump" id="mwBBo">his tax returns</a>, contrary to the practice of every major candidate since 1976 and to promises he made in 2014 and 2015 to release them if he ran for office.<sup about="#mwt596" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-155" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-155"}}'><a href="#cite_note-155" id="mwBBs"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBBw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBB0">[</span>155<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBB4">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt599" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-156" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-156"}}'><a href="#cite_note-156" id="mwBB8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBCA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBCE">[</span>156<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBCI">]</span></span></a></sup> He said his tax returns were being <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Income_tax_audit" title="Income tax audit" id="mwBCM">audited</a> and that his lawyers had advised him against releasing them.<sup about="#mwt602" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-157" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-157"}}'><a href="#cite_note-157" id="mwBCQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBCU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBCY">[</span>157<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBCc">]</span></span></a></sup> After a lengthy court battle to block release of his tax returns and other records to the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan_district_attorney" title="Manhattan district attorney" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBCg">Manhattan district attorney</a> for a criminal investigation, including two appeals by Trump to the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/U.S._Supreme_Court" title="U.S. Supreme Court" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBCk">U.S. Supreme Court</a>, in February 2021 the high court allowed the records to be released to the prosecutor for review by a grand jury.<sup about="#mwt605" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-158" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-158"}}'><a href="#cite_note-158" id="mwBCo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBCs"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBCw">[</span>158<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBC0">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt608" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-159" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-159"}}'><a href="#cite_note-159" id="mwBC4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBC8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBDA">[</span>159<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBDE">]</span></span></a></sup> In October 2016, portions of Trump's state filings for 1995 were leaked to a reporter from <i id="mwBDI">The New York Times</i>. They show that he had declared a loss of $916<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwBDM"> </span>million that year, which could have let him avoid taxes for up to 18 years.<sup about="#mwt611" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-160" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-160"}}'><a href="#cite_note-160" id="mwBDQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBDU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBDY">[</span>160<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBDc">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwBDg">Trump won the election with 304 <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electoral_College_(United_States)" title="Electoral College (United States)" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBDk">electoral votes</a> versus 227 for Democratic candidate <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hillary_Clinton" title="Hillary Clinton" id="mwBDo">Hillary Clinton</a>.<sup about="#mwt620" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-161" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-161"}}'><a href="#cite_note-161" id="mwBDs"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBDw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBD0">[</span>161<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBD4">]</span></span></a></sup> The fifth person to be elected president <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_United_States_presidential_elections_in_which_the_winner_lost_the_popular_vote" title="List of United States presidential elections in which the winner lost the popular vote" id="mwBD8">despite losing the popular vote</a>,<sup about="#mwt612" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-electoral-college_162-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"electoral-college","group":"lower-alpha"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-electoral-college-162"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"efn","href":"./Template:Efn"},"params":{"name":{"wt":"electoral-college"},"1":{"wt":"Presidential elections in the U.S. are decided by the [[United States Electoral College|Electoral College]]. Each state names a number of electors equal to its representation in [[United States Congress|Congress]] and (in most states) all electors vote for the winner of their state&apos;s popular vote."}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-electoral-college-162" data-mw-group="lower-alpha" id="mwBEA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBEE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBEI">[</span>a<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBEM">]</span></span></a></sup> he received about 2.87<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwBEQ"> </span>million fewer votes than Clinton, 46.1% to her 48.2%.<sup about="#mwt623" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-163" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-163"}}'><a href="#cite_note-163" id="mwBEU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBEY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBEc">[</span>162<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBEg">]</span></span></a></sup> He was the only president who had neither served in the military nor held any government office prior to becoming president.<sup about="#mwt626" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-164" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-164"}}'><a href="#cite_note-164" id="mwBEk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBEo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBEs">[</span>163<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBEw">]</span></span></a></sup> His election marked the return of a Republican <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Divided_government_in_the_United_States" title="Divided government in the United States" id="mwBE0">undivided government</a>.<sup about="#mwt615" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-165" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"lower-alpha"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-165"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"efn","href":"./Template:Efn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"A Republican president combined with Republican control of both chambers of Congress"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-165" data-mw-group="lower-alpha" id="mwBE4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBE8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBFA">[</span>b<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBFE">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt629" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-166" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-166"}}'><a href="#cite_note-166" id="mwBFI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBFM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBFQ">[</span>164<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBFU">]</span></span></a></sup> Trump's victory <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Protests_against_Donald_Trump#Before_first_presidency" title="Protests against Donald Trump" id="mwBFY">sparked protests</a> in major U.S. cities.<sup about="#mwt632" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-167" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-167"}}'><a href="#cite_note-167" id="mwBFc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBFg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBFk">[</span>165<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBFo">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt635" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-168" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-168"}}'><a href="#cite_note-168" id="mwBFs"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBFw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBF0">[</span>166<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBF4">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="15" id="mwBF8" aria-labelledby="First_presidency_(2017–2021)"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id="First_presidency_(2017–2021)"><span id="First_presidency_.282017.E2.80.932021.29" typeof="mw:FallbackId"></span>First presidency (2017–2021)</h2></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt636" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwBGA" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"First presidency of Donald Trump"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt637" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt636" id="mwBGE">Main article: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_presidency_of_Donald_Trump" title="First presidency of Donald Trump">First presidency of Donald Trump</a></div>

<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt638" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwBGI" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"for timeline","href":"./Template:For_timeline"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Timeline of the Donald Trump presidencies#First presidency (2017–2021)"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt639" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt638" id="mwBGM">For a chronological guide, see <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_the_Donald_Trump_presidencies#First_presidency_(2017–2021)" title="Timeline of the Donald Trump presidencies">Timeline of the Donald Trump presidencies §<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>First presidency (2017–2021)</a>.</div>
<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwBGQ"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Donald_Trump_swearing_in_ceremony.jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwBGU"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Donald_Trump_swearing_in_ceremony.jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Donald_Trump_swearing_in_ceremony.jpg/250px-Donald_Trump_swearing_in_ceremony.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Donald_Trump_swearing_in_ceremony.jpg/500px-Donald_Trump_swearing_in_ceremony.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" alt="Trump, with his family watching, raises his right hand and places his left hand on the Bible as he takes the oath of office. Roberts stands opposite him administering the oath" data-file-width="1600" data-file-height="1066" data-file-type="bitmap" height="167" width="250" class="mw-file-element" id="mwBGY"/></a><figcaption id="mwBGc">Taking the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oath_of_office_of_the_President_of_the_United_States" title="Oath of office of the President of the United States" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBGg">presidential oath of office</a>, administered by Chief Justice <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Roberts" title="John Roberts" id="mwBGk">John Roberts</a>, on January<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwBGo"> </span>20, 2017</figcaption></figure>
<p id="mwBGs">Trump <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_inauguration_of_Donald_Trump" title="First inauguration of Donald Trump" id="mwBGw">was inaugurated</a> on January<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwBG0"> </span>20, 2017. The day after his inauguration, an estimated 2.6<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwBG4"> </span>million people worldwide, including 500,000 in Washington, D.C., protested against him in the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2017_Women's_March" title="2017 Women's March" id="mwBG8">Women's Marches</a>.<sup about="#mwt642" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-169" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-169"}}'><a href="#cite_note-169" id="mwBHA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBHE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBHI">[</span>167<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBHM">]</span></span></a></sup> During his first two weeks in office, Trump signed <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_executive_orders_in_the_first_Trump_presidency" title="List of executive orders in the first Trump presidency" id="mwBHQ">eighteen executive orders</a>, including authorizing procedures for repealing the Patient Protection and Affordable Care Act ("Obamacare"), withdrawal from the Trans-Pacific Partnership negotiations, advancement of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Keystone_Pipeline" title="Keystone Pipeline" id="mwBHU">Keystone XL</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dakota_Access_Pipeline" title="Dakota Access Pipeline" id="mwBHY">Dakota Access Pipeline</a> projects, and planning for a wall along the U.S. border with Mexico.<sup about="#mwt645" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-170" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-170"}}'><a href="#cite_note-170" id="mwBHc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBHg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBHk">[</span>168<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBHo">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<section data-mw-section-id="16" id="mwBHs" aria-labelledby="Conflicts_of_interest"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Conflicts_of_interest">Conflicts of interest</h3></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt646" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwBHw" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"see also","href":"./Template:See_also"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"First presidency of Donald Trump#Ethics"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt647" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt646" id="mwBH0">See also: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_presidency_of_Donald_Trump#Ethics" title="First presidency of Donald Trump">First presidency of Donald Trump §<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>Ethics</a></div>
<p id="mwBH4">Trump continued to profit from his businesses during his first presidency and knew how his administration's policies affected them.<sup about="#mwt650" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-BBC041817_171-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"BBC041817"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-BBC041817-171"}}'><a href="#cite_note-BBC041817-171" id="mwBH8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBIA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBIE">[</span>169<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBII">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt653" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Venook_172-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Venook"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Venook-172"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Venook-172" id="mwBIM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBIQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBIU">[</span>170<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBIY">]</span></span></a></sup> He claimed he would eschew "new foreign deals", but the Trump Organization pursued operational expansions in Scotland, Dubai, and the Dominican Republic.<sup about="#mwt654" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-BBC041817_171-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"BBC041817"}}'><a href="#cite_note-BBC041817-171" id="mwBIc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBIg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBIk">[</span>169<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBIo">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt655" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Venook_172-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Venook"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Venook-172" id="mwBIs"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBIw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBI0">[</span>170<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBI4">]</span></span></a></sup> Lobbyists, foreign government officials, and Trump donors and allies generated hundreds of millions of dollars for his resorts and hotels.<sup about="#mwt658" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-173" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-173"}}'><a href="#cite_note-173" id="mwBI8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBJA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBJE">[</span>171<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBJI">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="17" id="mwBJM" aria-labelledby="Domestic_policy"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Domestic_policy">Domestic policy</h3></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt659" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwBJQ" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Domestic policy of the first Trump administration"},"2":{"wt":"Economic policy of the first Trump administration"},"3":{"wt":"Environmental policy of the first Trump administration"},"4":{"wt":"Social policy of the first Trump administration"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt660" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt659" id="mwBJU">Main articles: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Domestic_policy_of_the_first_Trump_administration" title="Domestic policy of the first Trump administration">Domestic policy of the first Trump administration</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Economic_policy_of_the_first_Trump_administration" title="Economic policy of the first Trump administration">Economic policy of the first Trump administration</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Environmental_policy_of_the_first_Trump_administration" title="Environmental policy of the first Trump administration">Environmental policy of the first Trump administration</a>, and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Social_policy_of_the_first_Trump_administration" title="Social policy of the first Trump administration">Social policy of the first Trump administration</a></div>

<p id="mwBJY">Trump took office at the height of the longest <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Economic_expansion" title="Economic expansion" id="mwBJc">economic expansion</a> in American history,<sup about="#mwt663" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-VanDam_174-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"VanDam"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-VanDam-174"}}'><a href="#cite_note-VanDam-174" id="mwBJg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBJk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBJo">[</span>172<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBJs">]</span></span></a></sup> which began in 2009 and continued until February 2020, when the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/COVID-19_recession" title="COVID-19 recession" id="mwBJw">COVID-19 recession</a> began.<sup about="#mwt666" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-175" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-175"}}'><a href="#cite_note-175" id="mwBJ0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBJ4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBJ8">[</span>173<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBKA">]</span></span></a></sup> In December 2017, he signed the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tax_Cuts_and_Jobs_Act_of_2017" title="Tax Cuts and Jobs Act of 2017" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBKE">Tax Cuts and Jobs Act of 2017</a>, which reduced tax rates for businesses and individuals and eliminated the penalty associated with the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Affordable_Care_Act" title="Affordable Care Act" id="mwBKI">Affordable Care Act</a>'s individual mandate.<sup about="#mwt669" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-176" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-176"}}'><a href="#cite_note-176" id="mwBKM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBKQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBKU">[</span>174<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBKY">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt672" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-177" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-177"}}'><a href="#cite_note-177" id="mwBKc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBKg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBKk">[</span>175<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBKo">]</span></span></a></sup> Under Trump, the federal budget deficit increased by almost 50<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwBKs"> </span>percent, to nearly $1<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwBKw"> </span>trillion in 2019.<sup about="#mwt675" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-178" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-178"}}'><a href="#cite_note-178" id="mwBK0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBK4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBK8">[</span>176<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBLA">]</span></span></a></sup> By the end of his term, the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/U.S._national_debt" title="U.S. national debt" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBLE">U.S. national debt</a> increased by 39<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwBLI"> </span>percent, reaching $27.75<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwBLM"> </span>trillion, and the U.S. <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Debt-to-GDP_ratio" title="Debt-to-GDP ratio" id="mwBLQ">debt-to-GDP ratio</a> hit a post-World War II high.<sup about="#mwt678" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-179" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-179"}}'><a href="#cite_note-179" id="mwBLU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBLY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBLc">[</span>177<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBLg">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwBLk">Trump rejects the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scientific_consensus_on_climate_change" title="Scientific consensus on climate change" id="mwBLo">scientific consensus on climate change</a>.<sup about="#mwt687" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-180" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-180"}}'><a href="#cite_note-180" id="mwBLs"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBLw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBL0">[</span>178<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBL4">]</span></span></a></sup>
He reduced the budget for <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Renewable_energy_in_the_United_States#Renewable_energy_research" title="Renewable energy in the United States" id="mwBL8">renewable energy</a> research by 40 percent and reversed Obama-era policies directed at curbing <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Climate_change" title="Climate change" id="mwBMA">climate change</a>.<sup about="#mwt690" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-181" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-181"}}'><a href="#cite_note-181" id="mwBME"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBMI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBMM">[</span>179<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBMQ">]</span></span></a></sup> He <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_and_the_Paris_Agreement" title="United States and the Paris Agreement" id="mwBMU">withdrew from the Paris Agreement</a>, making the U.S. the only nation to not ratify it.<sup about="#mwt693" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-182" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-182"}}'><a href="#cite_note-182" id="mwBMY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBMc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBMg">[</span>180<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBMk">]</span></span></a></sup></p>
 
<p id="mwBMo">Trump aimed and aims to boost the production and exports of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fossil_fuel" title="Fossil fuel" id="mwBMs">fossil fuels</a>.<sup about="#mwt696" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-183" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-183"}}'><a href="#cite_note-183" id="mwBMw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBM0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBM4">[</span>181<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBM8">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt699" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-184" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-184"}}'><a href="#cite_note-184" id="mwBNA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBNE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBNI">[</span>182<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBNM">]</span></span></a></sup> Natural gas production expanded under Trump, but that of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coal_mining_in_the_United_States#Production" title="Coal mining in the United States" id="mwBNQ">coal continued to decline</a>.<sup about="#mwt702" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-185" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-185"}}'><a href="#cite_note-185" id="mwBNU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBNY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBNc">[</span>183<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBNg">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt705" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Subramaniam_186-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Subramaniam"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Subramaniam-186"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Subramaniam-186" id="mwBNk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBNo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBNs">[</span>184<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBNw">]</span></span></a></sup> 
He <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rollback" title="Rollback" id="mwBN0">rolled back</a> more than 100 federal environmental regulations, including those that curbed <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greenhouse_gas_emissions" title="Greenhouse gas emissions" id="mwBN4">greenhouse gas emissions</a>, air and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Water_pollution" title="Water pollution" id="mwBN8">water pollution</a>, and the use of toxic substances. He weakened protections for animals and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Environmental_standard#United_States" title="Environmental standard" id="mwBOA">environmental standards</a> for federal infrastructure projects and expanded permitted areas for drilling and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Resource_extraction" title="Resource extraction" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBOE">resource extraction</a>, such as allowing <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arctic_Refuge_drilling_controversy" title="Arctic Refuge drilling controversy" id="mwBOI">drilling in the Arctic Refuge</a>.<sup about="#mwt708" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-187" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-187"}}'><a href="#cite_note-187" id="mwBOM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBOQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBOU">[</span>185<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBOY">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwBOc">Trump dismantled federal regulations on health,<sup about="#mwt711" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-188" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-188"}}'><a href="#cite_note-188" id="mwBOg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBOk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBOo">[</span>186<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBOs">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt714" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-midnight_189-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"midnight"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-midnight-189"}}'><a href="#cite_note-midnight-189" id="mwBOw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBO0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBO4">[</span>187<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBO8">]</span></span></a></sup> labor,<sup about="#mwt715" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-midnight_189-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"midnight"}}'><a href="#cite_note-midnight-189" id="mwBPA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBPE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBPI">[</span>187<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBPM">]</span></span></a></sup> the environment,<sup about="#mwt718" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-190" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-190"}}'><a href="#cite_note-190" id="mwBPQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBPU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBPY">[</span>188<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBPc">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt719" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-midnight_189-2" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"midnight"}}'><a href="#cite_note-midnight-189" id="mwBPg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBPk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBPo">[</span>187<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBPs">]</span></span></a></sup> and other areas, including a bill that revoked the Obama-era regulation restricting the sale of firearms to people diagnosed with severe mental illness.<sup about="#mwt722" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-191" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-191"}}'><a href="#cite_note-191" id="mwBPw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBP0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBP4">[</span>189<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBP8">]</span></span></a></sup> During his first six weeks in office, he delayed, suspended, or reversed ninety federal regulations,<sup about="#mwt725" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-192" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-192"}}'><a href="#cite_note-192" id="mwBQA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBQE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBQI">[</span>190<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBQM">]</span></span></a></sup> often "after requests by the regulated industries".<sup about="#mwt728" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-193" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-193"}}'><a href="#cite_note-193" id="mwBQQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBQU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBQY">[</span>191<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBQc">]</span></span></a></sup></p> 

<p id="mwBQg">Trump vowed to repeal and replace the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Affordable_Care_Act" title="Affordable Care Act" id="mwBQk">Affordable Care Act</a>.<sup about="#mwt731" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-194" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-194"}}'><a href="#cite_note-194" id="mwBQo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBQs"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBQw">[</span>192<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBQ0">]</span></span></a></sup> He scaled back the act's implementation through executive orders.<sup about="#mwt734" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-195" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-195"}}'><a href="#cite_note-195" id="mwBQ4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBQ8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBRA">[</span>193<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBRE">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt737" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-196" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-196"}}'><a href="#cite_note-196" id="mwBRI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBRM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBRQ">[</span>194<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBRU">]</span></span></a></sup> He expressed a desire to "let Obamacare fail"; his administration halved the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Annual_enrollment" title="Annual enrollment" id="mwBRY">enrollment period</a> and drastically reduced funding for enrollment promotion.<sup about="#mwt740" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-197" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-197"}}'><a href="#cite_note-197" id="mwBRc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBRg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBRk">[</span>195<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBRo">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt743" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-198" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-198"}}'><a href="#cite_note-198" id="mwBRs"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBRw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBR0">[</span>196<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBR4">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwBR8">In response to the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Opioid_epidemic_in_the_United_States" title="Opioid epidemic in the United States" id="mwBSA">opioid epidemic</a>, Trump signed legislation in 2018 to increase funding for drug treatments but was widely criticized for failing to make a concrete strategy.<sup about="#mwt746" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-199" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-199"}}'><a href="#cite_note-199" id="mwBSE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBSI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBSM">[</span>197<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBSQ">]</span></span></a></sup> He barred organizations that provide abortions or abortion referrals from receiving federal funds.<sup about="#mwt749" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-200" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-200"}}'><a href="#cite_note-200" id="mwBSU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBSY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBSc">[</span>198<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBSg">]</span></span></a></sup> His administration rolled back key components of the Obama administration's workplace protections against <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Discrimination_against_LGBT_people" title="Discrimination against LGBT people" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBSk">discrimination of LGBTQ people</a>.<sup about="#mwt752" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-201" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-201"}}'><a href="#cite_note-201" id="mwBSo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBSs"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBSw">[</span>199<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBS0">]</span></span></a></sup> His attempted rollback of anti-discrimination protections for <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Transgender" title="Transgender" id="mwBS4">transgender</a> patients in August 2020 was halted by a federal judge after a Supreme Court ruling extended employees' civil rights protections to <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gender_identity" title="Gender identity" id="mwBS8">gender identity</a> and sexual orientation.<sup about="#mwt755" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-202" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-202"}}'><a href="#cite_note-202" id="mwBTA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBTE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBTI">[</span>200<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBTM">]</span></span></a></sup> His administration <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cannabis_policy_of_the_first_Trump_administration" title="Cannabis policy of the first Trump administration" id="mwBTQ">took an anti-marijuana position</a>, revoking <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cole_Memorandum" title="Cole Memorandum" id="mwBTU">Obama-era policies</a> that provided protections for states that legalized marijuana.<sup about="#mwt758" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-203" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-203"}}'><a href="#cite_note-203" id="mwBTY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBTc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBTg">[</span>201<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBTk">]</span></span></a></sup> Trump is a long-time advocate of capital punishment,<sup about="#mwt761" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-204" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-204"}}'><a href="#cite_note-204" id="mwBTo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBTs"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBTw">[</span>202<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBT0">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt764" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-205" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-205"}}'><a href="#cite_note-205" id="mwBT4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBT8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBUA">[</span>203<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBUE">]</span></span></a></sup> and his administration oversaw the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Capital_punishment_by_the_United_States_federal_government" title="Capital punishment by the United States federal government" id="mwBUI">federal government execute</a> 13 prisoners, more than in the previous 56 years combined, ending a 17-year moratorium.<sup about="#mwt767" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-206" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-206"}}'><a href="#cite_note-206" id="mwBUM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBUQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBUU">[</span>204<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBUY">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="18" id="mwBUc" aria-labelledby="Pardons_and_commutations"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Pardons_and_commutations">Pardons and commutations</h3></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt768" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwBUg" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"further","href":"./Template:Further"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"List of people granted executive clemency in the first Trump presidency"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt769" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt768" id="mwBUk">Further information: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_people_granted_executive_clemency_in_the_first_Trump_presidency" title="List of people granted executive clemency in the first Trump presidency">List of people granted executive clemency in the first Trump presidency</a></div>
<p id="mwBUo">During his first term, Trump granted 237 requests for clemency, fewer than all presidents since 1900 with the exception of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_H._W._Bush" title="George H. W. Bush" id="mwBUs">George H. W. Bush</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_W._Bush" title="George W. Bush" id="mwBUw">George W. Bush</a>.<sup about="#mwt772" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-207" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-207"}}'><a href="#cite_note-207" id="mwBU0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBU4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBU8">[</span>205<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBVA">]</span></span></a></sup> Only 25 of them had been vetted by the Justice Department's <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Office_of_the_Pardon_Attorney" title="Office of the Pardon Attorney" id="mwBVE">Office of the Pardon Attorney</a>; the others were granted to people with personal or political connections to him, his family, and his allies, or recommended by celebrities.<sup about="#mwt775" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-road_208-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"road"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-road-208"}}'><a href="#cite_note-road-208" id="mwBVI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBVM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBVQ">[</span>206<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBVU">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt778" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-OloDaw_209-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"OloDaw"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-OloDaw-209"}}'><a href="#cite_note-OloDaw-209" id="mwBVY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBVc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBVg">[</span>207<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBVk">]</span></span></a></sup> In his last full day in office, he granted 73 pardons and commuted 70 sentences.<sup about="#mwt781" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-210" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-210"}}'><a href="#cite_note-210" id="mwBVo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBVs"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBVw">[</span>208<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBV0">]</span></span></a></sup> The pardons of three military service members convicted of or charged with violent crimes were opposed by military leaders.<sup about="#mwt784" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-211" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-211"}}'><a href="#cite_note-211" id="mwBV4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBV8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBWA">[</span>209<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBWE">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="19" id="mwBWI" aria-labelledby="Immigration"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Immigration">Immigration</h3></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt785" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwBWM" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Immigration policy of the first Trump administration"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt786" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt785" id="mwBWQ">Main article: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Immigration_policy_of_the_first_Trump_administration" title="Immigration policy of the first Trump administration">Immigration policy of the first Trump administration</a></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt787" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwBWU" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"further","href":"./Template:Further"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Mexico–United States border crisis#First Trump administration (2017–2021)"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt788" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt787" id="mwBWY">Further information: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mexico–United_States_border_crisis#First_Trump_administration_(2017–2021)" title="Mexico–United States border crisis">Mexico–United States border crisis §<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>First Trump administration (2017–2021)</a></div>
<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwBWc"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Texas_Photo_1.jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwBWg"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Texas_Photo_1.jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/Ursula_%28detention_center%29_2-res.jpg/250px-Ursula_%28detention_center%29_2-res.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/Ursula_%28detention_center%29_2-res.jpg/500px-Ursula_%28detention_center%29_2-res.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="4016" data-file-height="3012" data-file-type="bitmap" height="188" width="250" class="mw-file-element" id="mwBWk"/></a><figcaption id="mwBWo">Children, sleeping mats, and foil blankets in wire mesh compartment, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ursula_(detention_center)" title="Ursula (detention center)" id="mwBWs">Ursula detention facility</a>, June 2018</figcaption></figure>
<p id="mwBWw">As president, Trump described illegal immigration as an "invasion" of the United States<sup about="#mwt802" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-212" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-212"}}'><a href="#cite_note-212" id="mwBW0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBW4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBW8">[</span>210<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBXA">]</span></span></a></sup> and drastically escalated immigration enforcement.<sup about="#mwt789" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEJohnson2017a_213-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEJohnson2017a"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEJohnson2017a-213"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Johnson"},"2":{"wt":"2017a"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEJohnson2017a-213" id="mwBXE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBXI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBXM">[</span>211<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBXQ">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt792" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEJohnsonCuison-Villazor2019_214-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEJohnsonCuison-Villazor2019"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEJohnsonCuison-Villazor2019-214"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Johnson"},"2":{"wt":"Cuison-Villazor"},"3":{"wt":"2019"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEJohnsonCuison-Villazor2019-214" id="mwBXU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBXY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBXc">[</span>212<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBXg">]</span></span></a></sup> He implemented harsh policies against asylum seekers<sup about="#mwt795" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEJohnsonCuison-Villazor2019_214-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEJohnsonCuison-Villazor2019"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEJohnsonCuison-Villazor2019-214"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Johnson"},"2":{"wt":"Cuison-Villazor"},"3":{"wt":"2019"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEJohnsonCuison-Villazor2019-214" id="mwBXk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBXo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBXs">[</span>212<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBXw">]</span></span></a></sup> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Faithful_Patriot" title="Operation Faithful Patriot" id="mwBX0">deployed nearly 6,000 troops</a> to the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/U.S.–Mexico_border" title="U.S.–Mexico border" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBX4">U.S.–Mexico border</a> to stop illegal crossings.<sup about="#mwt805" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-215" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-215"}}'><a href="#cite_note-215" id="mwBX8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBYA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBYE">[</span>213<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBYI">]</span></span></a></sup> He reduced the number of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Refugee_Admissions_Program" title="United States Refugee Admissions Program" id="mwBYM">refugees admitted</a> to record lows, from an annual limit of 110,000 before he took office to 15,000 in 2021.<sup about="#mwt808" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-216" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-216"}}'><a href="#cite_note-216" id="mwBYQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBYU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBYY">[</span>214<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBYc">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt811" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-217" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-217"}}'><a href="#cite_note-217" id="mwBYg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBYk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBYo">[</span>215<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBYs">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt814" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-218" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-218"}}'><a href="#cite_note-218" id="mwBYw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBY0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBY4">[</span>216<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBY8">]</span></span></a></sup> Trump also increased restrictions on granting <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Permanent_residency_in_the_United_States" title="Permanent residency in the United States" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBZA">permanent residency</a> to <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Public_charge_rule" title="Public charge rule" id="mwBZE">immigrants needing public benefits</a>.<sup about="#mwt817" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-219" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-219"}}'><a href="#cite_note-219" id="mwBZI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBZM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBZQ">[</span>217<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBZU">]</span></span></a></sup> One of his central campaign promises was to build a <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mexico–United_States_border_wall" title="Mexico–United States border wall" id="mwBZY">wall along the U.S.<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwBZc">–</span>Mexico border</a>;<sup about="#mwt820" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-timm_220-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"timm"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-timm-220"}}'><a href="#cite_note-timm-220" id="mwBZg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBZk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBZo">[</span>218<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBZs">]</span></span></a></sup> during his first term, the U.S. built <span about="#mwt798" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwBZw" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"convert","href":"./Template:Convert"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"73"},"2":{"wt":"mi"},"3":{"wt":"km"}},"i":0}}]}'>73 miles (117</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt798"> </span><span about="#mwt798">km)</span> of wall in areas without barriers and <span about="#mwt799" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwBZ0" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"convert","href":"./Template:Convert"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"365"},"2":{"wt":"mi"},"3":{"wt":"km"}},"i":0}}]}'>365 miles (587</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt799"> </span><span about="#mwt799">km)</span> to replace older barriers.<sup about="#mwt823" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-221" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-221"}}'><a href="#cite_note-221" id="mwBZ4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBZ8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBaA">[</span>219<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBaE">]</span></span></a></sup> In 2018, Trump's refusal to sign any <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spending_bill" title="Spending bill" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBaI">spending bill</a> unless it allocated funding for the border wall<sup about="#mwt826" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-222" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-222"}}'><a href="#cite_note-222" id="mwBaM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBaQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBaU">[</span>220<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBaY">]</span></span></a></sup> resulted in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Government_shutdowns_in_the_United_States#2025" title="Government shutdowns in the United States" id="mwBac">the then-longest federal government shutdown</a> ever, for 35 days from December 2018 to January 2019,<sup about="#mwt829" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Gambino_223-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Gambino"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Gambino-223"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Gambino-223" id="mwBag"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBak"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBao">[</span>221<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBas">]</span></span></a></sup> later surpassed by the 2025 government shutdown.<sup about="#mwt832" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-longer_224-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"longer"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-longer-224"}}'><a href="#cite_note-longer-224" id="mwBaw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBa0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBa4">[</span>222<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBa8">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwBbA">The shutdown ended after he agreed to fund the government without any funds for the wall.<sup about="#mwt833" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Gambino_223-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Gambino"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Gambino-223" id="mwBbE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBbI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBbM">[</span>221<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBbQ">]</span></span></a></sup> To avoid another shutdown, Congress passed a funding bill with $1.4<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwBbU"> </span>billion for border fencing in February.<sup about="#mwt836" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Wilkie_225-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Wilkie"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Wilkie-225"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Wilkie-225" id="mwBbY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBbc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBbg">[</span>223<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBbk">]</span></span></a></sup> Trump later declared a <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Emergency_Concerning_the_Southern_Border_of_the_United_States" title="National Emergency Concerning the Southern Border of the United States" id="mwBbo">national emergency on the southern border</a> to divert $6.1<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwBbs"> </span>billion of funding to the border wall<sup about="#mwt837" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Wilkie_225-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Wilkie"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Wilkie-225" id="mwBbw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBb0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBb4">[</span>223<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBb8">]</span></span></a></sup> despite congressional disagreement.<sup about="#mwt840" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-226" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-226"}}'><a href="#cite_note-226" id="mwBcA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBcE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBcI">[</span>224<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBcM">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwBcQ">In January 2017, Trump signed <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Executive_Order_13769" title="Executive Order 13769" id="mwBcU">an executive order</a> that <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trump_travel_ban" title="Trump travel ban" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBcY">denied entry to citizens from six Muslim-majority countries</a> for four months and from Syria indefinitely.<sup about="#mwt843" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-frontline_227-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"frontline"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-frontline-227"}}'><a href="#cite_note-frontline-227" id="mwBcc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBcg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBck">[</span>225<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBco">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt846" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-airport_228-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"airport"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-airport-228"}}'><a href="#cite_note-airport-228" id="mwBcs"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBcw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBc0">[</span>226<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBc4">]</span></span></a></sup> The order caused <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Protests_against_Executive_Order_13769" title="Protests against Executive Order 13769" id="mwBc8">many protests</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Legal_challenges_to_the_Trump_travel_ban" title="Legal challenges to the Trump travel ban" id="mwBdA">legal challenges</a> that resulted in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nationwide_injunction" title="Nationwide injunction" id="mwBdE">nationwide injunctions</a>.<sup about="#mwt847" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-frontline_227-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"frontline"}}'><a href="#cite_note-frontline-227" id="mwBdI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBdM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBdQ">[</span>225<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBdU">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt848" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-airport_228-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"airport"}}'><a href="#cite_note-airport-228" id="mwBdY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBdc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBdg">[</span>226<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBdk">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt851" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-229" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-229"}}'><a href="#cite_note-229" id="mwBdo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBds"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBdw">[</span>227<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBd0">]</span></span></a></sup> <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Executive_Order_13780" title="Executive Order 13780" id="mwBd4">A revised order</a> was also blocked by courts,<sup about="#mwt854" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-230" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-230"}}'><a href="#cite_note-230" id="mwBd8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBeA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBeE">[</span>228<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBeI">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt857" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-231" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-231"}}'><a href="#cite_note-231" id="mwBeM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBeQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBeU">[</span>229<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBeY">]</span></span></a></sup> but <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/International_Refugee_Assistance_Project_v._Trump" title="International Refugee Assistance Project v. Trump" id="mwBec">the Supreme Court ruled in June</a> that the ban could be enforced on those lacking "a credible claim of a bona fide relationship with a person or entity" in the U.S.<sup about="#mwt860" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-232" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-232"}}'><a href="#cite_note-232" id="mwBeg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBek"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBeo">[</span>230<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBes">]</span></span></a></sup> Trump replaced the ban in September with <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Presidential_Proclamation_9645" title="Presidential Proclamation 9645" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBew">a presidential proclamation</a> extending travel bans to North Koreans, Chadians, and some Venezuelan officials but excluded Iraq and Sudan.<sup about="#mwt863" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-233" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-233"}}'><a href="#cite_note-233" id="mwBe0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBe4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBe8">[</span>231<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBfA">]</span></span></a></sup> The Supreme Court allowed that version to go into effect in December 2017,<sup about="#mwt866" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-234" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-234"}}'><a href="#cite_note-234" id="mwBfE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBfI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBfM">[</span>232<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBfQ">]</span></span></a></sup> and ultimately upheld the ban in 2019.<sup about="#mwt869" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-235" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-235"}}'><a href="#cite_note-235" id="mwBfU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBfY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBfc">[</span>233<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBfg">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwBfk">From 2017 to 2018, the Trump administration had <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trump_administration_family_separation_policy" title="Trump administration family separation policy" id="mwBfo">a policy of family separation</a> that separated over 4,400 children, some as young as four months old,<sup about="#mwt872" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-tina_v_236-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"tina_v"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-tina_v-236"}}'><a href="#cite_note-tina_v-236" id="mwBfs"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBfw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBf0">[</span>234<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBf4">]</span></span></a></sup> from migrant parents at the U.S.<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwBf8">–</span>Mexico border.<sup about="#mwt875" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-237" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-237"}}'><a href="#cite_note-237" id="mwBgA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBgE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBgI">[</span>235<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBgM">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt878" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Spagat_238-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Spagat"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Spagat-238"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Spagat-238" id="mwBgQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBgU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBgY">[</span>236<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBgc">]</span></span></a></sup> The unprecedented<sup about="#mwt881" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Domonoske_239-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Domonoske"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Domonoske-239"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Domonoske-239" id="mwBgg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBgk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBgo">[</span>237<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBgs">]</span></span></a></sup> policy sparked public outrage in the country.<sup about="#mwt884" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-240" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-240"}}'><a href="#cite_note-240" id="mwBgw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBg0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBg4">[</span>238<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBg8">]</span></span></a></sup> Despite Trump initially blaming Democrats<sup about="#mwt887" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-241" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-241"}}'><a href="#cite_note-241" id="mwBhA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBhE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBhI">[</span>239<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBhM">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt890" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-242" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-242"}}'><a href="#cite_note-242" id="mwBhQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBhU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBhY">[</span>240<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBhc">]</span></span></a></sup> and insisting he could not stop the policy with an executive order, he acceded to public pressure in June 2018 and mandated that illegal immigrant families be detained together unless "there is a concern" of risk for the child.<sup about="#mwt893" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-243" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-243"}}'><a href="#cite_note-243" id="mwBhg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBhk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBho">[</span>241<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBhs">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt896" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-244" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-244"}}'><a href="#cite_note-244" id="mwBhw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBh0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBh4">[</span>242<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBh8">]</span></span></a></sup> A federal judge later ordered that the families be reunited and further separations stopped except in limited circumstances,<sup about="#mwt899" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-245" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-245"}}'><a href="#cite_note-245" id="mwBiA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBiE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBiI">[</span>243<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBiM">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt902" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-246" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-246"}}'><a href="#cite_note-246" id="mwBiQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBiU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBiY">[</span>244<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBic">]</span></span></a></sup> though over 1,000 additional children were separated from their families after the order.<sup about="#mwt903" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Spagat_238-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Spagat"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Spagat-238" id="mwBig"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBik"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBio">[</span>236<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBis">]</span></span></a></sup> By April 2024, 1,360 children had not been confirmed as reunified with their families.<sup about="#mwt904" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-tina_v_236-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"tina_v"}}'><a href="#cite_note-tina_v-236" id="mwBiw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBi0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBi4">[</span>234<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBi8">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="20" id="mwBjA" aria-labelledby="Foreign_policy"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Foreign_policy">Foreign policy</h3></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt905" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwBjE" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Foreign policy of the first Trump administration"},"2":{"wt":"Tariffs in the first Trump administration"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt906" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt905" id="mwBjI">Main articles: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foreign_policy_of_the_first_Trump_administration" title="Foreign policy of the first Trump administration">Foreign policy of the first Trump administration</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tariffs_in_the_first_Trump_administration" title="Tariffs in the first Trump administration">Tariffs in the first Trump administration</a></div>

<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt907" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwBjM" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"further","href":"./Template:Further"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Russia–United States relations#First Trump administration (2017–2021)"},"2":{"wt":"China–United States relations#First Trump administration (2017–2021)"},"3":{"wt":"2017–2018 North Korea crisis"},"4":{"wt":"2018–19 Korean peace process"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt908" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt907" id="mwBjQ">Further information: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russia–United_States_relations#First_Trump_administration_(2017–2021)" title="Russia–United States relations">Russia–United States relations §<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>First Trump administration (2017–2021)</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China–United_States_relations#First_Trump_administration_(2017–2021)" title="China–United States relations">China–United States relations §<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>First Trump administration (2017–2021)</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2017–2018_North_Korea_crisis" title="2017–2018 North Korea crisis">2017–2018 North Korea crisis</a>, and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2018–19_Korean_peace_process" title="2018–19 Korean peace process">2018–19 Korean peace process</a></div>
<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwBjU"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:-G7Biarritz_(48616362963).jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwBjY"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:-G7Biarritz_(48616362963).jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/-G7Biarritz_%2848616362963%29.jpg/250px--G7Biarritz_%2848616362963%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/-G7Biarritz_%2848616362963%29.jpg/500px--G7Biarritz_%2848616362963%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" alt='A group of seven men and one woman, sitting at a round conference table. Trump wears a dark blue suit, white dress shirt, and light blue necktie. A small sign reading "G7 France Biarritz 2019" hangs on the wall behind them.' data-file-width="2800" data-file-height="1867" data-file-type="bitmap" height="167" width="250" class="mw-file-element" id="mwBjc"/></a><figcaption id="mwBjg"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Group_of_Seven" title="Group of Seven" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBjk">G7</a> leaders at the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/45th_G7_summit" title="45th G7 summit" id="mwBjo">45th summit</a> in France, 2019</figcaption></figure>
<p id="mwBjs">Trump described himself as a "nationalist"<sup about="#mwt914" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-247" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-247"}}'><a href="#cite_note-247" id="mwBjw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBj0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBj4">[</span>245<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBj8">]</span></span></a></sup> and his foreign policy as "<a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/America_First" title="America First" id="mwBkA">America First</a>".<sup about="#mwt917" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Bennhold_248-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Bennhold"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Bennhold-248"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Bennhold-248" id="mwBkE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBkI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBkM">[</span>246<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBkQ">]</span></span></a></sup> He supported <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Populist" title="Populist" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBkU">populist</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neo-nationalist" title="Neo-nationalist" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBkY">neo-nationalist</a>, and authoritarian governments.<sup about="#mwt920" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-249" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-249"}}'><a href="#cite_note-249" id="mwBkc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBkg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBkk">[</span>247<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBko">]</span></span></a></sup> Unpredictability, uncertainty, and inconsistency characterized foreign relations during his tenure.<sup about="#mwt921" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Bennhold_248-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Bennhold"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Bennhold-248" id="mwBks"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBkw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBk0">[</span>246<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBk4">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt909" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEMcGurk2020_250-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEMcGurk2020"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEMcGurk2020-250"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"McGurk"},"2":{"wt":"2020"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEMcGurk2020-250" id="mwBk8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBlA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBlE">[</span>248<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBlI">]</span></span></a></sup> Relations between the U.S. and its European allies were strained under Trump.<sup about="#mwt924" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-251" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-251"}}'><a href="#cite_note-251" id="mwBlM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBlQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBlU">[</span>249<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBlY">]</span></span></a></sup> He criticized <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Member_states_of_NATO" title="Member states of NATO" id="mwBlc">NATO allies</a> and privately suggested that the U.S. should <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Withdrawal_from_NATO#United_States" title="Withdrawal from NATO" id="mwBlg">withdraw from NATO</a>.<sup about="#mwt927" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-252" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-252"}}'><a href="#cite_note-252" id="mwBlk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBlo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBls">[</span>250<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBlw">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt930" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-253" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-253"}}'><a href="#cite_note-253" id="mwBl0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBl4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBl8">[</span>251<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBmA">]</span></span></a></sup></p> 

<p id="mwBmE">Trump supported many of the policies of Israeli prime minister <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benjamin_Netanyahu" title="Benjamin Netanyahu" id="mwBmI">Benjamin Netanyahu</a>.<sup about="#mwt933" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-254" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-254"}}'><a href="#cite_note-254" id="mwBmM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBmQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBmU">[</span>252<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBmY">]</span></span></a></sup> In 2020, Trump hosted the signing of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abraham_Accords" title="Abraham Accords" id="mwBmc">Abraham Accords</a> between Israel and the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Arab_Emirates" title="United Arab Emirates" id="mwBmg">United Arab Emirates</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bahrain" title="Bahrain" id="mwBmk">Bahrain</a> to normalize their foreign relations.<sup about="#mwt936" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-255" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-255"}}'><a href="#cite_note-255" id="mwBmo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBms"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBmw">[</span>253<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBm0">]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwBm4"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:President_Trump_&amp;_the_First_Lady's_Trip_to_Europe_(42547210635).jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwBm8"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:President_Trump_&amp;_the_First_Lady's_Trip_to_Europe_(42547210635).jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/President_Trump_%26_the_First_Lady%27s_Trip_to_Europe_%2842547210635%29.jpg/250px-President_Trump_%26_the_First_Lady%27s_Trip_to_Europe_%2842547210635%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/President_Trump_%26_the_First_Lady%27s_Trip_to_Europe_%2842547210635%29.jpg/500px-President_Trump_%26_the_First_Lady%27s_Trip_to_Europe_%2842547210635%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="1200" data-file-height="800" data-file-type="bitmap" height="167" width="250" class="mw-file-element" id="mwBnA"/></a><figcaption id="mwBnE">Shaking hands with Russian president Vladimir Putin during the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2018_Russia–United_States_Summit" title="2018 Russia–United States Summit" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBnI">2018 summit</a> in Helsinki, Finland</figcaption></figure>
<p id="mwBnM">Trump began <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China–United_States_trade_war" title="China–United States trade war" id="mwBnQ">a trade war with China</a> in 2018 after imposing tariffs and other trade barriers he said would force China to end long-standing unfair trade practice, and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Allegations_of_intellectual_property_infringement_by_China" title="Allegations of intellectual property infringement by China" id="mwBnU">intellectual property infringement</a>.<sup about="#mwt939" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-256" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-256"}}'><a href="#cite_note-256" id="mwBnY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBnc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBng">[</span>254<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBnk">]</span></span></a></sup> Trump weakened the toughest U.S. sanctions imposed after the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2014_Russian_annexation_of_Crimea" title="2014 Russian annexation of Crimea" id="mwBno">2014 Russian annexation of Crimea</a>.<sup about="#mwt942" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-257" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-257"}}'><a href="#cite_note-257" id="mwBns"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBnw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBn0">[</span>255<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBn4">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt945" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-258" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-258"}}'><a href="#cite_note-258" id="mwBn8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBoA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBoE">[</span>256<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBoI">]</span></span></a></sup> Trump praised and, according to some critics, rarely criticized Russian president <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vladimir_Putin" title="Vladimir Putin" id="mwBoM">Vladimir Putin</a>,<sup about="#mwt948" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-259" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-259"}}'><a href="#cite_note-259" id="mwBoQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBoU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBoY">[</span>257<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBoc">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt951" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-260" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-260"}}'><a href="#cite_note-260" id="mwBog"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBok"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBoo">[</span>258<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBos">]</span></span></a></sup> though he opposed some actions of Russia's government.<sup about="#mwt954" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-261" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-261"}}'><a href="#cite_note-261" id="mwBow"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBo0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBo4">[</span>259<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBo8">]</span></span></a></sup> He withdrew the U.S. from the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Intermediate-Range_Nuclear_Forces_Treaty" title="Intermediate-Range Nuclear Forces Treaty" id="mwBpA">Intermediate-Range Nuclear Forces Treaty</a>, citing alleged Russian noncompliance,<sup about="#mwt957" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-262" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-262"}}'><a href="#cite_note-262" id="mwBpE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBpI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBpM">[</span>260<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBpQ">]</span></span></a></sup> and supported Russia's possible return to the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/G7" title="G7" id="mwBpU">G7</a>.<sup about="#mwt960" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-G8_263-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"G8"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-G8-263"}}'><a href="#cite_note-G8-263" id="mwBpY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBpc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBpg">[</span>261<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBpk">]</span></span></a></sup></p> 

<p id="mwBpo">Trump became the first sitting U.S. president to meet a North Korean leader, meeting <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kim_Jong_Un" title="Kim Jong Un" id="mwBps">Kim Jong Un</a> three times between June 2018 and June 2019 to discuss North Korean <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Denuclearization" title="Denuclearization" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBpw">denuclearization</a>. The talks broke down in October 2019, and no agreement was reached.<sup about="#mwt963" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-264" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-264"}}'><a href="#cite_note-264" id="mwBp0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBp4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBp8">[</span>262<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBqA">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="21" id="mwBqE" aria-labelledby="Personnel"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Personnel">Personnel</h3></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt964" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwBqI" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Political appointments of the first Trump administration"},"2":{"wt":"First cabinet of Donald Trump"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt965" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt964" id="mwBqM">Main articles: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Political_appointments_of_the_first_Trump_administration" title="Political appointments of the first Trump administration">Political appointments of the first Trump administration</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_cabinet_of_Donald_Trump" title="First cabinet of Donald Trump">First cabinet of Donald Trump</a></div>

<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt966" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwBqQ" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"further","href":"./Template:Further"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Government hiring and personnel of Donald Trump"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt967" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt966" id="mwBqU">Further information: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Government_hiring_and_personnel_of_Donald_Trump" title="Government hiring and personnel of Donald Trump">Government hiring and personnel of Donald Trump</a></div>
<p id="mwBqY">By the end of Trump's first year in office, 34<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwBqc"> </span>percent of his original staff had resigned, been fired, or been reassigned.<sup about="#mwt970" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-265" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-265"}}'><a href="#cite_note-265" id="mwBqg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBqk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBqo">[</span>263<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBqs">]</span></span></a></sup> By July 2018, 61<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwBqw"> </span>percent of his senior aides had left<sup about="#mwt973" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-266" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-266"}}'><a href="#cite_note-266" id="mwBq0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBq4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBq8">[</span>264<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBrA">]</span></span></a></sup> and 141 staffers had left in the previous year.<sup about="#mwt976" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-267" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-267"}}'><a href="#cite_note-267" id="mwBrE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBrI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBrM">[</span>265<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBrQ">]</span></span></a></sup> Both figures set a record for recent presidents.<sup about="#mwt979" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Keith_268-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Keith"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Keith-268"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Keith-268" id="mwBrU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBrY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBrc">[</span>266<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBrg">]</span></span></a></sup> Various close personal aides to Trump stopped working for him or were forced to leave.<sup about="#mwt982" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Brookings_269-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Brookings"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Brookings-269"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Brookings-269" id="mwBrk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBro"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBrs">[</span>267<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBrw">]</span></span></a></sup> Trump publicly disparaged several of his former top officials.<sup about="#mwt985" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-270" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-270"}}'><a href="#cite_note-270" id="mwBr0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBr4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBr8">[</span>268<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBsA">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwBsE">Trump had four <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_House_chiefs_of_staff" title="White House chiefs of staff" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBsI">White House chiefs of staff</a>, marginalizing or pushing out several.<sup about="#mwt988" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Keither_271-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Keither"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Keither-271"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Keither-271" id="mwBsM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBsQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBsU">[</span>269<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBsY">]</span></span></a></sup> In May 2017, he <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dismissal_of_James_Comey" title="Dismissal of James Comey" id="mwBsc">dismissed Federal Bureau of Investigation (FBI) director James Comey</a>, saying a few days later that he was concerned about Comey's role in the Trump<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwBsg">–</span>Russia investigations.<sup about="#mwt991" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-272" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-272"}}'><a href="#cite_note-272" id="mwBsk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBso"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBss">[</span>270<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBsw">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt994" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-273" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-273"}}'><a href="#cite_note-273" id="mwBs0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBs4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBs8">[</span>271<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBtA">]</span></span></a></sup> Three of Trump's 15 original cabinet members left or were forced to resign within his first year.<sup about="#mwt997" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-538_Cabinet_274-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"538 Cabinet"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-538_Cabinet-274"}}'><a href="#cite_note-538_Cabinet-274" id="mwBtE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBtI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBtM">[</span>272<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBtQ">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt998" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Brookings_269-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Brookings"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Brookings-269" id="mwBtU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBtY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBtc">[</span>267<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBtg">]</span></span></a></sup> Trump was slow to appoint second-tier officials in the executive branch, saying many of the positions are unnecessary. In October 2017, there were hundreds of sub-cabinet positions without a nominee.<sup about="#mwt1001" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-275" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-275"}}'><a href="#cite_note-275" id="mwBtk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBto"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBts">[</span>273<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBtw">]</span></span></a></sup> By January<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwBt0"> </span>8, 2019, of 706 key positions, 433 had been filled, and he had no nominee for 264.<sup about="#mwt1004" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-276" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-276"}}'><a href="#cite_note-276" id="mwBt4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBt8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBuA">[</span>274<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBuE">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="22" id="mwBuI" aria-labelledby="Judiciary"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Judiciary">Judiciary</h3></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt1005" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwBuM" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"further","href":"./Template:Further"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"List of federal judges appointed by Donald Trump"},"2":{"wt":"Donald Trump judicial appointment controversies"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt1006" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt1005" id="mwBuQ">Further information: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_federal_judges_appointed_by_Donald_Trump" title="List of federal judges appointed by Donald Trump">List of federal judges appointed by Donald Trump</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump_judicial_appointment_controversies" title="Donald Trump judicial appointment controversies">Donald Trump judicial appointment controversies</a></div>
<p id="mwBuU">Trump appointed 234 <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_federal_judge" title="United States federal judge" id="mwBuY">federal judges</a>, including 54 to the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_courts_of_appeals" title="United States courts of appeals" id="mwBuc">courts of appeals</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump_Supreme_Court_candidates" title="Donald Trump Supreme Court candidates" id="mwBug">three</a> to the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Supreme_Court_of_the_United_States" title="Supreme Court of the United States" id="mwBuk">Supreme Court</a>: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neil_Gorsuch" title="Neil Gorsuch" id="mwBuo">Neil Gorsuch</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brett_Kavanaugh" title="Brett Kavanaugh" id="mwBus">Brett Kavanaugh</a>, and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amy_Coney_Barrett" title="Amy Coney Barrett" id="mwBuw">Amy Coney Barrett</a>,<sup about="#mwt1009" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-277" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-277"}}'><a href="#cite_note-277" id="mwBu0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBu4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBu8">[</span>275<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBvA">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1012" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-278" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-278"}}'><a href="#cite_note-278" id="mwBvE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBvI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBvM">[</span>276<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBvQ">]</span></span></a></sup> which politically shifted the Court to the right.<sup about="#mwt1015" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-279" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-279"}}'><a href="#cite_note-279" id="mwBvU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBvY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBvc">[</span>277<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBvg">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1018" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-280" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-280"}}'><a href="#cite_note-280" id="mwBvk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBvo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBvs">[</span>278<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBvw">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1021" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-281" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-281"}}'><a href="#cite_note-281" id="mwBv0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBv4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBv8">[</span>279<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBwA">]</span></span></a></sup> In the 2016 campaign, he pledged that <i id="mwBwE"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roe_v._Wade" title="Roe v. Wade" id="mwBwI">Roe v. Wade</a></i> would be overturned "automatically" if he were elected and given the opportunity to appoint two or three anti-abortion justices. He later took credit when <i id="mwBwM">Roe</i> was overturned by <i id="mwBwQ"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dobbs_v._Jackson_Women's_Health_Organization" title="Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization" id="mwBwU">Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization</a></i> in 2022; all three of his Supreme Court nominees voted with the majority.<sup about="#mwt1024" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-282" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-282"}}'><a href="#cite_note-282" id="mwBwY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBwc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBwg">[</span>280<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBwk">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1027" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-283" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-283"}}'><a href="#cite_note-283" id="mwBwo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBws"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBww">[</span>281<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBw0">]</span></span></a></sup> Trump disparaged courts and judges he disagreed with, often in personal terms, and questioned the judiciary's constitutional authority. His attacks on courts drew rebukes from observers, including sitting federal judges, concerned about the effect of his statements on the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Judicial_independence" title="Judicial independence" id="mwBw4">judicial independence</a> and public confidence in the judiciary.<sup about="#mwt1030" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-284" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-284"}}'><a href="#cite_note-284" id="mwBw8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBxA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBxE">[</span>282<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBxI">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1033" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-285" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-285"}}'><a href="#cite_note-285" id="mwBxM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBxQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBxU">[</span>283<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBxY">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="23" id="mwBxc" aria-labelledby="COVID-19_pandemic"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="COVID-19_pandemic">COVID-19 pandemic</h3></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt1034" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwBxg" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"COVID-19 pandemic in the United States"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt1035" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt1034" id="mwBxk">Main article: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/COVID-19_pandemic_in_the_United_States" title="COVID-19 pandemic in the United States">COVID-19 pandemic in the United States</a></div>

<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt1036" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwBxo" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"further","href":"./Template:Further"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"U.S. federal government response to the COVID-19 pandemic"},"2":{"wt":"Communication of the Trump administration during the COVID-19 pandemic"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt1037" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt1036" id="mwBxs">Further information: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/U.S._federal_government_response_to_the_COVID-19_pandemic" title="U.S. federal government response to the COVID-19 pandemic">U.S. federal government response to the COVID-19 pandemic</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Communication_of_the_Trump_administration_during_the_COVID-19_pandemic" title="Communication of the Trump administration during the COVID-19 pandemic">Communication of the Trump administration during the COVID-19 pandemic</a></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt1038" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwBxw" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"see also","href":"./Template:See_also"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Economic impact of the COVID-19 pandemic in the United States"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt1039" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt1038" id="mwBx0">See also: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Economic_impact_of_the_COVID-19_pandemic_in_the_United_States" title="Economic impact of the COVID-19 pandemic in the United States">Economic impact of the COVID-19 pandemic in the United States</a></div>
<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwBx4"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:White_House_Press_Briefing_(49666120807).jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwBx8"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:White_House_Press_Briefing_(49666120807).jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/White_House_Press_Briefing_%2849666120807%29.jpg/250px-White_House_Press_Briefing_%2849666120807%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/White_House_Press_Briefing_%2849666120807%29.jpg/500px-White_House_Press_Briefing_%2849666120807%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" alt="Trump speaks in the West Wing briefing room with various officials standing behind him, all in formal attire and without face masks" data-file-width="2500" data-file-height="1667" data-file-type="bitmap" height="167" width="250" class="mw-file-element" id="mwByA"/></a><figcaption id="mwByE">Conducting a COVID-19 press briefing with members of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_House_Coronavirus_Task_Force" title="White House Coronavirus Task Force" id="mwByI">White House Coronavirus Task Force</a> on March<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwByM"> </span>15, 2020</figcaption></figure>
<p id="mwByQ">After ignoring public health warnings and calls for action from health officials within his administration for weeks,<sup about="#mwt1042" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-286" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-286"}}'><a href="#cite_note-286" id="mwByU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwByY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwByc">[</span>284<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwByg">]</span></span></a></sup> Trump established the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_House_Coronavirus_Task_Force" title="White House Coronavirus Task Force" id="mwByk">White House Coronavirus Task Force</a> in late January.<sup about="#mwt1045" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-287" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-287"}}'><a href="#cite_note-287" id="mwByo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBys"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwByw">[</span>285<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBy0">]</span></span></a></sup> In March, he signed into law the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CARES_Act" title="CARES Act" id="mwBy4">CARES Act</a><span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwBy8">—</span>a $2.2<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwBzA"> </span>trillion bipartisan <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Economic_stimulus" title="Economic stimulus" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBzE">economic stimulus</a> bill, the largest stimulus in U.S. history.<sup about="#mwt1048" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-NY_Times_stimulus_united_senate_288-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"NY Times stimulus united senate"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-NY_Times_stimulus_united_senate-288"}}'><a href="#cite_note-NY_Times_stimulus_united_senate-288" id="mwBzI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBzM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBzQ">[</span>286<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBzU">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1051" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-AP_Trump_signs_2.2_289-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"AP Trump signs 2.2"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-AP_Trump_signs_2.2-289"}}'><a href="#cite_note-AP_Trump_signs_2.2-289" id="mwBzY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBzc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBzg">[</span>287<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBzk">]</span></span></a></sup> After weeks of attacks to draw attention away from his slow response, Trump halted funding of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World_Health_Organization" title="World Health Organization" id="mwBzo">World Health Organization</a> in April.<sup about="#mwt1054" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-290" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-290"}}'><a href="#cite_note-290" id="mwBzs"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBzw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBz0">[</span>288<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBz4">]</span></span></a></sup> He encouraged right-wing <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/COVID-19_protests_in_the_United_States" title="COVID-19 protests in the United States" id="mwBz8">protests against social-distancing policies</a> in states with stay-at-home orders<sup about="#mwt1057" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-291" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-291"}}'><a href="#cite_note-291" id="mwB0A"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB0E"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB0I">[</span>289<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB0M">]</span></span></a></sup> and repeatedly pressured federal health agencies to take actions he favored,<sup about="#mwt1060" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-CNN-testing-pressure_292-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"CNN-testing-pressure"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-CNN-testing-pressure-292"}}'><a href="#cite_note-CNN-testing-pressure-292" id="mwB0Q"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB0U"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB0Y">[</span>290<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB0c">]</span></span></a></sup> such as approving unproven treatments.<sup about="#mwt1063" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-293" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-293"}}'><a href="#cite_note-293" id="mwB0g"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB0k"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB0o">[</span>291<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB0s">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1066" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-pressed_294-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"pressed"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-pressed-294"}}'><a href="#cite_note-pressed-294" id="mwB0w"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB00"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB04">[</span>292<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB08">]</span></span></a></sup></p> 

<p id="mwB1A">In October, Trump was <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_House_COVID-19_outbreak#Presidential_hospitalization_and_early_release" title="White House COVID-19 outbreak" id="mwB1E">hospitalized for three days</a> with a severe case of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/COVID-19" title="COVID-19" id="mwB1I">COVID-19</a> while his medical team downplayed the severity of his condition.<sup about="#mwt1069" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-295" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-295"}}'><a href="#cite_note-295" id="mwB1M"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB1Q"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB1U">[</span>293<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB1Y">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="24" id="mwB1c" aria-labelledby="Investigations"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Investigations">Investigations</h3></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt1070" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwB1g" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"further","href":"./Template:Further"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Russian interference in the 2016 United States elections"},"2":{"wt":"Mueller special counsel investigation"},"3":{"wt":"Mueller report"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt1071" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt1070" id="mwB1k">Further information: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russian_interference_in_the_2016_United_States_elections" title="Russian interference in the 2016 United States elections">Russian interference in the 2016 United States elections</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mueller_special_counsel_investigation" title="Mueller special counsel investigation">Mueller special counsel investigation</a>, and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mueller_report" title="Mueller report">Mueller report</a></div>
<p id="mwB1o">After he assumed office, Trump was the subject of increasing <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Department_of_Justice" title="United States Department of Justice" id="mwB1s">Justice Department</a> and congressional scrutiny, with investigations covering his election campaign, transition, and inauguration, actions taken during his presidency, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Trump_Organization" title="The Trump Organization" id="mwB1w">his private businesses</a>, personal taxes, and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_J._Trump_Foundation" title="Donald J. Trump Foundation" id="mwB10">his charitable foundation</a>.<sup about="#mwt1074" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-296" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-296"}}'><a href="#cite_note-296" id="mwB14"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB18"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB2A">[</span>294<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB2E">]</span></span></a></sup> There were ten federal criminal investigations, eight state and local investigations, and twelve congressional investigations.<sup about="#mwt1077" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-297" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-297"}}'><a href="#cite_note-297" id="mwB2I"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB2M"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB2Q">[</span>295<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB2U">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwB2Y">In July 2016, the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/FBI" title="FBI" class="mw-redirect" id="mwB2c">FBI</a> launched <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crossfire_Hurricane_(FBI_investigation)" title="Crossfire Hurricane (FBI investigation)" id="mwB2g">Crossfire Hurricane</a>, an investigation into possible links between Russia and Trump's 2016 campaign.<sup about="#mwt1080" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-298" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-298"}}'><a href="#cite_note-298" id="mwB2k"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB2o"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB2s">[</span>296<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB2w">]</span></span></a></sup> After Trump fired Comey in May 2017, the FBI opened a second investigation into Trump's personal and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Business_projects_of_Donald_Trump_in_Russia" title="Business projects of Donald Trump in Russia" id="mwB20">business dealings with Russia</a>.<sup about="#mwt1083" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-299" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-299"}}'><a href="#cite_note-299" id="mwB24"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB28"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB3A">[</span>297<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB3E">]</span></span></a></sup> In January 2017, three U.S. intelligence agencies jointly stated with "high confidence" that <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russian_interference_in_the_2016_United_States_elections" title="Russian interference in the 2016 United States elections" id="mwB3I">Russia interfered in the 2016 presidential election</a> to favor Trump.<sup about="#mwt1086" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-300" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-300"}}'><a href="#cite_note-300" id="mwB3M"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB3Q"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB3U">[</span>298<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB3Y">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1089" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-301" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-301"}}'><a href="#cite_note-301" id="mwB3c"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB3g"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB3k">[</span>299<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB3o">]</span></span></a></sup> Crossfire Hurricane was later transferred to <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_Mueller" title="Robert Mueller" id="mwB3s">Robert Mueller</a>'s <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mueller_special_counsel_investigation" title="Mueller special counsel investigation" id="mwB3w">special counsel investigation</a>;<sup about="#mwt1092" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-302" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-302"}}'><a href="#cite_note-302" id="mwB30"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB34"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB38">[</span>300<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB4A">]</span></span></a></sup> the investigation into Trump's ties to Russia was ended by Deputy Attorney General <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rod_Rosenstein" title="Rod Rosenstein" id="mwB4E">Rod Rosenstein</a> after he told the FBI that Mueller would pursue the matter.<sup about="#mwt1095" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-never_303-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"never"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-never-303"}}'><a href="#cite_note-never-303" id="mwB4I"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB4M"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB4Q">[</span>301<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB4U">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1098" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-304" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-304"}}'><a href="#cite_note-304" id="mwB4Y"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB4c"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB4g">[</span>302<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB4k">]</span></span></a></sup> At the request of Rosenstein, the Mueller investigation examined criminal matters "in connection with Russia's 2016 election interference".<sup about="#mwt1099" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-never_303-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"never"}}'><a href="#cite_note-never-303" id="mwB4o"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB4s"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB4w">[</span>301<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB40">]</span></span></a></sup> <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mueller_report" title="Mueller report" id="mwB44">Mueller's final report</a> in March 2019<sup about="#mwt1102" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-305" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-305"}}'><a href="#cite_note-305" id="mwB48"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB5A"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB5E">[</span>303<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB5I">]</span></span></a></sup> found that Russia interfered in 2016 to favor Trump<sup about="#mwt1105" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-306" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-306"}}'><a href="#cite_note-306" id="mwB5M"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB5Q"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB5U">[</span>304<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB5Y">]</span></span></a></sup> and that Trump and his campaign welcomed and encouraged the effort,<sup about="#mwt1108" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-307" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-307"}}'><a href="#cite_note-307" id="mwB5c"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB5g"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB5k">[</span>305<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB5o">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1111" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-308" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-308"}}'><a href="#cite_note-308" id="mwB5s"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB5w"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB50">[</span>306<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB54">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1114" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-309" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-309"}}'><a href="#cite_note-309" id="mwB58"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB6A"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB6E">[</span>307<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB6I">]</span></span></a></sup> but that the evidence "did not establish" that Trump campaign members conspired or coordinated with Russia.<sup about="#mwt1117" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-310" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-310"}}'><a href="#cite_note-310" id="mwB6M"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB6Q"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB6U">[</span>308<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB6Y">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1120" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-takeaways_311-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"takeaways"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-takeaways-311"}}'><a href="#cite_note-takeaways-311" id="mwB6c"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB6g"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB6k">[</span>309<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB6o">]</span></span></a></sup> Trump falsely claimed the report exonerated him despite Mueller writing that it did not.<sup about="#mwt1123" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-312" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-312"}}'><a href="#cite_note-312" id="mwB6s"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB6w"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB60">[</span>310<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB64">]</span></span></a></sup> The report also detailed potential obstruction of justice by Trump but "did not draw ultimate conclusions"<sup about="#mwt1126" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-313" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-313"}}'><a href="#cite_note-313" id="mwB68"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB7A"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB7E">[</span>311<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB7I">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1129" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-314" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-314"}}'><a href="#cite_note-314" id="mwB7M"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB7Q"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB7U">[</span>312<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB7Y">]</span></span></a></sup> and left the decision to charge the laws to Congress.<sup about="#mwt1132" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-LM_315-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"LM"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-LM-315"}}'><a href="#cite_note-LM-315" id="mwB7c"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB7g"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB7k">[</span>313<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB7o">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="25" id="mwB7s" aria-labelledby="Impeachments"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Impeachments">Impeachments</h3></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt1133" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwB7w" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"First impeachment of Donald Trump"},"2":{"wt":"Second impeachment of Donald Trump"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt1134" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt1133" id="mwB70">Main articles: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_impeachment_of_Donald_Trump" title="First impeachment of Donald Trump">First impeachment of Donald Trump</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second_impeachment_of_Donald_Trump" title="Second impeachment of Donald Trump">Second impeachment of Donald Trump</a></div>

<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwB74"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:President_Trump_Delivers_Remarks_(49498772251).jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwB78"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:President_Trump_Delivers_Remarks_(49498772251).jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/President_Trump_Delivers_Remarks_%2849498772251%29.jpg/250px-President_Trump_Delivers_Remarks_%2849498772251%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/President_Trump_Delivers_Remarks_%2849498772251%29.jpg/500px-President_Trump_Delivers_Remarks_%2849498772251%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="3000" data-file-height="2000" data-file-type="bitmap" height="167" width="250" class="mw-file-element" id="mwB8A"/></a><figcaption id="mwB8E">Displaying the headline "Trump acquitted" in 2020</figcaption></figure>
<p id="mwB8I">Trump was <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Impeachment_in_the_United_States" title="Impeachment in the United States" id="mwB8M">impeached</a> twice by the House of Representatives, though acquitted by the Senate on both occasions. The <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_impeachment_of_Donald_Trump" title="First impeachment of Donald Trump" id="mwB8Q">first impeachment</a> arose from a whistleblower complaint that in July 2019 Trump <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2019_Trump–Ukraine_scandal" title="2019 Trump–Ukraine scandal" id="mwB8U">had pressured</a> Ukrainian president <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Volodymyr_Zelenskyy" title="Volodymyr Zelenskyy" id="mwB8Y">Volodymyr Zelenskyy</a> to investigate Joe and Hunter Biden,<sup about="#mwt1137" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-undermine_316-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"undermine"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-undermine-316"}}'><a href="#cite_note-undermine-316" id="mwB8c"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB8g"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB8k">[</span>314<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB8o">]</span></span></a></sup> in an attempt to gain an advantage in the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2020_United_States_presidential_election" title="2020 United States presidential election" id="mwB8s">2020 presidential election</a>.<sup about="#mwt1140" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-317" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-317"}}'><a href="#cite_note-317" id="mwB8w"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB80"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB84">[</span>315<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB88">]</span></span></a></sup> In December 2019, the House voted to impeach Trump for <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abuse_of_power" title="Abuse of power" id="mwB9A">abuse of power</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Obstruction_of_Congress" title="Obstruction of Congress" class="mw-redirect" id="mwB9E">obstruction of Congress</a>,<sup about="#mwt1143" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-318" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-318"}}'><a href="#cite_note-318" id="mwB9I"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB9M"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB9Q">[</span>316<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB9U">]</span></span></a></sup> and the Senate acquitted him in February 2020.<sup about="#mwt1146" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-319" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-319"}}'><a href="#cite_note-319" id="mwB9Y"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB9c"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB9g">[</span>317<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB9k">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwB9o">The <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second_impeachment_of_Donald_Trump" title="Second impeachment of Donald Trump" id="mwB9s">second impeachment</a> came after the January<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwB9w"> </span>6 Capitol attack, for which the House charged Trump with <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Incitement_of_insurrection" title="Incitement of insurrection" class="mw-redirect" id="mwB90">incitement of insurrection</a> on January<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwB94"> </span>13, 2021.<sup about="#mwt1149" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-SecondImpeachment_320-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"SecondImpeachment"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-SecondImpeachment-320"}}'><a href="#cite_note-SecondImpeachment-320" id="mwB98"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB-A"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB-E">[</span>318<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB-I">]</span></span></a></sup> Trump left office on January<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwB-M"> </span>20 and was acquitted on February<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwB-Q"> </span>13. Seven Republican senators voted for conviction.<sup about="#mwt1152" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-321" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-321"}}'><a href="#cite_note-321" id="mwB-U"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB-Y"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB-c">[</span>319<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB-g">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="26" id="mwB-k" aria-labelledby="2020_presidential_election_and_rejection_of_outcome"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="2020_presidential_election_and_rejection_of_outcome">2020 presidential election and rejection of outcome</h3></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt1153" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwB-o" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"further","href":"./Template:Further"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"2020 United States presidential election"},"2":{"wt":"Donald Trump 2020 presidential campaign"},"3":{"wt":"Attempts to overturn the 2020 United States presidential election"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt1154" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt1153" id="mwB-s">Further information: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2020_United_States_presidential_election" title="2020 United States presidential election">2020 United States presidential election</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump_2020_presidential_campaign" title="Donald Trump 2020 presidential campaign">Donald Trump 2020 presidential campaign</a>, and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Attempts_to_overturn_the_2020_United_States_presidential_election" title="Attempts to overturn the 2020 United States presidential election">Attempts to overturn the 2020 United States presidential election</a></div>
<p id="mwB-w">Trump filed to run for reelection only a few hours after becoming president in 2017.<sup about="#mwt1157" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-322" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-322"}}'><a href="#cite_note-322" id="mwB-0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB-4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB-8">[</span>320<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB_A">]</span></span></a></sup> He held his first reelection rally less than a month after taking office<sup about="#mwt1160" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-323" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-323"}}'><a href="#cite_note-323" id="mwB_E"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB_I"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB_M">[</span>321<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB_Q">]</span></span></a></sup> and officially became the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2020_Republican_Party_presidential_primaries" title="2020 Republican Party presidential primaries" id="mwB_U">Republican nominee</a> in August 2020.<sup about="#mwt1163" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-324" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-324"}}'><a href="#cite_note-324" id="mwB_Y"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB_c"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB_g">[</span>322<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB_k">]</span></span></a></sup> Trump's campaign focused on crime, claiming that cities would descend into lawlessness if Democratic nominee <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joe_Biden" title="Joe Biden" id="mwB_o">Joe Biden</a> won.<sup about="#mwt1166" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-325" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-325"}}'><a href="#cite_note-325" id="mwB_s"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB_w"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB_0">[</span>323<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB_4">]</span></span></a></sup> Starting in early 2020, Trump sowed doubts about the election, claiming without evidence that it would be rigged and that widespread use of mail balloting would produce massive election fraud.<sup about="#mwt1169" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-326" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-326"}}'><a href="#cite_note-326" id="mwB_8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCAA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCAE">[</span>324<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCAI">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1172" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-327" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-327"}}'><a href="#cite_note-327" id="mwCAM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCAQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCAU">[</span>325<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCAY">]</span></span></a></sup> He repeatedly refused to say whether he would accept the results if he lost and commit to a <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peaceful_transition_of_power" title="Peaceful transition of power" id="mwCAc">peaceful transition of power</a>.<sup about="#mwt1175" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-328" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-328"}}'><a href="#cite_note-328" id="mwCAg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCAk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCAo">[</span>326<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCAs">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1178" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-329" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-329"}}'><a href="#cite_note-329" id="mwCAw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCA0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCA4">[</span>327<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCA8">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwCBA">Joe Biden won the November 2020 election, receiving 81.3<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwCBE"> </span>million votes (51.3<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwCBI"> </span>percent) to Trump's 74.2<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwCBM"> </span>million (46.8<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwCBQ"> </span>percent)<sup about="#mwt1181" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-vote1_330-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"vote1"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-vote1-330"}}'><a href="#cite_note-vote1-330" id="mwCBU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCBY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCBc">[</span>328<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCBg">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1184" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-vote2_331-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"vote2"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-vote2-331"}}'><a href="#cite_note-vote2-331" id="mwCBk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCBo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCBs">[</span>329<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCBw">]</span></span></a></sup> and 306 electoral votes to Trump's 232,<sup about="#mwt1187" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-formalize_332-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"formalize"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-formalize-332"}}'><a href="#cite_note-formalize-332" id="mwCB0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCB4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCB8">[</span>330<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCCA">]</span></span></a></sup> with the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Electoral_College" title="United States Electoral College" id="mwCCE">Electoral College</a> formalizing Biden's victory on December<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwCCI"> </span>14.<sup about="#mwt1188" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-formalize_332-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"formalize"}}'><a href="#cite_note-formalize-332" id="mwCCM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCCQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCCU">[</span>330<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCCY">]</span></span></a></sup> Trump declared victory before the results were known on the morning after the election.<sup about="#mwt1191" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-333" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-333"}}'><a href="#cite_note-333" id="mwCCc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCCg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCCk">[</span>331<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCCo">]</span></span></a></sup> Days later, when Biden was projected the winner, Trump baselessly alleged election fraud.<sup about="#mwt1194" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-334" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-334"}}'><a href="#cite_note-334" id="mwCCs"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCCw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCC0">[</span>332<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCC4">]</span></span></a></sup> As part of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Attempts_to_overturn_the_2020_United_States_presidential_election" title="Attempts to overturn the 2020 United States presidential election" id="mwCC8">an effort to overturn the results</a>, Trump and his allies filed many <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Post-election_lawsuits_related_to_the_2020_U.S._presidential_election" title="Post-election lawsuits related to the 2020 U.S. presidential election" id="mwCDA">lawsuits challenging the results</a>, which were rejected by at least 86 judges in both state and federal courts for having no factual or legal basis.<sup about="#mwt1197" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-335" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-335"}}'><a href="#cite_note-335" id="mwCDE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCDI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCDM">[</span>333<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCDQ">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1200" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-336" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-336"}}'><a href="#cite_note-336" id="mwCDU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCDY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCDc">[</span>334<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCDg">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwCDk">Trump's allegations were also refuted by state election officials,<sup about="#mwt1203" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-337" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-337"}}'><a href="#cite_note-337" id="mwCDo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCDs"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCDw">[</span>335<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCD0">]</span></span></a></sup> and the Supreme Court declined to hear <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Texas_v._Pennsylvania" title="Texas v. Pennsylvania" id="mwCD4">a case asking it to overturn the results</a> in four states won by Biden.<sup about="#mwt1206" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-338" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-338"}}'><a href="#cite_note-338" id="mwCD8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCEA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCEE">[</span>336<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCEI">]</span></span></a></sup> Trump repeatedly sought help to overturn the results, personally pressuring Republican local and state office-holders,<sup about="#mwt1209" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-amyG_339-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"amyG"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-amyG-339"}}'><a href="#cite_note-amyG-339" id="mwCEM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCEQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCEU">[</span>337<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCEY">]</span></span></a></sup> Republican legislators,<sup about="#mwt1212" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-pressure_340-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"pressure"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-pressure-340"}}'><a href="#cite_note-pressure-340" id="mwCEc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCEg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCEk">[</span>338<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCEo">]</span></span></a></sup> the Justice Department,<sup about="#mwt1215" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-341" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-341"}}'><a href="#cite_note-341" id="mwCEs"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCEw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCE0">[</span>339<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCE4">]</span></span></a></sup> and Vice President Pence,<sup about="#mwt1218" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-342" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-342"}}'><a href="#cite_note-342" id="mwCE8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCFA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCFE">[</span>340<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCFI">]</span></span></a></sup> urging actions such as <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trump_fake_electors_plot" title="Trump fake electors plot" id="mwCFM">replacing presidential electors</a>,<sup about="#mwt1219" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-pressure_340-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"pressure"}}'><a href="#cite_note-pressure-340" id="mwCFQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCFU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCFY">[</span>338<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCFc">]</span></span></a></sup> or that Georgia officials <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trump–Raffensperger_phone_call" title="Trump–Raffensperger phone call" id="mwCFg">"find" votes and announce</a> a "recalculated" result.<sup about="#mwt1220" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-amyG_339-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"amyG"}}'><a href="#cite_note-amyG-339" id="mwCFk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCFo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCFs">[</span>337<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCFw">]</span></span></a></sup> Trump did not attend <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Biden's_inauguration" title="Biden's inauguration" class="mw-redirect" id="mwCF0">Biden's inauguration</a> on January<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwCF4"> </span>20.<sup about="#mwt1223" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-343" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-343"}}'><a href="#cite_note-343" id="mwCF8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCGA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCGE">[</span>341<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCGI">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="27" id="mwCGM" aria-labelledby="January_6_attack"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="January_6_attack">January 6 attack</h3></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt1224" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwCGQ" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"January 6 United States Capitol attack"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt1225" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt1224" id="mwCGU">Main article: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/January_6_United_States_Capitol_attack" title="January 6 United States Capitol attack">January 6 United States Capitol attack</a></div>
<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwCGY"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:2021_storming_of_the_United_States_Capitol_DSC09254-2_(50820534063)_(retouched).jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwCGc"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:2021_storming_of_the_United_States_Capitol_DSC09254-2_(50820534063)_(retouched).jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/2021_storming_of_the_United_States_Capitol_DSC09254-2_%2850820534063%29_%28retouched%29.jpg/250px-2021_storming_of_the_United_States_Capitol_DSC09254-2_%2850820534063%29_%28retouched%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/2021_storming_of_the_United_States_Capitol_DSC09254-2_%2850820534063%29_%28retouched%29.jpg/500px-2021_storming_of_the_United_States_Capitol_DSC09254-2_%2850820534063%29_%28retouched%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="4887" data-file-height="2998" data-file-type="bitmap" height="153" width="250" class="mw-file-element" id="mwCGg"/></a><figcaption id="mwCGk">A crowd of Trump supporters during the attack</figcaption></figure>
<p id="mwCGo">On January<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwCGs"> </span>6, 2021, while <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2021_United_States_Electoral_College_vote_count" title="2021 United States Electoral College vote count" id="mwCGw">Congress was certifying the presidential election results</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/January_6_Trump_rally" title="January 6 Trump rally" class="mw-redirect" id="mwCG0">Trump held a rally</a> at which he called for the election to be overturned and urged his supporters to "fight like hell" and "take back our country" by marching to the Capitol.<sup about="#mwt1237" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-344" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-344"}}'><a href="#cite_note-344" id="mwCG4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCG8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCHA">[</span>342<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCHE">]</span></span></a></sup> His supporters then formed a mob that broke into the building, disrupting certification and causing the evacuation of Congress.<sup about="#mwt1240" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-345" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-345"}}'><a href="#cite_note-345" id="mwCHI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCHM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCHQ">[</span>343<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCHU">]</span></span></a></sup> More than 140 police officers were injured, and five people died during or after the attack.<sup about="#mwt1243" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-346" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-346"}}'><a href="#cite_note-346" id="mwCHY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCHc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCHg">[</span>344<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCHk">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1246" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-347" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-347"}}'><a href="#cite_note-347" id="mwCHo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCHs"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCHw">[</span>345<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCH0">]</span></span></a></sup> The event has been described as an attempted <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Self-coup" title="Self-coup" id="mwCH4">self-coup</a> by Trump.<sup about="#mwt1226" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEPion-BerlinBruneauGoetze2022_348-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEPion-BerlinBruneauGoetze2022"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEPion-BerlinBruneauGoetze2022-348"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Pion-Berlin"},"2":{"wt":"Bruneau"},"3":{"wt":"Goetze"},"4":{"wt":"2022"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEPion-BerlinBruneauGoetze2022-348" id="mwCH8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCIA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCIE">[</span>346<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCII">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1229" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEHarvey20223_349-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEHarvey20223"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEHarvey20223-349"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Harvey"},"2":{"wt":"2022"},"p":{"wt":"3"},"3":{"wt":""}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEHarvey20223-349" id="mwCIM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCIQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCIU">[</span>347<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCIY">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1232" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTECastañedaJenks2023246_350-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTECastañedaJenks2023246"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTECastañedaJenks2023246-350"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Castañeda"},"2":{"wt":"Jenks"},"3":{"wt":"2023"},"p":{"wt":"246"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTECastañedaJenks2023246-350" id="mwCIc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCIg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCIk">[</span>348<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCIo">]</span></span></a></sup> Congress later reconvened and confirmed Biden's victory in the early hours of January<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwCIs"> </span>7.<sup about="#mwt1249" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-351" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-351"}}'><a href="#cite_note-351" id="mwCIw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCI0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCI4">[</span>349<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCI8">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section></section><section data-mw-section-id="28" id="mwCJA" aria-labelledby="Between_presidencies_(2021–2025)"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id="Between_presidencies_(2021–2025)"><span id="Between_presidencies_.282021.E2.80.932025.29" typeof="mw:FallbackId"></span>Between presidencies (2021–2025)</h2></div>
<p id="mwCJE">Upon leaving the White House, Trump began living at Mar-a-Lago, establishing an office there as provided for by the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Former_Presidents_Act" title="Former Presidents Act" id="mwCJI">Former Presidents Act</a>.<sup about="#mwt1252" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-352" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-352"}}'><a href="#cite_note-352" id="mwCJM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCJQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCJU">[</span>350<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCJY">]</span></span></a></sup> His continuing <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Big_lie#Donald_Trump's_false_claims_of_a_stolen_election" title="Big lie" id="mwCJc">false claims concerning the 2020 election</a> were commonly referred to as the "<a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Big_lie" title="Big lie" id="mwCJg">big lie</a>" by his critics, although in May 2021, he and many of his supporters began using the term to refer to the election itself.<sup about="#mwt1255" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-353" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-353"}}'><a href="#cite_note-353" id="mwCJk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCJo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCJs">[</span>351<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCJw">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1258" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-key2_354-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"key2"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-key2-354"}}'><a href="#cite_note-key2-354" id="mwCJ0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCJ4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCJ8">[</span>352<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCKA">]</span></span></a></sup> The Republican Party used his false claims about the election to justify <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Republican_efforts_to_restrict_voting_following_the_2020_United_States_presidential_election" title="Republican efforts to restrict voting following the 2020 United States presidential election" id="mwCKE">imposing new voting restrictions</a> in its favor.<sup about="#mwt1261" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-355" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-355"}}'><a href="#cite_note-355" id="mwCKI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCKM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCKQ">[</span>353<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCKU">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1264" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-356" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-356"}}'><a href="#cite_note-356" id="mwCKY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCKc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCKg">[</span>354<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCKk">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1267" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-357" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-357"}}'><a href="#cite_note-357" id="mwCKo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCKs"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCKw">[</span>355<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCK0">]</span></span></a></sup> He continued to pressure state legislators to overturn the election.<sup about="#mwt1270" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-358" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-358"}}'><a href="#cite_note-358" id="mwCK4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCK8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCLA">[</span>356<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCLE">]</span></span></a></sup> Unlike other former presidents, Trump continued to dominate his party; a 2022 profile in <i id="mwCLI">The New York Times</i> described him as a modern <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Party_boss" title="Party boss" class="mw-redirect" id="mwCLM">party boss</a>.<sup about="#mwt1273" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-PB2_359-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"PB2"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-PB2-359"}}'><a href="#cite_note-PB2-359" id="mwCLQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCLU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCLY">[</span>357<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCLc">]</span></span></a></sup> He continued fundraising, raising a <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/War_chest" title="War chest" id="mwCLg">war chest</a> containing more than twice that of the Republican Party, and profited from fundraisers many Republican candidates held at Mar-a-Lago. Much of his focus was on party governance and installing in key posts officials loyal to him.<sup about="#mwt1274" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-PB2_359-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"PB2"}}'><a href="#cite_note-PB2-359" id="mwCLk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCLo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCLs">[</span>357<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCLw">]</span></span></a></sup> In the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2022_midterm_elections" title="2022 midterm elections" class="mw-redirect" id="mwCL0">2022 midterm elections</a>, Trump endorsed over 200 candidates for various offices.<sup about="#mwt1277" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-360" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-360"}}'><a href="#cite_note-360" id="mwCL4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCL8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCMA">[</span>358<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCME">]</span></span></a></sup> In February 2022, his <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trump_Media_&amp;_Technology_Group" title="Trump Media &amp; Technology Group" id="mwCMI">Trump Media &amp; Technology Group</a> (TMTG) launched a pro-Trump social media platform called <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Truth_Social" title="Truth Social" id="mwCMM">Truth Social</a>.<sup about="#mwt1280" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-361" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-361"}}'><a href="#cite_note-361" id="mwCMQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCMU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCMY">[</span>359<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCMc">]</span></span></a></sup> In March 2024, TMTG became a public company.<sup about="#mwt1283" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-362" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-362"}}'><a href="#cite_note-362" id="mwCMg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCMk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCMo">[</span>360<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCMs">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<section data-mw-section-id="29" id="mwCMw" aria-labelledby="Legal_issues"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Legal_issues">Legal issues</h3></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt1284" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwCM0" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"see also","href":"./Template:See_also"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Personal and business legal affairs of Donald Trump"},"2":{"wt":"Legal affairs of the first Trump presidency"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt1285" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt1284" id="mwCM4">See also: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Personal_and_business_legal_affairs_of_Donald_Trump" title="Personal and business legal affairs of Donald Trump">Personal and business legal affairs of Donald Trump</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Legal_affairs_of_the_first_Trump_presidency" title="Legal affairs of the first Trump presidency">Legal affairs of the first Trump presidency</a></div>

<p id="mwCM8">In 2022, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_York_business_fraud_lawsuit_against_the_Trump_Organization" title="New York business fraud lawsuit against the Trump Organization" id="mwCNA">New York filed a civil lawsuit</a> against Trump accusing him of inflating the Trump Organization's value to gain an advantage with lenders and banks.<sup about="#mwt1288" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-363" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-363"}}'><a href="#cite_note-363" id="mwCNE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCNI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCNM">[</span>361<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCNQ">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1291" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-TF_364-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"TF"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-TF-364"}}'><a href="#cite_note-TF-364" id="mwCNU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCNY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCNc">[</span>362<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCNg">]</span></span></a></sup> He was found liable and ordered to pay nearly $355<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwCNk"> </span>million plus interest.<sup about="#mwt1292" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-TF_364-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"TF"}}'><a href="#cite_note-TF-364" id="mwCNo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCNs"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCNw">[</span>362<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCN0">]</span></span></a></sup> In August 2025, the appeals court upheld his liability and nonmonetary penalties but voided the monetary penalty as excessive.<sup about="#mwt1295" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-365" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-365"}}'><a href="#cite_note-365" id="mwCN4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCN8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCOA">[</span>363<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCOE">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwCOI">In connection with Trump's efforts to overturn the 2020 election and his involvement in the January<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwCOM"> </span>6 attack, in December 2022 the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_House_Select_Committee_on_the_January_6_Attack" title="United States House Select Committee on the January 6 Attack" class="mw-redirect" id="mwCOQ">U.S. House committee on the attack</a> recommended criminal charges against him for <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Obstructing_an_official_proceeding" title="Obstructing an official proceeding" id="mwCOU">obstructing an official proceeding</a>, inciting or assisting an insurrection, and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conspiracy_to_defraud_the_United_States" title="Conspiracy to defraud the United States" class="mw-redirect" id="mwCOY">conspiracy to defraud the United States</a>.<sup about="#mwt1298" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-366" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-366"}}'><a href="#cite_note-366" id="mwCOc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCOg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCOk">[</span>364<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCOo">]</span></span></a></sup> In August 2023, a grand jury in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fulton_County,_Georgia" title="Fulton County, Georgia" id="mwCOs">Fulton County, Georgia</a>, indicted him on <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Georgia_election_racketeering_prosecution" title="Georgia election racketeering prosecution" id="mwCOw">13 charges, including racketeering</a>, for his efforts to subvert the 2020 election in the state.<sup about="#mwt1301" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-367" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-367"}}'><a href="#cite_note-367" id="mwCO0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCO4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCO8">[</span>365<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCPA">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1304" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-368" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-368"}}'><a href="#cite_note-368" id="mwCPE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCPI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCPM">[</span>366<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCPQ">]</span></span></a></sup> The case was dismissed in November 2025 after the new prosecutor declined to pursue the charges.<sup about="#mwt1307" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-369" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-369"}}'><a href="#cite_note-369" id="mwCPU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCPY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCPc">[</span>367<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCPg">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwCPk"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Classified_intelligence_material_found_during_search_of_Mar-a-Lago.jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwCPo"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Classified_intelligence_material_found_during_search_of_Mar-a-Lago.jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Classified_intelligence_material_found_during_search_of_Mar-a-Lago.jpg/250px-Classified_intelligence_material_found_during_search_of_Mar-a-Lago.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Classified_intelligence_material_found_during_search_of_Mar-a-Lago.jpg/500px-Classified_intelligence_material_found_during_search_of_Mar-a-Lago.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="3717" data-file-height="2473" data-file-type="bitmap" height="166" width="250" class="mw-file-element" id="mwCPs"/></a><figcaption id="mwCPw">Classified intelligence material found during search of Mar-a-Lago</figcaption></figure>
<p id="mwCP0">In January 2022, the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Archives_and_Records_Administration" title="National Archives and Records Administration" id="mwCP4">National Archives and Records Administration</a> retrieved <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Federal_prosecution_of_Donald_Trump_(classified_documents_case)" title="Federal prosecution of Donald Trump (classified documents case)" id="mwCP8">15 boxes of documents</a> Trump had taken to <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mar-a-Lago" title="Mar-a-Lago" id="mwCQA">Mar-a-Lago</a> after leaving the White House, some of which were classified.<sup about="#mwt1310" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-cnn-tl222_370-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"cnn-tl222"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-cnn-tl222-370"}}'><a href="#cite_note-cnn-tl222-370" id="mwCQE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCQI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCQM">[</span>368<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCQQ">]</span></span></a></sup> In the ensuing Justice Department investigation, officials retrieved more classified documents from his lawyers.<sup about="#mwt1311" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-cnn-tl222_370-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"cnn-tl222"}}'><a href="#cite_note-cnn-tl222-370" id="mwCQU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCQY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCQc">[</span>368<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCQg">]</span></span></a></sup> On August<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwCQk"> </span>8, 2022, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/FBI_search_of_Mar-a-Lago" title="FBI search of Mar-a-Lago" id="mwCQo">FBI agents searched Mar-a-Lago</a> for illegally held documents, including those in breach of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Espionage_Act" title="Espionage Act" class="mw-redirect" id="mwCQs">Espionage Act</a>, collecting 11 sets of classified documents, some marked top secret.<sup about="#mwt1314" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-bddj0812222_371-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"bddj0812222"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-bddj0812222-371"}}'><a href="#cite_note-bddj0812222-371" id="mwCQw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCQ0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCQ4">[</span>369<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCQ8">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1317" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-NYT-20220812222_372-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"NYT-20220812222"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-NYT-20220812222-372"}}'><a href="#cite_note-NYT-20220812222-372" id="mwCRA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCRE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCRI">[</span>370<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCRM">]</span></span></a></sup> A <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Federal_grand_jury" title="Federal grand jury" class="mw-redirect" id="mwCRQ">federal grand jury</a> constituted by Special Counsel <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jack_Smith_(lawyer)" title="Jack Smith (lawyer)" id="mwCRU">Jack Smith</a> indicted Trump in June 2023 on 31 counts of "willfully retaining national defense information" under the Espionage Act, among other charges.<sup about="#mwt1318" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-cnn-tl222_370-2" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"cnn-tl222"}}'><a href="#cite_note-cnn-tl222-370" id="mwCRY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCRc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCRg">[</span>368<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCRk">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1321" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-373" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-373"}}'><a href="#cite_note-373" id="mwCRo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCRs"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCRw">[</span>371<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCR0">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1324" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-374" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-374"}}'><a href="#cite_note-374" id="mwCR4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCR8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCSA">[</span>372<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCSE">]</span></span></a></sup> Trump pleaded not guilty.<sup about="#mwt1327" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-375" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-375"}}'><a href="#cite_note-375" id="mwCSI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCSM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCSQ">[</span>373<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCSU">]</span></span></a></sup> In July 2024, judge <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aileen_Cannon" title="Aileen Cannon" id="mwCSY">Aileen Cannon</a> dismissed the case, ruling Smith's appointment as special prosecutor was unconstitutional.<sup about="#mwt1330" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-CD2_376-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"CD2"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-CD2-376"}}'><a href="#cite_note-CD2-376" id="mwCSc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCSg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCSk">[</span>374<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCSo">]</span></span></a></sup> After his reelection, the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Federal_prosecution_of_Donald_Trump_(election_obstruction_case)" title="Federal prosecution of Donald Trump (election obstruction case)" id="mwCSs">2020 election obstruction case</a> and the classified documents case were dismissed without <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prejudice_(legal_term)" title="Prejudice (legal term)" id="mwCSw">prejudice</a> (a legal term meaning charges could be refiled after the end of Trump's second term) due to Justice Department policy against prosecuting sitting presidents.<sup about="#mwt1333" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-377" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-377"}}'><a href="#cite_note-377" id="mwCS0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCS4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCS8">[</span>375<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCTA">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwCTE">In May 2024, Trump <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prosecution_of_Donald_Trump_in_New_York" title="Prosecution of Donald Trump in New York" id="mwCTI">was convicted on 34 felony counts</a> of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Falsifying_business_records" title="Falsifying business records" id="mwCTM">falsifying business records</a>.<sup about="#mwt1336" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-conv_378-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"conv"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-conv-378"}}'><a href="#cite_note-conv-378" id="mwCTQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCTU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCTY">[</span>376<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCTc">]</span></span></a></sup> The case stemmed from evidence that he booked <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Michael_Cohen_(lawyer)" title="Michael Cohen (lawyer)" id="mwCTg">Michael Cohen</a>'s hush-money payments to adult film actress <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stormy_Daniels" title="Stormy Daniels" id="mwCTk">Stormy Daniels</a> as business expenses to cover up his alleged 2006<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwCTo">–</span>2007 affair with Daniels during the 2016 election.<sup about="#mwt1337" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-conv_378-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"conv"}}'><a href="#cite_note-conv-378" id="mwCTs"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCTw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCT0">[</span>376<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCT4">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1340" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-379" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-379"}}'><a href="#cite_note-379" id="mwCT8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCUA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCUE">[</span>377<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCUI">]</span></span></a></sup> On January<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwCUM"> </span>10, 2025, Judge <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juan_Merchan" title="Juan Merchan" id="mwCUQ">Juan Merchan</a> gave Trump a <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Discharge_(sentence)" title="Discharge (sentence)" id="mwCUU">no-penalty sentence</a>, saying that punitive requirements would have interfered with presidential immunity.<sup about="#mwt1343" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-uds_380-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"uds"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-uds-380"}}'><a href="#cite_note-uds-380" id="mwCUY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCUc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCUg">[</span>378<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCUk">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="30" id="mwCUo" aria-labelledby="2024_presidential_election"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="2024_presidential_election">2024 presidential election</h3></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt1344" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwCUs" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"2024 United States presidential election"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt1345" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt1344" id="mwCUw">Main article: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2024_United_States_presidential_election" title="2024 United States presidential election">2024 United States presidential election</a></div>

<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt1346" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwCU0" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"further","href":"./Template:Further"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Donald Trump 2024 presidential campaign"},"2":{"wt":"2024 Republican Party presidential primaries"},"3":{"wt":"Second presidential transition of Donald Trump"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt1347" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt1346" id="mwCU4">Further information: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump_2024_presidential_campaign" title="Donald Trump 2024 presidential campaign">Donald Trump 2024 presidential campaign</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2024_Republican_Party_presidential_primaries" title="2024 Republican Party presidential primaries">2024 Republican Party presidential primaries</a>, and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second_presidential_transition_of_Donald_Trump" title="Second presidential transition of Donald Trump">Second presidential transition of Donald Trump</a></div>
<p id="mwCU8">In November 2022, Trump announced his candidacy for the 2024 presidential election and created a fundraising account.<sup about="#mwt1350" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-381" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-381"}}'><a href="#cite_note-381" id="mwCVA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCVE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCVI">[</span>379<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCVM">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1353" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-382" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-382"}}'><a href="#cite_note-382" id="mwCVQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCVU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCVY">[</span>380<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCVc">]</span></span></a></sup> In March 2023, the campaign began diverting ten<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwCVg"> </span>percent of the donations to his <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leadership_PAC" title="Leadership PAC" class="mw-redirect" id="mwCVk">leadership PAC</a>. His campaign had paid $100<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwCVo"> </span>million towards his legal bills by March 2024.<sup about="#mwt1356" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-383" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-383"}}'><a href="#cite_note-383" id="mwCVs"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCVw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCV0">[</span>381<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCV4">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1359" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-384" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-384"}}'><a href="#cite_note-384" id="mwCV8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCWA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCWE">[</span>382<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCWI">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwCWM">Trump's policy platform, named <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agenda_47" title="Agenda 47" id="mwCWQ">Agenda 47</a>, was vague and aimed at Republican primaries.<sup about="#mwt1362" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-385" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-385"}}'><a href="#cite_note-385" id="mwCWU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCWY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCWc">[</span>383<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCWg">]</span></span></a></sup> He occasionally disavowed knowledge of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Project_2025" title="Project 2025" id="mwCWk">Project 2025</a>, an outline for consolidating power in the executive branch,<sup about="#mwt1365" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-386" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-386"}}'><a href="#cite_note-386" id="mwCWo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCWs"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCWw">[</span>384<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCW0">]</span></span></a></sup> despite personnel overlap with his first administration.<sup about="#mwt1368" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-387" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-387"}}'><a href="#cite_note-387" id="mwCW4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCW8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCXA">[</span>385<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCXE">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1371" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-388" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-388"}}'><a href="#cite_note-388" id="mwCXI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCXM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCXQ">[</span>386<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCXU">]</span></span></a></sup> During the campaign, he made increasingly violent and authoritarian statements.<sup about="#mwt1378" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-389" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-389"}}'><a href="#cite_note-389" id="mwCXY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCXc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCXg">[</span>387<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCXk">]</span></span></a></sup>
He said that he would weaponize the FBI and the Justice Department against his political opponents<sup about="#mwt1381" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-390" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-390"}}'><a href="#cite_note-390" id="mwCXo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCXs"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCXw">[</span>388<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCX0">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1384" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-391" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-391"}}'><a href="#cite_note-391" id="mwCX4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCX8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCYA">[</span>389<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCYE">]</span></span></a></sup> and use the military to target Democratic politicians and those not supporting his candidacy.<sup about="#mwt1387" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-392" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-392"}}'><a href="#cite_note-392" id="mwCYI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCYM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCYQ">[</span>390<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCYU">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1390" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Lerer_10152024_393-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Lerer 10152024"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Lerer_10152024-393"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Lerer_10152024-393" id="mwCYY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCYc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCYg">[</span>391<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCYk">]</span></span></a></sup> He used harsher and more dehumanizing anti-immigrant rhetoric than during his presidency.<sup about="#mwt1397" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-394" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-394"}}'><a href="#cite_note-394" id="mwCYo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCYs"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCYw">[</span>392<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCY0">]</span></span></a></sup>
His rhetoric, calling his political opponents "the enemy", vermin, and fascists, has been described by some historians and scholars as authoritarian, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump_and_fascism" title="Donald Trump and fascism" id="mwCY4">fascist</a>,<sup about="#mwt1404" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-395" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-395"}}'><a href="#cite_note-395" id="mwCY8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCZA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCZE">[</span>393<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCZI">]</span></span></a></sup>
and unlike anything a political candidate has ever said in American history.<sup about="#mwt1407" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-NYMag_Never_Sounded_Like_This_396-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"NYMag Never Sounded Like This"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-NYMag_Never_Sounded_Like_This-396"}}'><a href="#cite_note-NYMag_Never_Sounded_Like_This-396" id="mwCZM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCZQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCZU">[</span>394<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCZY">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1408" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Lerer_10152024_393-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Lerer 10152024"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Lerer_10152024-393" id="mwCZc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCZg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCZk">[</span>391<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCZo">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1411" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Applebaum18_397-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Applebaum18"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Applebaum18-397"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Applebaum18-397" id="mwCZs"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCZw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCZ0">[</span>395<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCZ4">]</span></span></a></sup> <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Age_and_health_concerns_about_Donald_Trump" title="Age and health concerns about Donald Trump" id="mwCZ8">Age and health concerns</a> also arose during the campaign, with several medical experts highlighting an increase in rambling, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tangential_speech" title="Tangential speech" id="mwCaA">tangential speech</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Disinhibition" title="Disinhibition" id="mwCaE">behavioral disinhibition</a>.<sup about="#mwt1414" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-398" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-398"}}'><a href="#cite_note-398" id="mwCaI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCaM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCaQ">[</span>396<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCaU">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwCaY">Trump mentioned "rigged election" and "election interference" earlier and more frequently in the 2024 campaign than in the 2016 and 2020 campaigns.<sup about="#mwt1417" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-399" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-399"}}'><a href="#cite_note-399" id="mwCac"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCag"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCak">[</span>397<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCao">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1420" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-NYTDoubts_400-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"NYTDoubts"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-NYTDoubts-400"}}'><a href="#cite_note-NYTDoubts-400" id="mwCas"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCaw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCa0">[</span>398<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCa4">]</span></span></a></sup> On July<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwCa8"> </span>13, 2024, Trump was shot in the ear in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Attempted_assassination_of_Donald_Trump_in_Pennsylvania" title="Attempted assassination of Donald Trump in Pennsylvania" id="mwCbA">an assassination attempt</a> at a campaign rally in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Butler_Township,_Butler_County,_Pennsylvania" title="Butler Township, Butler County, Pennsylvania" id="mwCbE">Butler Township, Pennsylvania</a>.<sup about="#mwt1423" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Browne-2024_401-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Browne-2024"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Browne-2024-401"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Browne-2024-401" id="mwCbI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCbM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCbQ">[</span>399<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCbU">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1426" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-402" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-402"}}'><a href="#cite_note-402" id="mwCbY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCbc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCbg">[</span>400<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCbk">]</span></span></a></sup> On July 15, the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2024_Republican_National_Convention" title="2024 Republican National Convention" id="mwCbo">2024 Republican National Convention</a> nominated him as their presidential candidate.<sup about="#mwt1429" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-403" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-403"}}'><a href="#cite_note-403" id="mwCbs"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCbw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCb0">[</span>401<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCb4">]</span></span></a></sup> In September, he was targeted but unharmed in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Attempted_assassination_of_Donald_Trump_in_Florida" title="Attempted assassination of Donald Trump in Florida" id="mwCb8">another assassination attempt</a> in Florida.<sup about="#mwt1432" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-404" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-404"}}'><a href="#cite_note-404" id="mwCcA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCcE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCcI">[</span>402<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCcM">]</span></span></a></sup> Trump won the election in November 2024 with 312 electoral votes to incumbent vice president Kamala Harris's 226.<sup about="#mwt1435" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-405" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-405"}}'><a href="#cite_note-405" id="mwCcQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCcU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCcY">[</span>403<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCcc">]</span></span></a></sup> He also won the popular vote with 49.8% to Harris's 48.3%.<sup about="#mwt1438" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-406" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-406"}}'><a href="#cite_note-406" id="mwCcg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCck"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCco">[</span>404<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCcs">]</span></span></a></sup> His victory in 2024 was in large part due to the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2021–2023_inflation_surge" title="2021–2023 inflation surge" id="mwCcw">2021–2023 inflation surge</a>.<sup about="#mwt1441" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Global_Politics_407-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Global Politics"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Global_Politics-407"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Global_Politics-407" id="mwCc0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCc4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCc8">[</span>405<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCdA">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1444" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-408" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-408"}}'><a href="#cite_note-408" id="mwCdE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCdI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCdM">[</span>406<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCdQ">]</span></span></a></sup> Several outlets described his reelection as an extraordinary comeback.<sup about="#mwt1447" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-409" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-409"}}'><a href="#cite_note-409" id="mwCdU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCdY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCdc">[</span>407<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCdg">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1450" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-410" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-410"}}'><a href="#cite_note-410" id="mwCdk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCdo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCds">[</span>408<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCdw">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section></section><section data-mw-section-id="31" id="mwCd0" aria-labelledby="Second_presidency_(2025–present)"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id="Second_presidency_(2025–present)"><span id="Second_presidency_.282025.E2.80.93present.29" typeof="mw:FallbackId"></span>Second presidency (2025–present)</h2></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt1451" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwCd4" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Second presidency of Donald Trump"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt1452" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt1451" id="mwCd8">Main article: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second_presidency_of_Donald_Trump" title="Second presidency of Donald Trump">Second presidency of Donald Trump</a></div>

<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt1453" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwCeA" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"for timeline","href":"./Template:For_timeline"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Timeline of the Donald Trump presidencies#Second presidency (2025–present)"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt1454" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt1453" id="mwCeE">For a chronological guide, see <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_the_Donald_Trump_presidencies#Second_presidency_(2025–present)" title="Timeline of the Donald Trump presidencies">Timeline of the Donald Trump presidencies §<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>Second presidency (2025–present)</a>.</div>
<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwCeI"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Donald_Trump_takes_the_oath_of_office_(2025)_(alternate).jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwCeM"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Donald_Trump_takes_the_oath_of_office_(2025)_(alternate).jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/Donald_Trump_takes_the_oath_of_office_%282025%29_%28alternate%29.jpg/250px-Donald_Trump_takes_the_oath_of_office_%282025%29_%28alternate%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/Donald_Trump_takes_the_oath_of_office_%282025%29_%28alternate%29.jpg/500px-Donald_Trump_takes_the_oath_of_office_%282025%29_%28alternate%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="4200" data-file-height="2798" data-file-type="bitmap" height="167" width="250" class="mw-file-element" id="mwCeQ"/></a><figcaption id="mwCeU">Taking the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oath_of_office_of_the_President_of_the_United_States" title="Oath of office of the President of the United States" class="mw-redirect" id="mwCeY">presidential oath of office</a>, administered by Chief Justice <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Roberts" title="John Roberts" id="mwCec">John Roberts</a>, on January<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwCeg"> </span>20, 2025</figcaption></figure>
<p id="mwCek">Trump began his second term upon <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second_inauguration_of_Donald_Trump" title="Second inauguration of Donald Trump" id="mwCeo">his inauguration</a> on January<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwCes"> </span>20, 2025.<sup about="#mwt1457" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-411" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-411"}}'><a href="#cite_note-411" id="mwCew"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCe0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCe4">[</span>409<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCe8">]</span></span></a></sup> He became the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_presidents_of_the_United_States_by_age" title="List of presidents of the United States by age" id="mwCfA">oldest to assume the presidency</a>,<sup about="#mwt1460" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-412" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-412"}}'><a href="#cite_note-412" id="mwCfE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCfI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCfM">[</span>410<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCfQ">]</span></span></a></sup> the first president with a felony conviction, and the second to serve two nonconsecutive terms.<sup about="#mwt1463" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-413" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-413"}}'><a href="#cite_note-413" id="mwCfU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCfY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCfc">[</span>411<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCfg">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwCfk">Trump chose several Project 2025 authors for his second administration;<sup about="#mwt1466" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-414" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-414"}}'><a href="#cite_note-414" id="mwCfo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCfs"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCfw">[</span>412<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCf0">]</span></span></a></sup> after the first year of his second term about half of the project's agenda had been implemented.<sup about="#mwt1469" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-415" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-415"}}'><a href="#cite_note-415" id="mwCf4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCf8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCgA">[</span>413<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCgE">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1472" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-416" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-416"}}'><a href="#cite_note-416" id="mwCgI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCgM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCgQ">[</span>414<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCgU">]</span></span></a></sup> Emphasizing his conflicts with judicial authority, <i id="mwCgY">The New York Times</i> said his attempts to expand presidential power are a defining characteristic of his second term.<sup about="#mwt1475" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-417" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-417"}}'><a href="#cite_note-417" id="mwCgc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCgg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCgk">[</span>415<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCgo">]</span></span></a></sup> Trump's second term saw frequent policy announcements, delays, and reversals leading to the emergence of the term "TACO" (<a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trump_Always_Chickens_Out" title="Trump Always Chickens Out" id="mwCgs">Trump Always Chickens Out</a>) to describe it.<sup about="#mwt1478" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-418" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-418"}}'><a href="#cite_note-418" id="mwCgw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCg0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCg4">[</span>416<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCg8">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1481" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-419" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-419"}}'><a href="#cite_note-419" id="mwChA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwChE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwChI">[</span>417<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwChM">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<section data-mw-section-id="32" id="mwChQ" aria-labelledby="Early_actions"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Early_actions">Early actions</h3></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt1482" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwChU" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"see also","href":"./Template:See_also"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"First 100 days of the second Trump presidency"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt1483" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt1482" id="mwChY">See also: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_100_days_of_the_second_Trump_presidency" title="First 100 days of the second Trump presidency">First 100 days of the second Trump presidency</a></div>
<p id="mwChc">More than a month before the 100-day mark of Trump's second term, he had <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_executive_orders_in_the_second_Trump_presidency" title="List of executive orders in the second Trump presidency" id="mwChg">issued more executive orders</a> than any other U.S. president's first 100 days.<sup about="#mwt1486" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-420" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-420"}}'><a href="#cite_note-420" id="mwChk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCho"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwChs">[</span>418<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwChw">]</span></span></a></sup> As of mid-January 2026, his orders and actions had been challenged in over 550 lawsuits nationwide. Of these, plaintiffs had prevailed in 195 cases, the government had prevailed in 109 cases, there were split rulings in 16 cases, 228 cases were pending, and 25 cases were closed.<sup about="#mwt1489" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-421" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-421"}}'><a href="#cite_note-421" id="mwCh0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCh4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCh8">[</span>419<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCiA">]</span></span></a></sup> Both Republican and Democratic judicial appointees have found numerous constitutional and statutory flaws with Trump administration policies.<sup about="#mwt1496" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-422" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-422"}}'><a href="#cite_note-422" id="mwCiE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCiI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCiM">[</span>420<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCiQ">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwCiU">Following legal setbacks, Trump increased his criticism of the judiciary and called for impeachment of federal judges who ruled against him.<sup about="#mwt1499" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-423" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-423"}}'><a href="#cite_note-423" id="mwCiY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCic"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCig">[</span>421<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCik">]</span></span></a></sup> By mid-July 2025, a <i id="mwCio"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Post" title="Washington Post" class="mw-redirect" id="mwCis">Washington Post</a></i> analysis found he defied judges and the courts in roughly one third of all cases against him, actions that were described by legal experts as unprecedented for any presidential administration.<sup about="#mwt1502" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-424" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-424"}}'><a href="#cite_note-424" id="mwCiw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCi0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCi4">[</span>422<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCi8">]</span></span></a></sup></p> 

<p id="mwCjA">His administration asserted a constitutional right to ignore federal law in its justification of several actions. It refused to enforce <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Protecting_Americans_from_Foreign_Adversary_Controlled_Applications_Act" title="Protecting Americans from Foreign Adversary Controlled Applications Act" id="mwCjE">a federal ban on TikTok</a>, and legal experts said Trump was "claiming a constitutional power to immunize private parties to commit otherwise illegal acts with impunity".<sup about="#mwt1505" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-425" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-425"}}'><a href="#cite_note-425" id="mwCjI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCjM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCjQ">[</span>423<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCjU">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1508" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-426" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-426"}}'><a href="#cite_note-426" id="mwCjY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCjc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCjg">[</span>424<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCjk">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1511" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-427" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-427"}}'><a href="#cite_note-427" id="mwCjo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCjs"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCjw">[</span>425<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCj0">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="33" id="mwCj4" aria-labelledby="Conflicts_of_interest_(2025–present)"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Conflicts_of_interest_(2025–present)"><span id="Conflicts_of_interest_.282025.E2.80.93present.29" typeof="mw:FallbackId"></span>Conflicts of interest (2025–present)</h3></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt1512" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwCj8" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"further","href":"./Template:Further"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Corruption allegations during the second Trump presidency"},"2":{"wt":"Cryptocurrency in the second Trump presidency"},"3":{"wt":"Insider trading allegations during the second Trump administration"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt1513" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt1512" id="mwCkA">Further information: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Corruption_allegations_during_the_second_Trump_presidency" title="Corruption allegations during the second Trump presidency">Corruption allegations during the second Trump presidency</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cryptocurrency_in_the_second_Trump_presidency" title="Cryptocurrency in the second Trump presidency">Cryptocurrency in the second Trump presidency</a>, and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Insider_trading_allegations_during_the_second_Trump_administration" title="Insider trading allegations during the second Trump administration">Insider trading allegations during the second Trump administration</a></div>
<p id="mwCkE">Trump's second presidency faced unprecedented conflicts of interest, self-enrichment, and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Insider_trading_allegations_during_the_second_Trump_administration" title="Insider trading allegations during the second Trump administration" id="mwCkI">allegations of insider trading</a> and corruption unrivaled in American history from historians, legal experts, and federal judges.<sup about="#mwt1521" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-428" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-428"}}'><a href="#cite_note-428" id="mwCkM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCkQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCkU">[</span>426<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCkY">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1522" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-cs_429-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"cs"}}'><a href="#cite_note-cs-429" id="mwCkc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCkg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCkk">[</span>427<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCko">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwCks">Trump's second presidency has been described as having fewer guardrails against conflicts of interest than his first,<sup about="#mwt1525" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Yourish_2025_430-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Yourish 2025"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Yourish_2025-430"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Yourish_2025-430" id="mwCkw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCk0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCk4">[</span>428<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCk8">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1528" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Schouten_2025_431-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Schouten 2025"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Schouten_2025-431"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Schouten_2025-431" id="mwClA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwClE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwClI">[</span>429<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwClM">]</span></span></a></sup> breaking with decades of ethical norms,<sup about="#mwt1531" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-432" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-432"}}'><a href="#cite_note-432" id="mwClQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwClU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwClY">[</span>430<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwClc">]</span></span></a></sup> and raising substantial corruption concerns.<sup about="#mwt1534" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Savage_05122025_433-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Savage 05122025"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Savage_05122025-433"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Savage_05122025-433" id="mwClg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwClk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwClo">[</span>431<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCls">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1537" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-cs_429-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"cs"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-cs-429"}}'><a href="#cite_note-cs-429" id="mwClw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCl0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCl4">[</span>427<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCl8">]</span></span></a></sup> He maintained a publicly traded company in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trump_Media_&amp;_Technology_Group" title="Trump Media &amp; Technology Group" id="mwCmA">Trump Media &amp; Technology Group</a> and diversified it into financial services.<sup about="#mwt1540" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-434" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-434"}}'><a href="#cite_note-434" id="mwCmE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCmI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCmM">[</span>432<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCmQ">]</span></span></a></sup> He pursued new overseas real estate deals involving state-affiliated entities and had several branding and licensing deals selling Trump-branded merchandise.<sup about="#mwt1541" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Yourish_2025_430-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Yourish 2025"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Yourish_2025-430" id="mwCmU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCmY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCmc">[</span>428<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCmg">]</span></span></a></sup> He profited from events held at his hotels and golf courses and did not place his assets in a blind trust, as previous presidents had done.<sup about="#mwt1544" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Schouten_1232025_435-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Schouten 1232025"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Schouten_1232025-435"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Schouten_1232025-435" id="mwCmk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCmo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCms">[</span>433<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCmw">]</span></span></a></sup> He made an unparalleled number of stock trades, totaling hundreds of millions of dollars across thousands of transactions, in companies with extensive business dealings before the federal government.<sup about="#mwt1547" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-436" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-436"}}'><a href="#cite_note-436" id="mwCm0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCm4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCm8">[</span>434<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCnA">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwCnE">Trump launched, promoted, and personally benefited from <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Trump_Organization#Other_ventures_and_investments" title="The Trump Organization" id="mwCnI">two cryptocurrency tokens</a> ("<a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meme_coin" title="Meme coin" id="mwCnM">meme coins</a>"), <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/$Trump" title="$Trump" id="mwCnQ">$Trump</a> and $Melania.<sup about="#mwt1550" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Weissert_1222025_437-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Weissert 1222025"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Weissert_1222025-437"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Weissert_1222025-437" id="mwCnU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCnY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCnc">[</span>435<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCng">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1553" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-438" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-438"}}'><a href="#cite_note-438" id="mwCnk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCno"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCns">[</span>436<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCnw">]</span></span></a></sup> He also benefited from his family's cryptocurrency company, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World_Liberty_Financial" title="World Liberty Financial" id="mwCn0">World Liberty Financial</a>, which engaged in an unprecedented mixing of private enterprise and government policy.<sup about="#mwt1556" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Lipton_04292025_439-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Lipton 04292025"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Lipton_04292025-439"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Lipton_04292025-439" id="mwCn4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCn8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCoA">[</span>437<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCoE">]</span></span></a></sup> By June 2026, a Reuters analysis estimated Trump and his family had made $2.3 billion from his crypto ventures, with investors losing the same amount.<sup about="#mwt1559" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-440" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-440"}}'><a href="#cite_note-440" id="mwCoI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCoM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCoQ">[</span>438<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCoU">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="34" id="mwCoY" aria-labelledby="Mass_terminations_of_federal_employees"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Mass_terminations_of_federal_employees">Mass terminations of federal employees</h3></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt1560" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwCoc" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"2025 United States federal mass layoffs"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt1561" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt1560" id="mwCog">Main article: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025_United_States_federal_mass_layoffs" title="2025 United States federal mass layoffs">2025 United States federal mass layoffs</a></div>

<p id="mwCok">Trump implemented a hiring freeze across the federal government and ordered telework of federal employees to be discontinued within 30 days.<sup about="#mwt1564" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-441" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-441"}}'><a href="#cite_note-441" id="mwCoo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCos"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCow">[</span>439<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCo0">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1567" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-upend_442-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"upend"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-upend-442"}}'><a href="#cite_note-upend-442" id="mwCo4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCo8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCpA">[</span>440<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCpE">]</span></span></a></sup> He ordered a review of many career civil service positions with the intention of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Policy/Career_appointment" title="Policy/Career appointment" class="mw-redirect" id="mwCpI">reclassifying them into at-will positions</a> without job protections.<sup about="#mwt1568" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-upend_442-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"upend"}}'><a href="#cite_note-upend-442" id="mwCpM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCpQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCpU">[</span>440<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCpY">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1571" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-443" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-443"}}'><a href="#cite_note-443" id="mwCpc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCpg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCpk">[</span>441<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCpo">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1574" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-444" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-444"}}'><a href="#cite_note-444" id="mwCps"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCpw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCp0">[</span>442<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCp4">]</span></span></a></sup> He initiated mass job terminations of federal employees,<sup about="#mwt1577" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Collinson_445-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Collinson"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Collinson-445"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Collinson-445" id="mwCp8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCqA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCqE">[</span>443<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCqI">]</span></span></a></sup> which were described by legal experts as unprecedented or in violation of federal law,<sup about="#mwt1580" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-446" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-446"}}'><a href="#cite_note-446" id="mwCqM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCqQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCqU">[</span>444<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCqY">]</span></span></a></sup> with the intent of replacing them with workers more aligned with his agenda.<sup about="#mwt1583" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-447" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-447"}}'><a href="#cite_note-447" id="mwCqc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCqg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCqk">[</span>445<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCqo">]</span></span></a></sup> By late February 2025, the administration had fired more than 30,000 people.<sup about="#mwt1586" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-448" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-448"}}'><a href="#cite_note-448" id="mwCqs"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCqw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCq0">[</span>446<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCq4">]</span></span></a></sup> He rescinded <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lyndon_B._Johnson" title="Lyndon B. Johnson" id="mwCq8">Lyndon B. Johnson</a>'s 1965 <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Executive_Order_11246" title="Executive Order 11246" id="mwCrA">Executive Order 11246</a>, which had mandated that federal contractors take affirmative action to end racial discrimination.<sup about="#mwt1589" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-449" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-449"}}'><a href="#cite_note-449" id="mwCrE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCrI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCrM">[</span>447<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCrQ">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1592" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-450" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-450"}}'><a href="#cite_note-450" id="mwCrU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCrY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCrc">[</span>448<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCrg">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwCrk">Trump and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elon_Musk" title="Elon Musk" id="mwCro">Elon Musk</a> with his <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Department_of_Government_Efficiency" title="Department of Government Efficiency" id="mwCrs">Department of Government Efficiency</a> largely dismantled several federal agencies, including <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/USAID" title="USAID" class="mw-redirect" id="mwCrw">USAID</a> and the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Department_of_Education" title="United States Department of Education" id="mwCr0">Department of Education</a>; fired several thousand staff; and reduced administrative functions to statutory minimums.<sup about="#mwt1595" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-451" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-451"}}'><a href="#cite_note-451" id="mwCr4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCr8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCsA">[</span>449<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCsE">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1598" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-452" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-452"}}'><a href="#cite_note-452" id="mwCsI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCsM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCsQ">[</span>450<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCsU">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1601" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-453" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-453"}}'><a href="#cite_note-453" id="mwCsY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCsc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCsg">[</span>451<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCsk">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="35" id="mwCso" aria-labelledby="Targeting_political_opponents"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Targeting_political_opponents">Targeting political opponents</h3></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt1602" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwCss" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Targeting of political opponents and civil society under the second Trump administration"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt1603" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt1602" id="mwCsw">Main article: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Targeting_of_political_opponents_and_civil_society_under_the_second_Trump_administration" title="Targeting of political opponents and civil society under the second Trump administration">Targeting of political opponents and civil society under the second Trump administration</a></div>
<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwCs0"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Donald_Trump_banner_on_DOJ_building,_Washington,_D.C._(2026-02-26).jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwCs4"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Donald_Trump_banner_on_DOJ_building,_Washington,_D.C._(2026-02-26).jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Donald_Trump_banner_on_DOJ_building%2C_Washington%2C_D.C._%282026-02-26%29.jpg/250px-Donald_Trump_banner_on_DOJ_building%2C_Washington%2C_D.C._%282026-02-26%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Donald_Trump_banner_on_DOJ_building%2C_Washington%2C_D.C._%282026-02-26%29.jpg/500px-Donald_Trump_banner_on_DOJ_building%2C_Washington%2C_D.C._%282026-02-26%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" alt='Nighttime photograph of a large vertical banner hanging between two neoclassical limestone columns. The banner shows Trump&apos;s official portrait, with "U.S. Department of Justice Robert F. Kennedy Building" above and "Make America Safe Again" below.' data-file-width="2925" data-file-height="3901" data-file-type="bitmap" height="240" width="180" class="mw-file-element mw-file-upright" style="--mw-file-upright: 0.7" id="mwCs8"/></a><figcaption id="mwCtA">Banner at the Department of Justice as of February 2026</figcaption></figure>

<p id="mwCtE">During his second presidency, the Trump administration took a series of actions using the government to target political opponents and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Civil_society" title="Civil society" id="mwCtI">civil society</a>. He threatened, signed executive actions, and ordered investigations into his political opponents, critics, and organizations aligned with the Democratic Party.<sup about="#mwt1606" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-454" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-454"}}'><a href="#cite_note-454" id="mwCtM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCtQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCtU">[</span>452<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCtY">]</span></span></a></sup> He ended the post-<a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Watergate" title="Watergate" class="mw-redirect" id="mwCtc">Watergate</a> norm of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Department_of_Justice" title="United States Department of Justice" id="mwCtg">Justice Department</a> (DOJ) independence, weaponizing it and agencies across the federal government to target his political enemies.<sup about="#mwt1609" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-455" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-455"}}'><a href="#cite_note-455" id="mwCtk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCto"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCts">[</span>453<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCtw">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1612" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-456" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-456"}}'><a href="#cite_note-456" id="mwCt0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCt4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCt8">[</span>454<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCuA">]</span></span></a></sup> Under pressure to bring charges against his opponents, prosecutors advanced weak cases that were rejected by <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grand_juries" title="Grand juries" class="mw-redirect" id="mwCuE">grand juries</a> and admonished by judges who accused them of misconduct, leading to an erosion of the DOJ's credibility.<sup about="#mwt1615" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-457" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-457"}}'><a href="#cite_note-457" id="mwCuI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCuM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCuQ">[</span>455<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCuU">]</span></span></a></sup> Trump's actions against civil society were described by hundreds of legal experts and political scientists as authoritarian, contributing to <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Democratic_backsliding_in_the_United_States" title="Democratic backsliding in the United States" id="mwCuY">democratic backsliding</a>, and negatively impacting the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rule_of_law" title="Rule of law" id="mwCuc">rule of law</a>.<sup about="#mwt1618" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-458" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-458"}}'><a href="#cite_note-458" id="mwCug"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCuk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCuo">[</span>456<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCus">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1621" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-459" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-459"}}'><a href="#cite_note-459" id="mwCuw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCu0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCu4">[</span>457<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCu8">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1624" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-460" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-460"}}'><a href="#cite_note-460" id="mwCvA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCvE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCvI">[</span>458<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCvM">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="36" id="mwCvQ" aria-labelledby="Pardons_and_commutations_(2025–present)"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Pardons_and_commutations_(2025–present)"><span id="Pardons_and_commutations_.282025.E2.80.93present.29" typeof="mw:FallbackId"></span>Pardons and commutations (2025–present)</h3></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt1625" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwCvU" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"further","href":"./Template:Further"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"List of people granted executive clemency in the second Trump presidency"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt1626" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt1625" id="mwCvY">Further information: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_people_granted_executive_clemency_in_the_second_Trump_presidency" title="List of people granted executive clemency in the second Trump presidency">List of people granted executive clemency in the second Trump presidency</a></div>
<p id="mwCvc">Trump's pardons and grants of clemency favored political allies and loyalists,<sup about="#mwt1629" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-461" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-461"}}'><a href="#cite_note-461" id="mwCvg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCvk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCvo">[</span>459<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCvs">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1632" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Thrush_May292025_462-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Thrush May292025"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Thrush_May292025-462"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Thrush_May292025-462" id="mwCvw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCv0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCv4">[</span>460<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCv8">]</span></span></a></sup> and disproportionately pardoned "the powerful, famous, well-connected, and wealthy" accused of white-collar crime.<sup about="#mwt1633" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Thrush_May292025_462-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Thrush May292025"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Thrush_May292025-462" id="mwCwA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCwE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCwI">[</span>460<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCwM">]</span></span></a></sup> Trump <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pardon_of_January_6_United_States_Capitol_attack_defendants" title="Pardon of January 6 United States Capitol attack defendants" id="mwCwQ">granted clemency to all January<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwCwU"> </span>6 rioters</a> convicted or charged on his first day in office, including those who violently attacked police.<sup about="#mwt1636" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Reilly2025_463-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Reilly2025"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Reilly2025-463"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Reilly2025-463" id="mwCwY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCwc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCwg">[</span>461<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCwk">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1639" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-NPR2025_464-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"NPR2025"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-NPR2025-464"}}'><a href="#cite_note-NPR2025-464" id="mwCwo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCws"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCww">[</span>462<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCw0">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwCw4">Trump is the first American president to issue pardons for businesses and to cancel unpaid penalties. By July 2026, he had issued nine pardons and wiped out $200<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwCw8"> </span>million in penalties, some of which was destined for victims of wrongdoing.<sup about="#mwt1642" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-465" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-465"}}'><a href="#cite_note-465" id="mwCxA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCxE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCxI">[</span>463<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCxM">]</span></span></a></sup> A June 2026 <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reuters" title="Reuters" id="mwCxQ">Reuters</a> investigation found 96% of clemency grants failed to meet long-standing Department of Justice guidelines for such requests, that they were based on partisan and personal criteria.<sup about="#mwt1645" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-466" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-466"}}'><a href="#cite_note-466" id="mwCxU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCxY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCxc">[</span>464<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCxg">]</span></span></a></sup> Lobbyists have told <i id="mwCxk"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Wall_Street_Journal" title="The Wall Street Journal" id="mwCxo">The Wall Street Journal</a></i> and other news outlets that fees typically range from $1<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwCxs"> </span>million to $6<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwCxw"> </span>million for a successful application.<sup about="#mwt1648" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-467" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-467"}}'><a href="#cite_note-467" id="mwCx0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCx4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCx8">[</span>465<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCyA">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1651" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-468" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-468"}}'><a href="#cite_note-468" id="mwCyE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCyI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCyM">[</span>466<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCyQ">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1654" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-469" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-469"}}'><a href="#cite_note-469" id="mwCyU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCyY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCyc">[</span>467<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCyg">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="37" id="mwCyk" aria-labelledby="Domestic_policy_(2025–present)"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Domestic_policy_(2025–present)"><span id="Domestic_policy_.282025.E2.80.93present.29" typeof="mw:FallbackId"></span>Domestic policy (2025–present)</h3></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt1655" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwCyo" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Domestic policy of the second Trump administration"},"2":{"wt":"Economic policy of the second Trump administration"},"3":{"wt":"Education policy of the second Trump administration"},"4":{"wt":"Science policy of the second Trump administration"},"5":{"wt":"Persecution of transgender people under the second Trump administration"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt1656" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt1655" id="mwCys">Main articles: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Domestic_policy_of_the_second_Trump_administration" title="Domestic policy of the second Trump administration">Domestic policy of the second Trump administration</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Economic_policy_of_the_second_Trump_administration" title="Economic policy of the second Trump administration">Economic policy of the second Trump administration</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Education_policy_of_the_second_Trump_administration" title="Education policy of the second Trump administration">Education policy of the second Trump administration</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Science_policy_of_the_second_Trump_administration" title="Science policy of the second Trump administration">Science policy of the second Trump administration</a>, and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Persecution_of_transgender_people_under_the_second_Trump_administration" title="Persecution of transgender people under the second Trump administration">Persecution of transgender people under the second Trump administration</a></div>

<p id="mwCyw">Trump ordered agencies to stop enforcement of disfavored rules in an attempt at large-scale deregulation that legal experts described as illegal and contrary to decades of federal law.<sup about="#mwt1659" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-470" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-470"}}'><a href="#cite_note-470" id="mwCy0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCy4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCy8">[</span>468<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCzA">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1662" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-471" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-471"}}'><a href="#cite_note-471" id="mwCzE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCzI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCzM">[</span>469<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCzQ">]</span></span></a></sup> He <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Branding_of_United_States_government_programs_and_facilities_after_Donald_Trump" title="Branding of United States government programs and facilities after Donald Trump" id="mwCzU">branded government programs, buildings, and paper money</a> with his name or likeness, the first sitting president to do so.<sup about="#mwt1665" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-472" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-472"}}'><a href="#cite_note-472" id="mwCzY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCzc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCzg">[</span>470<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCzk">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1668" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-473" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-473"}}'><a href="#cite_note-473" id="mwCzo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCzs"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCzw">[</span>471<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCz0">]</span></span></a></sup> He sought greater government control over private businesses and shifted away from traditional conservative <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Free_market" title="Free market" id="mwCz4">free market</a> orthodoxy,<sup about="#mwt1677" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-474" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-474"}}'><a href="#cite_note-474" id="mwCz8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC0A"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC0E">[</span>472<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC0I">]</span></span></a></sup> engaging in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/State_capitalism" title="State capitalism" id="mwC0M">state capitalism</a> by taking direct government equity stakes in multiple U.S. corporations.<sup about="#mwt1680" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-475" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-475"}}'><a href="#cite_note-475" id="mwC0Q"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC0U"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC0Y">[</span>473<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC0c">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1683" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Economist_08132025_476-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Economist 08132025"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Economist_08132025-476"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Economist_08132025-476" id="mwC0g"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC0k"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC0o">[</span>474<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC0s">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1686" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-477" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-477"}}'><a href="#cite_note-477" id="mwC0w"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC00"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC04">[</span>475<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC08">]</span></span></a></sup> Economists describe his economic policy and use of tariffs as contributing to inflation amidst the ongoing <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_affordability_crisis" title="United States affordability crisis" id="mwC1A">United States affordability crisis</a>, which Trump has referred to as a "hoax".<sup about="#mwt1689" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-478" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-478"}}'><a href="#cite_note-478" id="mwC1E"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC1I"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC1M">[</span>476<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC1Q">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<section data-mw-section-id="38" id="mwC1U" aria-labelledby="Science"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading4"><h4 id="Science">Science</h4></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt1690" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwC1Y" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"2025 United States federal government grant pause"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt1691" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt1690" id="mwC1c">Main article: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025_United_States_federal_government_grant_pause" title="2025 United States federal government grant pause">2025 United States federal government grant pause</a></div>
<p id="mwC1g">Trump canceled and paused federal grants and made large cuts to scientific research,<sup about="#mwt1694" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-479" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-479"}}'><a href="#cite_note-479" id="mwC1k"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC1o"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC1s">[</span>477<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC1w">]</span></span></a></sup> several of which were found by judges and the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Government_Accountability_Office" title="Government Accountability Office" class="mw-redirect" id="mwC10">Government Accountability Office</a> as being illegal and unconstitutional.<sup about="#mwt1697" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-480" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-480"}}'><a href="#cite_note-480" id="mwC14"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC18"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC2A">[</span>478<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC2E">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1700" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-481" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-481"}}'><a href="#cite_note-481" id="mwC2I"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC2M"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC2Q">[</span>479<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC2U">]</span></span></a></sup> Trump and his administration's <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Make_America_Healthy_Again" title="Make America Healthy Again" id="mwC2Y">Make America Healthy Again</a> agenda promoted <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anti-science" title="Anti-science" class="mw-redirect" id="mwC2c">anti-science</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anti-vaccine_activism" title="Anti-vaccine activism" id="mwC2g">anti-vaccine activism</a>, resulting in a resurgence of whooping cough and measles.<sup about="#mwt1703" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-482" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-482"}}'><a href="#cite_note-482" id="mwC2k"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC2o"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC2s">[</span>480<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC2w">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1706" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-483" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-483"}}'><a href="#cite_note-483" id="mwC20"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC24"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC28">[</span>481<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC3A">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="39" id="mwC3E" aria-labelledby="National_emergencies/health_policy"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading4"><h4 id="National_emergencies/health_policy"><span id="National_emergencies.2Fhealth_policy" typeof="mw:FallbackId"></span>National emergencies/health policy</h4></div>
<p id="mwC3I">Trump relied on declaring national emergencies to justify hundreds of actions and bypass congressional approval or regulatory review.<sup about="#mwt1709" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-484" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-484"}}'><a href="#cite_note-484" id="mwC3M"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC3Q"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC3U">[</span>482<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC3Y">]</span></span></a></sup> For instance, he declared a national energy emergency, allowing the suspension of environmental regulations, loosening the rules for fossil fuel extraction, and limiting renewable energy projects.<sup about="#mwt1712" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-485" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-485"}}'><a href="#cite_note-485" id="mwC3c"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC3g"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC3k">[</span>483<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC3o">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1715" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-486" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-486"}}'><a href="#cite_note-486" id="mwC3s"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC3w"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC30">[</span>484<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC34">]</span></span></a></sup> Trump withdrew the U.S. from the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paris_Agreement" title="Paris Agreement" id="mwC38">Paris Agreement</a> on climate change during his first presidency, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Presidency_of_Joe_Biden" title="Presidency of Joe Biden" id="mwC4A">Biden</a> reentered the agreement in 2021, and Trump again withdrew from the agreement during his second presidency.<sup about="#mwt1718" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-487" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-487"}}'><a href="#cite_note-487" id="mwC4E"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC4I"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC4M">[</span>485<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC4Q">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="40" id="mwC4U" aria-labelledby="Social"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading4"><h4 id="Social">Social</h4></div>
<p id="mwC4Y">Trump has attributed societal problems to <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diversity,_equity,_and_inclusion" title="Diversity, equity, and inclusion" id="mwC4c">diversity, equity, and inclusion</a> (DEI) initiatives and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Woke#United_States" title="Woke" id="mwC4g">wokeness</a>.<sup about="#mwt1721" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Green250203_488-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Green250203"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Green250203-488"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Green250203-488" id="mwC4k"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC4o"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC4s">[</span>486<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC4w">]</span></span></a></sup> Equating diversity with incompetence,<sup about="#mwt1722" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Green250203_488-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Green250203"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Green250203-488" id="mwC40"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC44"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC48">[</span>486<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC5A">]</span></span></a></sup> he reversed pro-diversity policies in the federal government.<sup about="#mwt1725" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-489" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-489"}}'><a href="#cite_note-489" id="mwC5E"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC5I"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC5M">[</span>487<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC5Q">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1728" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-490" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-490"}}'><a href="#cite_note-490" id="mwC5U"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC5Y"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC5c">[</span>488<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC5g">]</span></span></a></sup> He threatened cultural institutions on DEI grounds<sup about="#mwt1731" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-491" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-491"}}'><a href="#cite_note-491" id="mwC5k"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC5o"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC5s">[</span>489<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC5w">]</span></span></a></sup> and sixty universities on accusations of antisemitism,<sup about="#mwt1734" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-WSJed_492-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"WSJed"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-WSJed-492"}}'><a href="#cite_note-WSJed-492" id="mwC50"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC54"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC58">[</span>490<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC6A">]</span></span></a></sup> and forced law firms to capitulate to his political agenda.<sup about="#mwt1737" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-APfirms_493-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"APfirms"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-APfirms-493"}}'><a href="#cite_note-APfirms-493" id="mwC6E"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC6I"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC6M">[</span>491<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC6Q">]</span></span></a></sup> He banned transgender people from the military <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Persecution_of_transgender_people_under_the_second_Trump_administration" title="Persecution of transgender people under the second Trump administration" id="mwC6U">through a series of executive orders and other actions</a>;<sup about="#mwt1740" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-494" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-494"}}'><a href="#cite_note-494" id="mwC6Y"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC6c"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC6g">[</span>492<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC6k">]</span></span></a></sup> restricted or defunded gender-affirming healthcare; opposed <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inclusive_language" title="Inclusive language" id="mwC6o">inclusive language</a>;<sup about="#mwt1743" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-495" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-495"}}'><a href="#cite_note-495" id="mwC6s"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC6w"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC60">[</span>493<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC64">]</span></span></a></sup> censored research and education materials;<sup about="#mwt1746" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-496" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-496"}}'><a href="#cite_note-496" id="mwC68"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC7A"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC7E">[</span>494<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC7I">]</span></span></a></sup> targeted schools, universities, and cultural institutions accused of promoting what his government calls "gender ideology";<sup about="#mwt1749" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-npr-ideology_497-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"npr-ideology"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-npr-ideology-497"}}'><a href="#cite_note-npr-ideology-497" id="mwC7M"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC7Q"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC7U">[</span>495<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC7Y">]</span></span></a></sup> barred transgender athletes from sports; and required U.S. passports to state transgender people's sex assigned at birth.<sup about="#mwt1752" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-498" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-498"}}'><a href="#cite_note-498" id="mwC7c"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC7g"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC7k">[</span>496<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC7o">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwC7s">Trump expanded the domestic use of the military, ordering <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025_deployment_of_federal_forces_in_the_United_States" title="2025 deployment of federal forces in the United States" class="mw-redirect" id="mwC7w">military deployments to several cities led by the Democratic Party</a>, and threatened to expand his deployments further.<sup about="#mwt1755" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-499" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-499"}}'><a href="#cite_note-499" id="mwC70"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC74"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC78">[</span>497<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC8A">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1758" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-500" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-500"}}'><a href="#cite_note-500" id="mwC8E"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC8I"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC8M">[</span>498<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC8Q">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="41" id="mwC8U" aria-labelledby="One_Big_Beautiful_Bill_Act"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading4"><h4 id="One_Big_Beautiful_Bill_Act">One Big Beautiful Bill Act</h4></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt1759" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwC8Y" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"One Big Beautiful Bill Act"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt1760" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt1759" id="mwC8c">Main article: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/One_Big_Beautiful_Bill_Act" title="One Big Beautiful Bill Act">One Big Beautiful Bill Act</a></div>
<p id="mwC8g">In July 2025, Trump signed the One Big Beautiful Bill Act into law. The bill made the temporary tax cuts of the 2017 <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tax_Cuts_and_Jobs_Act" title="Tax Cuts and Jobs Act" id="mwC8k">Tax Cuts and Jobs Act</a> permanent and added additional tax deductions for a total of around $4.5<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwC8o"> </span>trillion, mostly benefiting the highest income brackets and costing people in the lowest income bracket $1,600 per year. It increased funding for national defense, deportations, the border wall, and Trump's proposed <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Golden_Dome_(missile_defense_system)" title="Golden Dome (missile defense system)" id="mwC8s">Golden Dome</a> missile defense system. It removed tax credits for renewable energy sources such as wind and solar power and for buyers of electric vehicles. The bill cut funding for <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Medicaid" title="Medicaid" id="mwC8w">Medicaid</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Supplemental_Nutrition_Assistance_Program" title="Supplemental Nutrition Assistance Program" id="mwC80">SNAP</a> and added additional work requirements for eligibility and a $35 co-payment for some Medicaid services; the cuts and additional requirements will take effect after the 2026 general election. The bill was projected by the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Congressional_Budget_Office" title="Congressional Budget Office" id="mwC84">Congressional Budget Office</a> to increase the budget deficit by $3.4<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwC88"> </span>trillion by 2034, cause 11.8<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwC9A"> </span>million people to lose Medicaid coverage, and eliminate SNAP benefits for three<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwC9E"> </span>million people.<sup about="#mwt1763" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-501" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-501"}}'><a href="#cite_note-501" id="mwC9I"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC9M"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC9Q">[</span>499<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC9U">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1766" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-502" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-502"}}'><a href="#cite_note-502" id="mwC9Y"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC9c"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC9g">[</span>500<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC9k">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section></section><section data-mw-section-id="42" id="mwC9o" aria-labelledby="Immigration_(2025–present)"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Immigration_(2025–present)"><span id="Immigration_.282025.E2.80.93present.29" typeof="mw:FallbackId"></span>Immigration (2025–present)</h3></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt1767" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwC9s" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Immigration policy of the second Trump administration"},"2":{"wt":"Deportation in the second Trump administration"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt1768" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt1767" id="mwC9w">Main articles: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Immigration_policy_of_the_second_Trump_administration" title="Immigration policy of the second Trump administration">Immigration policy of the second Trump administration</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deportation_in_the_second_Trump_administration" title="Deportation in the second Trump administration">Deportation in the second Trump administration</a></div>

<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt1769" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwC90" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"further","href":"./Template:Further"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Mexico–United States border crisis#Second Trump administration (2025–present)"},"2":{"wt":"Mexico–United States border wall#Second Trump administration (2025–present)"},"3":{"wt":"Visa and deportation controversies in the second Trump administration"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt1770" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt1769" id="mwC94">Further information: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mexico–United_States_border_crisis#Second_Trump_administration_(2025–present)" title="Mexico–United States border crisis">Mexico–United States border crisis §<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>Second Trump administration (2025–present)</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mexico–United_States_border_wall#Second_Trump_administration_(2025–present)" title="Mexico–United States border wall">Mexico–United States border wall §<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>Second Trump administration (2025–present)</a>, and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Visa_and_deportation_controversies_in_the_second_Trump_administration" title="Visa and deportation controversies in the second Trump administration">Visa and deportation controversies in the second Trump administration</a></div>
<p id="mwC98">During Trump's first days in office, he instructed Border Patrol agents to summarily deport immigrants crossing the border and disabled the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CBP_One" title="CBP One" id="mwC-A">CBP One</a> app that was being used to schedule border crossings. He resumed the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Remain_in_Mexico" title="Remain in Mexico" id="mwC-E">Remain in Mexico</a> policy, designated drug cartels as terrorist groups, and ordered construction to be resumed on a border wall.<sup about="#mwt1773" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-503" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-503"}}'><a href="#cite_note-503" id="mwC-I"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC-M"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC-Q">[</span>501<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC-U">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1776" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Cbsjan28_504-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Cbsjan28"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Cbsjan28-504"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Cbsjan28-504" id="mwC-Y"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC-c"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC-g">[</span>502<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC-k">]</span></span></a></sup> He also revoked the parole status of immigrants who entered the U.S. under CBP One and ended <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parole_for_Cubans,_Haitians,_Nicaraguans,_and_Venezuelans" title="Parole for Cubans, Haitians, Nicaraguans, and Venezuelans" id="mwC-o">humanitarian parole for Cubans, Haitians, Nicaraguans, and Venezuelans</a>.<sup about="#mwt1777" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Cbsjan28_504-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Cbsjan28"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Cbsjan28-504" id="mwC-s"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC-w"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC-0">[</span>502<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC-4">]</span></span></a></sup> In March 2025, he used the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alien_Enemies_Act" title="Alien Enemies Act" class="mw-redirect" id="mwC-8">Alien Enemies Act</a> of 1798 to <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/March_2025_American_deportations_of_Venezuelans" title="March 2025 American deportations of Venezuelans" class="mw-redirect" id="mwC_A">deport migrants without trial</a> to be imprisoned at the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Terrorism_Confinement_Center" title="Terrorism Confinement Center" class="mw-redirect" id="mwC_E">Terrorism Confinement Center</a> in El Salvador.<sup about="#mwt1780" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-505" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-505"}}'><a href="#cite_note-505" id="mwC_I"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC_M"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC_Q">[</span>503<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC_U">]</span></span></a></sup> Trump targeted activists, legal immigrants, tourists, and students with visas who expressed criticism of his policies or pro-Palestinian advocacy.<sup about="#mwt1783" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-506" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-506"}}'><a href="#cite_note-506" id="mwC_Y"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC_c"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC_g">[</span>504<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC_k">]</span></span></a></sup> Several <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Detention_and_deportation_of_American_citizens_in_the_second_Trump_administration" title="Detention and deportation of American citizens in the second Trump administration" class="mw-redirect" id="mwC_o">American citizens were detained and deported</a>.<sup about="#mwt1786" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-507" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-507"}}'><a href="#cite_note-507" id="mwC_s"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC_w"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC_0">[</span>505<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC_4">]</span></span></a></sup> His aggressive and hardline deportation campaign led to large nationwide protests and violent confrontations with migrants and protesters, which increased in intensity following high-profile killings of U.S. citizens by federal agents during <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Metro_Surge" title="Operation Metro Surge" id="mwC_8">Operation Metro Surge</a> in January 2026.<sup about="#mwt1789" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-508" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-508"}}'><a href="#cite_note-508" id="mwDAA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDAE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDAI">[</span>506<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDAM">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1792" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-509" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-509"}}'><a href="#cite_note-509" id="mwDAQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDAU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDAY">[</span>507<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDAc">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="43" id="mwDAg" aria-labelledby="Foreign_policy_(2025–present)"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Foreign_policy_(2025–present)"><span id="Foreign_policy_.282025.E2.80.93present.29" typeof="mw:FallbackId"></span>Foreign policy (2025–present)</h3></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt1793" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwDAk" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Foreign policy of the second Trump administration"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt1794" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt1793" id="mwDAo">Main article: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foreign_policy_of_the_second_Trump_administration" title="Foreign policy of the second Trump administration">Foreign policy of the second Trump administration</a></div>

<p id="mwDAs">Trump's second-term foreign policy was described as <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_expansionism_under_Donald_Trump" title="American expansionism under Donald Trump" id="mwDAw">expansionist</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_imperialism" title="American imperialism" id="mwDA0">imperialist</a>.<sup about="#mwt1797" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-510" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-510"}}'><a href="#cite_note-510" id="mwDA4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDA8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDBA">[</span>508<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDBE">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1800" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-511" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-511"}}'><a href="#cite_note-511" id="mwDBI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDBM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDBQ">[</span>509<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDBU">]</span></span></a></sup> His administration favored <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hard_power" title="Hard power" id="mwDBY">hard power</a> to achieve its <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/America_First" title="America First" id="mwDBc">America First</a> foreign policy goals,<sup about="#mwt1803" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-512" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-512"}}'><a href="#cite_note-512" id="mwDBg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDBk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDBo">[</span>510<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDBs">]</span></span></a></sup> and dismantled or withdrew support from domestic and international organizations dedicated to advancing American <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soft_power" title="Soft power" id="mwDBw">soft power</a>.<sup about="#mwt1806" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-513" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-513"}}'><a href="#cite_note-513" id="mwDB0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDB4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDB8">[</span>511<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDCA">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1809" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-514" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-514"}}'><a href="#cite_note-514" id="mwDCE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDCI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDCM">[</span>512<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDCQ">]</span></span></a></sup> Trump's relations with U.S. allies have been transactional and ranged from indifference to hostility, while he has sought friendlier relations with certain U.S. adversaries.<sup about="#mwt1812" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-515" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-515"}}'><a href="#cite_note-515" id="mwDCU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDCY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDCc">[</span>513<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDCg">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwDCk"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:President_Trump_and_Ukrainian_President_Zelenskyy_Clash_During_Meeting_in_Oval_Office,_Feb._28,_2025.jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwDCo"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:President_Trump_and_Ukrainian_President_Zelenskyy_Clash_During_Meeting_in_Oval_Office,_Feb._28,_2025.jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/President_Trump_and_Ukrainian_President_Zelenskyy_Clash_During_Meeting_in_Oval_Office%2C_Feb._28%2C_2025.jpg/250px-President_Trump_and_Ukrainian_President_Zelenskyy_Clash_During_Meeting_in_Oval_Office%2C_Feb._28%2C_2025.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/President_Trump_and_Ukrainian_President_Zelenskyy_Clash_During_Meeting_in_Oval_Office%2C_Feb._28%2C_2025.jpg/500px-President_Trump_and_Ukrainian_President_Zelenskyy_Clash_During_Meeting_in_Oval_Office%2C_Feb._28%2C_2025.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="1920" data-file-height="1080" data-file-type="bitmap" height="141" width="250" class="mw-file-element" id="mwDCs"/></a><figcaption id="mwDCw">Meeting with <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Volodymyr_Zelenskyy" title="Volodymyr Zelenskyy" id="mwDC0">Volodymyr Zelenskyy</a>, February 2025</figcaption></figure>
<p id="mwDC4">In February 2025, Trump and Vice President Vance <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025_Trump–Zelenskyy_Oval_Office_meeting" title="2025 Trump–Zelenskyy Oval Office meeting" id="mwDC8">berated Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelenskyy</a> in a highly contentious televised meeting. Media outlets described it as an unprecedented public confrontation between an American president and a foreign <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Head_of_state" title="Head of state" id="mwDDA">head of state</a>.<sup about="#mwt1815" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Liptak_20250228_516-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Liptak 20250228"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Liptak_20250228-516"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Liptak_20250228-516" id="mwDDE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDDI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDDM">[</span>514<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDDQ">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1818" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-517" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-517"}}'><a href="#cite_note-517" id="mwDDU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDDY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDDc">[</span>515<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDDg">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwDDk">In 2025, Trump <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greenland_crisis" title="Greenland crisis" id="mwDDo">threatened the annexation of Greenland</a>, including by military force and the imposition of tariffs on European allies. In January 2026, he withdrew both threats, stating that he had reached "the framework of a future deal" with NATO.<sup about="#mwt1821" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-518" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-518"}}'><a href="#cite_note-518" id="mwDDs"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDDw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDD0">[</span>516<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDD4">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<section data-mw-section-id="44" id="mwDD8" aria-labelledby="Tariffs"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading4"><h4 id="Tariffs">Tariffs</h4></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt1822" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwDEA" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Tariffs in the second Trump administration"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt1823" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt1822" id="mwDEE">Main article: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tariffs_in_the_second_Trump_administration" title="Tariffs in the second Trump administration">Tariffs in the second Trump administration</a></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt1824" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwDEI" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Further","href":"./Template:Further"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"China–United States trade war"},"2":{"wt":"2025–2026 United States trade war with Canada"},"3":{"wt":"2025–2026 United States trade war with Mexico"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt1825" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt1824" id="mwDEM">Further information: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China–United_States_trade_war" title="China–United States trade war">China–United States trade war</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025–2026_United_States_trade_war_with_Canada" title="2025–2026 United States trade war with Canada" class="mw-redirect">2025–2026 United States trade war with Canada</a>, and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025–2026_United_States_trade_war_with_Mexico" title="2025–2026 United States trade war with Mexico" class="mw-redirect">2025–2026 United States trade war with Mexico</a></div>
<p id="mwDEQ">Trump's economic policies have been described as <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Protectionism_in_the_United_States" title="Protectionism in the United States" id="mwDEU">protectionist</a>,<sup about="#mwt1828" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-519" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-519"}}'><a href="#cite_note-519" id="mwDEY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDEc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDEg">[</span>517<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDEk">]</span></span></a></sup> with him imposing tariffs on most countries, including large tariffs on major trading partners China, Canada, and Mexico.<sup about="#mwt1831" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-520" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-520"}}'><a href="#cite_note-520" id="mwDEo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDEs"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDEw">[</span>518<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDE0">]</span></span></a></sup> He started a global trade war, imposing tariffs at the highest level since the 1930 <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Smoot–Hawley_Tariff_Act" title="Smoot–Hawley Tariff Act" id="mwDE4">Smoot–Hawley Tariff Act</a> at the onset of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_Depression" title="Great Depression" id="mwDE8">Great Depression</a>.<sup about="#mwt1834" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-521" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-521"}}'><a href="#cite_note-521" id="mwDFA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDFE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDFI">[</span>519<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDFM">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1837" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-522" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-522"}}'><a href="#cite_note-522" id="mwDFQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDFU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDFY">[</span>520<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDFc">]</span></span></a></sup> In February 2026, the Supreme Court ruled the tariffs he imposed under the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/International_Emergency_Economic_Powers_Act" title="International Emergency Economic Powers Act" id="mwDFg">International Emergency Economic Powers Act</a> to be illegal.<sup about="#mwt1840" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-523" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-523"}}'><a href="#cite_note-523" id="mwDFk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDFo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDFs">[</span>521<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDFw">]</span></span></a></sup> Trump frequently threatened and enacted tariffs against treaty allies for opposing his political objectives.<sup about="#mwt1843" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-524" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-524"}}'><a href="#cite_note-524" id="mwDF0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDF4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDF8">[</span>522<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDGA">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1846" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-525" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-525"}}'><a href="#cite_note-525" id="mwDGE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDGI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDGM">[</span>523<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDGQ">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="45" id="mwDGU" aria-labelledby="Latin_America"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading4"><h4 id="Latin_America">Latin America</h4></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt1847" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwDGY" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Further","href":"./Template:Further"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Operation Southern Spear"},"2":{"wt":"American expansionism under Donald Trump"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt1848" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt1847" id="mwDGc">Further information: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Southern_Spear" title="Operation Southern Spear" class="mw-redirect">Operation Southern Spear</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_expansionism_under_Donald_Trump" title="American expansionism under Donald Trump">American expansionism under Donald Trump</a></div>

<p id="mwDGg">Trump pursued legally controversial military strikes against <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_strikes_on_alleged_drug_traffickers_during_Operation_Southern_Spear" title="United States strikes on alleged drug traffickers during Operation Southern Spear" id="mwDGk">alleged drug boats</a> in international waters,<sup about="#mwt1851" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-526" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-526"}}'><a href="#cite_note-526" id="mwDGo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDGs"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDGw">[</span>524<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDG0">]</span></span></a></sup> and ordered <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_military_buildup_in_the_Caribbean_during_Operation_Southern_Spear" title="United States military buildup in the Caribbean during Operation Southern Spear" id="mwDG4">a large military buildup in the Caribbean</a>.<sup about="#mwt1854" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-527" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-527"}}'><a href="#cite_note-527" id="mwDG8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDHA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDHE">[</span>525<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDHI">]</span></span></a></sup> In November 2025, Trump pardoned former Honduran president <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juan_Orlando_Hernández" title="Juan Orlando Hernández" id="mwDHM">Juan Orlando Hernández</a>, who had been extradited to the U.S. in 2022 and sentenced to 45 years in prison for drug trafficking. In January 2026, the U.S. captured Venezuelan president <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nicolás_Maduro" title="Nicolás Maduro" id="mwDHQ">Nicolás Maduro</a> in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_United_States_strikes_in_Venezuela#Capture_of_Maduro" title="2026 United States strikes in Venezuela" class="mw-redirect" id="mwDHU">a military raid in Venezuela</a> and took him to New York, where he was charged with drug trafficking.<sup about="#mwt1857" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-528" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-528"}}'><a href="#cite_note-528" id="mwDHY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDHc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDHg">[</span>526<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDHk">]</span></span></a></sup> Following the raid, the United States <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_United_States_intervention_in_Venezuela" title="2026 United States intervention in Venezuela" id="mwDHo">de facto controlled Venezuela</a>, including its domestic finances, foreign policy, government appointments, revenues, and oil reserves.<sup about="#mwt1860" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-529" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-529"}}'><a href="#cite_note-529" id="mwDHs"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDHw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDH0">[</span>527<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDH4">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1863" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-530" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-530"}}'><a href="#cite_note-530" id="mwDH8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDIA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDIE">[</span>528<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDII">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwDIM">In 2026, Trump ordered <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_United_States_fuel_blockade_on_Cuba" title="2026 United States fuel blockade on Cuba" id="mwDIQ">an oil blockade of Cuba</a> and sanctions against foreign companies doing business in Cuba, resulting in repeated island-wide power outages<sup about="#mwt1866" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-531" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-531"}}'><a href="#cite_note-531" id="mwDIU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDIY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDIc">[</span>529<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDIg">]</span></span></a></sup> and a near collapse of the tourism industry.<sup about="#mwt1869" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-532" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-532"}}'><a href="#cite_note-532" id="mwDIk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDIo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDIs">[</span>530<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDIw">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="46" id="mwDI0" aria-labelledby="Middle_East_wars"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading4"><h4 id="Middle_East_wars">Middle East wars</h4></div>

<p id="mwDI4">Trump has taken a pro-Israel stance and continued <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_support_for_Israel_in_the_Gaza_war" title="United States support for Israel in the Gaza war" id="mwDI8">support for Israel in the Gaza war</a>.<sup about="#mwt1872" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-533" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-533"}}'><a href="#cite_note-533" id="mwDJA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDJE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDJI">[</span>531<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDJM">]</span></span></a></sup> From March to May 2025, Trump <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/March–May_2025_United_States_attacks_in_Yemen" title="March–May 2025 United States attacks in Yemen" id="mwDJQ">launched an extensive aerial campaign</a> against <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Houthis" title="Houthis" id="mwDJU">Houthi</a> targets in Yemen<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwDJY">—</span>his first major military operation in the Middle East during his second term.<sup about="#mwt1875" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-534" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-534"}}'><a href="#cite_note-534" id="mwDJc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDJg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDJk">[</span>532<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDJo">]</span></span></a></sup> In June 2025, Trump joined Israel in its <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Twelve-Day_War" title="Twelve-Day War" id="mwDJs">Twelve-Day War</a> with Iran, ordering the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025_United_States_strikes_on_Iranian_nuclear_sites" title="2025 United States strikes on Iranian nuclear sites" id="mwDJw">bombing of Iranian nuclear sites</a> by U.S. planes.<sup about="#mwt1878" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-535" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-535"}}'><a href="#cite_note-535" id="mwDJ0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDJ4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDJ8">[</span>533<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDKA">]</span></span></a></sup> His <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gaza_peace_plan" title="Gaza peace plan" id="mwDKE">plan for a Gaza ceasefire deal</a> between Israel and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hamas" title="Hamas" id="mwDKI">Hamas</a> was signed in October 2025, leading to the creation of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Board_of_Peace" title="Board of Peace" id="mwDKM">Board of Peace</a>.<sup about="#mwt1881" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-536" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-536"}}'><a href="#cite_note-536" id="mwDKQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDKU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDKY">[</span>534<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDKc">]</span></span></a></sup></p> 

<p id="mwDKg">In February 2026, Trump authorized joint U.S.–Israeli air strikes on Iran, sparking the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_Iran_war" title="2026 Iran war" id="mwDKk">2026 Iran war</a> and killing Iranian supreme leader <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ali_Khamenei" title="Ali Khamenei" id="mwDKo">Ali Khamenei</a>.<sup about="#mwt1884" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-537" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-537"}}'><a href="#cite_note-537" id="mwDKs"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDKw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDK0">[</span>535<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDK4">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1887" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-538" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-538"}}'><a href="#cite_note-538" id="mwDK8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDLA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDLE">[</span>536<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDLI">]</span></span></a></sup> During the conflict, Trump and his administration made diverse and inconsistent <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rationale_for_the_2026_Iran_war" title="Rationale for the 2026 Iran war" id="mwDLM">explanations for starting the war</a>.<sup about="#mwt1890" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-539" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-539"}}'><a href="#cite_note-539" id="mwDLQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDLU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDLY">[</span>537<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDLc">]</span></span></a></sup> On April<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwDLg"> </span>8, a temporary ceasefire went into effect. The Strait of Hormuz remained closed, and both sides continued sporadic attacks while several rounds of talks did not result in a peace settlement.<sup about="#mwt1893" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-540" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-540"}}'><a href="#cite_note-540" id="mwDLk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDLo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDLs">[</span>538<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDLw">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1896" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-541" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-541"}}'><a href="#cite_note-541" id="mwDL0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDL4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDL8">[</span>539<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDMA">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section></section><section data-mw-section-id="47" id="mwDME" aria-labelledby="Personnel_(2025–present)"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Personnel_(2025–present)"><span id="Personnel_.282025.E2.80.93present.29" typeof="mw:FallbackId"></span>Personnel (2025–present)</h3></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt1897" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwDMI" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Political appointments of the second Trump administration"},"2":{"wt":"Second cabinet of Donald Trump"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt1898" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt1897" id="mwDMM">Main articles: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Political_appointments_of_the_second_Trump_administration" title="Political appointments of the second Trump administration">Political appointments of the second Trump administration</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second_cabinet_of_Donald_Trump" title="Second cabinet of Donald Trump">Second cabinet of Donald Trump</a></div>

<p id="mwDMQ">In his second term, Trump selected <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second_cabinet_of_Donald_Trump" title="Second cabinet of Donald Trump" id="mwDMU">cabinet</a> members with personal loyalty to him,<sup about="#mwt1901" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-542" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-542"}}'><a href="#cite_note-542" id="mwDMY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDMc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDMg">[</span>540<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDMk">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1904" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-focus_543-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"focus"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-focus-543"}}'><a href="#cite_note-focus-543" id="mwDMo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDMs"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDMw">[</span>541<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDM0">]</span></span></a></sup> with the "focus on loyalty over subject-matter expertise".<sup about="#mwt1905" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-focus_543-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"focus"}}'><a href="#cite_note-focus-543" id="mwDM4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDM8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDNA">[</span>541<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDNE">]</span></span></a></sup> In February 2025, the White House stated that <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elon_Musk" title="Elon Musk" id="mwDNI">Elon Musk</a> was a <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special_government_employee" title="Special government employee" id="mwDNM">special government employee</a>.<sup about="#mwt1908" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-musk_544-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"musk"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-musk-544"}}'><a href="#cite_note-musk-544" id="mwDNQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDNU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDNY">[</span>542<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDNc">]</span></span></a></sup> Trump gave Musk's <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Department_of_Government_Efficiency" title="Department of Government Efficiency" id="mwDNg">Department of Government Efficiency</a> (DOGE) access to many federal government agencies.<sup about="#mwt1909" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-musk_544-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"musk"}}'><a href="#cite_note-musk-544" id="mwDNk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDNo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDNs">[</span>542<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDNw">]</span></span></a></sup> Musk's teams operated in eighteen departments and agencies in the administration's first month,<sup about="#mwt1912" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-545" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-545"}}'><a href="#cite_note-545" id="mwDN0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDN4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDN8">[</span>543<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDOA">]</span></span></a></sup> including in the Treasury Department's $5<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwDOE"> </span>trillion payment system,<sup about="#mwt1915" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-546" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-546"}}'><a href="#cite_note-546" id="mwDOI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDOM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDOQ">[</span>544<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDOU">]</span></span></a></sup> the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Small_Business_Administration" title="Small Business Administration" id="mwDOY">Small Business Administration</a>, the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Office_of_Personnel_Management" title="Office of Personnel Management" class="mw-redirect" id="mwDOc">Office of Personnel Management</a>, and the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/General_Services_Administration" title="General Services Administration" id="mwDOg">General Services Administration</a>.<sup about="#mwt1918" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-547" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-547"}}'><a href="#cite_note-547" id="mwDOk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDOo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDOs">[</span>545<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDOw">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section></section><section data-mw-section-id="48" id="mwDO0" aria-labelledby="Rhetoric,_behavior,_and_political_practice"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id="Rhetoric,_behavior,_and_political_practice"><span id="Rhetoric.2C_behavior.2C_and_political_practice" typeof="mw:FallbackId"></span>Rhetoric, behavior, and political practice</h2></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt1919" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwDO4" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Political positions of Donald Trump"},"2":{"wt":"Rhetoric of Donald Trump"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt1920" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt1919" id="mwDO8">Main articles: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Political_positions_of_Donald_Trump" title="Political positions of Donald Trump">Political positions of Donald Trump</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rhetoric_of_Donald_Trump" title="Rhetoric of Donald Trump">Rhetoric of Donald Trump</a></div>

<p id="mwDPA">Starting with his advocacy of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Birther" title="Birther" class="mw-redirect" id="mwDPE">birther</a> conspiracy theories in the 2010s, Trump helped bring once-fringe far-right ideas and organizations into the mainstream.<sup about="#mwt1921" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-548" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":""},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-548"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"refn","href":"./Template:Refn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"\n* {{cite news |last=Bierman |first=Noah |date=August 22, 2016 |title=Donald Trump helps bring far-right media&apos;s edgier elements into the mainstream |work=[[Los Angeles Times]] |url=https://www.latimes.com/politics/la-na-pol-trump-media-20160820-snap-story.html |access-date=October 7, 2021}}\n* {{cite news |last=Baker |first=Peter |author-link=Peter Baker (journalist) |date=December 1, 2022 |title=Trump Embraces Extremism as He Seeks to Reclaim Office |work=[[The New York Times]] |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2022/12/01/us/politics/trump-extremism-candidacy.html |url-access=subscription |access-date=January 20, 2025}}\n* {{cite news |last=Oreskes |first=Benjamin |date=May 23, 2024 |title=Trump and GOP repeatedly echo Nazi and far-right ideology as they aim to retake White House |url=https://www.latimes.com/politics/story/2024-05-23/trump-gop-nazi-presidential-election-politics |access-date=July 27, 2025 |work=[[Los Angeles Times]]}}\n* {{cite news |last1=Swenson |first1=Ali |last2=Kunzelman |first2=Michael |date=November 18, 2023 |title=Fears of political violence are growing as the 2024 campaign heats up and conspiracy theories evolve |work=[[AP News]] |url=https://apnews.com/article/depape-paul-pelosi-qanon-conspiracy-theories-violence-390ad310fa34b0edb925d88540a7ddcd |access-date=January 20, 2025}}\n"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-548" id="mwDPI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDPM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDPQ">[</span>546<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDPU">]</span></span></a></sup>
The <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alt-right" title="Alt-right" id="mwDPY">alt-right</a> movement coalesced around and supported his candidacy due in part to <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Opposition_to_multiculturalism" title="Opposition to multiculturalism" class="mw-redirect" id="mwDPc">opposition to multiculturalism</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Opposition_to_immigration" title="Opposition to immigration" id="mwDPg">immigration</a>.<sup about="#mwt1934" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-549" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-549"}}'><a href="#cite_note-549" id="mwDPk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDPo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDPs">[</span>547<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDPw">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1937" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-550" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-550"}}'><a href="#cite_note-550" id="mwDP0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDP4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDP8">[</span>548<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDQA">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1940" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-551" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-551"}}'><a href="#cite_note-551" id="mwDQE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDQI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDQM">[</span>549<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDQQ">]</span></span></a></sup> 
During his 2016 campaign, Trump's politics and rhetoric led to the formation of a <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Political_movement" title="Political movement" id="mwDQU">political movement</a> known as <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trumpism" title="Trumpism" id="mwDQY">Trumpism</a>,<sup about="#mwt1956" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-552" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-552"}}'><a href="#cite_note-552" id="mwDQc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDQg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDQk">[</span>550<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDQo">]</span></span></a></sup> which has been <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trumpism#Cult_of_personality" title="Trumpism" id="mwDQs">compared to a cult of personality</a>.<sup about="#mwt1941" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-553" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":""},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-553"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"refn","href":"./Template:Refn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"\n* {{cite journal |last=Sundahl |first=Anne-Mette Holmgård |title=Personality Cult or a Mere Matter of Popularity? |journal=[[International Journal of Politics, Culture, and Society]] |volume=36 |issue=4 |date=May 4, 2022 |pages=431–458 |doi=10.1007/s10767-022-09423-0 |pmid=35528318 |pmc=9066393}}\n* {{cite journal |last1=Goldsmith |first1=Benajmin E. |last2=Moen |first2=Lars J. K. |date=May 14, 2024 |title=The personality of a personality cult? Personality characteristics of Donald Trump&apos;s most loyal supporters |journal=[[Political Psychology]] |volume=46 |issue=Special Issue |pages=225–243 |issn=0162-895X |doi=10.1111/pops.12991 |doi-access=free|hdl=1885/733752725 |hdl-access=free}}\n* {{cite journal |last1=Adams |first1=Kenneth Alan |date=Spring 2021 |title=The Trump Death Cult |url=https://www.proquest.com/openview/c5d4601ebe8dcb232f9ab2965e900d70/1?pq-origsite=gscholar&amp;cbl=35407 |journal=[[Journal of Psychohistory]] |volume=48 |issue=4 |access-date=February 22, 2026 |pages=256–276 |issn=0145-3378}}\n* {{cite journal |last1=Diamond |first1=Michael J. |date=February 22, 2023 |title=Perverted Containment: Trumpism, Cult Creation, and the Rise of Destructive American Populism |url=https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/07351690.2023.2163147 |journal=Psychoanalytic Inquiry |volume=43 |issue=2 |access-date=February 22, 2026 |pages=96–109 |issn=0735-1690 |doi=10.1080/07351690.2023.2163147 |publisher=[[Taylor &amp; Francis]] |url-access=subscription}}\n*{{cite journal |last1=Reyes |first1=Antonio |date=May 4, 2020 |title=I, Trump The cult of personality, anti-intellectualism and the Post-Truth era |url=https://www.jbe-platform.com/content/journals/10.1075/jlp.20002.rey |journal=Journal of Language and Politics |volume=19 |issue=6 |access-date=February 22, 2026 |pages=869–892 |issn=1569-2159 |doi=10.1075/jlp.20002.rey |url-access=subscription}}\n"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-553" id="mwDQw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDQ0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDQ4">[</span>551<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDQ8">]</span></span></a></sup>
His political positions have been described as <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Populist" title="Populist" class="mw-redirect" id="mwDRA">populist</a><sup about="#mwt1957" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTERoss2024298_554-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTERoss2024298"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTERoss2024298-554"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Ross"},"2":{"wt":"2024"},"p":{"wt":"298"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTERoss2024298-554" id="mwDRE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDRI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDRM">[</span>552<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDRQ">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1960" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEUrbinati2019_555-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEUrbinati2019"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEUrbinati2019-555"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Urbinati"},"2":{"wt":"2019"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEUrbinati2019-555" id="mwDRU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDRY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDRc">[</span>553<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDRg">]</span></span></a></sup> or <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Right-wing_populist" title="Right-wing populist" class="mw-redirect" id="mwDRk">right-wing populist</a>,<sup about="#mwt1963" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTECampaniConcepciónSolerSavín2022_556-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTECampaniConcepciónSolerSavín2022"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTECampaniConcepciónSolerSavín2022-556"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Campani"},"2":{"wt":"Concepción"},"3":{"wt":"Soler"},"4":{"wt":"Savín"},"5":{"wt":"2022"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTECampaniConcepciónSolerSavín2022-556" id="mwDRo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDRs"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDRw">[</span>554<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDR0">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1968" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-557" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-557"}}'><a href="#cite_note-557" id="mwDR4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDR8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDSA">[</span>555<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDSE">]</span></span></a></sup> and favoring an expansion of presidential power under a maximalist interpretation of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unitary_executive_theory" title="Unitary executive theory" id="mwDSI">unitary executive theory</a>.<sup about="#mwt1971" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-558" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-558"}}'><a href="#cite_note-558" id="mwDSM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDSQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDSU">[</span>556<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDSY">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1974" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-559" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-559"}}'><a href="#cite_note-559" id="mwDSc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDSg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDSk">[</span>557<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDSo">]</span></span></a></sup></p> 

<p id="mwDSs">Many of his actions and rhetoric have been described as <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Authoritarianism_during_the_Trump_presidencies" title="Authoritarianism during the Trump presidencies" id="mwDSw">authoritarian</a> and contributing to <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Democratic_backsliding_in_the_United_States" title="Democratic backsliding in the United States" id="mwDS0">democratic backsliding</a>,<sup about="#mwt1975" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEParkerTowler2019505;_513_560-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEParkerTowler2019505; 513"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEParkerTowler2019505;_513-560"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Parker"},"2":{"wt":"Towler"},"3":{"wt":"2019"},"p":{"wt":"505; 513"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEParkerTowler2019505;_513-560" id="mwDS4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDS8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDTA">[</span>558<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDTE">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1978" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEKaufmanHaggard2019_561-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEKaufmanHaggard2019"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEKaufmanHaggard2019-561"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Kaufman"},"2":{"wt":"Haggard"},"3":{"wt":"2019"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEKaufmanHaggard2019-561" id="mwDTI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDTM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDTQ">[</span>559<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDTU">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1981" class="mw-ref reference" id='cite_ref-FOOTNOTERoss2024300"Donald_Trump_is_broadly_seen_as_posing_an_existential_threat_to_American_democracy."_562-0' rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTERoss2024300\"Donald Trump is broadly seen as posing an existential threat to American democracy.\""},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTERoss2024300\"Donald_Trump_is_broadly_seen_as_posing_an_existential_threat_to_American_democracy.\"-562"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Ross"},"2":{"wt":"2024"},"p":{"wt":"300"},"loc":{"wt":"\"Donald Trump is broadly seen as posing an existential threat to American democracy.\""}},"i":0}}]}'><a href='#cite_note-FOOTNOTERoss2024300"Donald_Trump_is_broadly_seen_as_posing_an_existential_threat_to_American_democracy."-562' id="mwDTY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDTc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDTg">[</span>560<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDTk">]</span></span></a></sup> as well as establishing an <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/In-group_and_out-group" title="In-group and out-group" id="mwDTo">"us versus them" narrative</a>.<sup about="#mwt1984" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTERoss2024299_563-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTERoss2024299"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTERoss2024299-563"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Ross"},"2":{"wt":"2024"},"p":{"wt":"299"},"3":{"wt":""}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTERoss2024299-563" id="mwDTs"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDTw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDT0">[</span>561<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDT4">]</span></span></a></sup> Trump's rhetoric has been described as using <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fearmongering" title="Fearmongering" id="mwDT8">fearmongering</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Demagogy" title="Demagogy" class="mw-redirect" id="mwDUA">demagogy</a> which <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump_2024_presidential_campaign#Fearmongering" title="Donald Trump 2024 presidential campaign" id="mwDUE">intensified during his 2024 presidential campaign</a>.<sup about="#mwt1992" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-564" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-564"}}'><a href="#cite_note-564" id="mwDUI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDUM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDUQ">[</span>562<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDUU">]</span></span></a></sup> He appeals to <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trumpism#Christian_Trumpism" title="Trumpism" id="mwDUY">evangelical Christians</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Christian_nationalism_in_the_United_States" title="Christian nationalism in the United States" id="mwDUc">Christian nationalists</a>,<sup about="#mwt1987" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEPerryWhiteheadGrubbs2021229Christian_nationalist_ideology_was_among_the_strongest_predictors_of_Americans_voting_for_Trump_in_2016_and_remained_a_strong_predictor_of_intent_to_vote_for_him_prior_to_the_2020_election_565-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEPerryWhiteheadGrubbs2021229Christian nationalist ideology was among the strongest predictors of Americans voting for Trump in 2016 and remained a strong predictor of intent to vote for him prior to the 2020 election"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEPerryWhiteheadGrubbs2021229Christian_nationalist_ideology_was_among_the_strongest_predictors_of_Americans_voting_for_Trump_in_2016_and_remained_a_strong_predictor_of_intent_to_vote_for_him_prior_to_the_2020_election-565"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Perry"},"2":{"wt":"Whitehead"},"3":{"wt":"Grubbs"},"4":{"wt":"2021"},"p":{"wt":"229"},"loc":{"wt":"Christian nationalist ideology was among the strongest predictors of Americans voting for Trump in 2016 and remained a strong predictor of intent to vote for him prior to the 2020 election"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEPerryWhiteheadGrubbs2021229Christian_nationalist_ideology_was_among_the_strongest_predictors_of_Americans_voting_for_Trump_in_2016_and_remained_a_strong_predictor_of_intent_to_vote_for_him_prior_to_the_2020_election-565" id="mwDUg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDUk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDUo">[</span>563<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDUs">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1995" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-566" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-566"}}'><a href="#cite_note-566" id="mwDUw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDU0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDU4">[</span>564<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDU8">]</span></span></a></sup> and regularly calls his political opponents "evil".<sup about="#mwt1998" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-567" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-567"}}'><a href="#cite_note-567" id="mwDVA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDVE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDVI">[</span>565<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDVM">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<section data-mw-section-id="49" id="mwDVQ" aria-labelledby="Race_and_gender"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Race_and_gender">Race and gender</h3></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt1999" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwDVU" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Racial views of Donald Trump"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt2000" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt1999" id="mwDVY">Main article: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Racial_views_of_Donald_Trump" title="Racial views of Donald Trump">Racial views of Donald Trump</a></div>

<p id="mwDVc">Many of Trump's comments and actions have been characterized as racist.<sup about="#mwt2024" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-568" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-568"}}'><a href="#cite_note-568" id="mwDVg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDVk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDVo">[</span>566<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDVs">]</span></span></a></sup> Several studies and surveys found that racist attitudes fueled Trump's political ascent and were more important than economic factors in determining the allegiance of Trump voters.<sup about="#mwt2027" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-569" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-569"}}'><a href="#cite_note-569" id="mwDVw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDV0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDV4">[</span>567<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDV8">]</span></span></a></sup> He explicitly and routinely disparages racial, religious, and ethnic minorities,<sup about="#mwt2001" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEStephens-Dougan2021302_570-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEStephens-Dougan2021302"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEStephens-Dougan2021302-570"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Stephens-Dougan"},"2":{"wt":"2021"},"p":{"wt":"302"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEStephens-Dougan2021302-570" id="mwDWA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDWE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDWI">[</span>568<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDWM">]</span></span></a></sup> and scholars consistently find that racist and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Islamophobic" title="Islamophobic" class="mw-redirect" id="mwDWQ">Islamophobic</a> attitudes regarding Black people, immigrants, and Muslims are strong indicators of support for Trump.<sup about="#mwt2004" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTELajevardiOskooii2018_571-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTELajevardiOskooii2018"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTELajevardiOskooii2018-571"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Lajevardi"},"2":{"wt":"Oskooii"},"3":{"wt":"2018"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTELajevardiOskooii2018-571" id="mwDWU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDWY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDWc">[</span>569<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDWg">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt2007" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBerman202176_572-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBerman202176"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBerman202176-572"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Berman"},"2":{"wt":"2021"},"p":{"wt":"76"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBerman202176-572" id="mwDWk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDWo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDWs">[</span>570<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDWw">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwDW0">Trump has been accused of racism<sup about="#mwt2030" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-573" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-573"}}'><a href="#cite_note-573" id="mwDW4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDW8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDXA">[</span>571<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDXE">]</span></span></a></sup> for running full-page newspaper advertisements and insisting that a group of five Black and Latino teenagers were guilty of raping a white woman in the 1989 <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Central_Park_jogger_case" title="Central Park jogger case" id="mwDXI">Central Park jogger case</a>, even after they were cleared of suspicion in 2002.<sup about="#mwt2033" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-574" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-574"}}'><a href="#cite_note-574" id="mwDXM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDXQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDXU">[</span>572<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDXY">]</span></span></a></sup> Trump's comments on the 2017 <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unite_the_Right_rally" title="Unite the Right rally" id="mwDXc">Unite the Right rally</a>, condemning "this egregious display of hatred, bigotry, and violence on many sides" and stating that there were "very fine people on both sides", were criticized as implying a <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moral_equivalence" title="Moral equivalence" id="mwDXg">moral equivalence</a> between the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_supremacist" title="White supremacist" class="mw-redirect" id="mwDXk">white supremacist</a> demonstrators and the counter-protesters.<sup about="#mwt2036" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-575" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-575"}}'><a href="#cite_note-575" id="mwDXo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDXs"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDXw">[</span>573<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDX0">]</span></span></a></sup> In a January 2018 discussion of immigration legislation, he reportedly referred to El Salvador, Haiti, Honduras, and African nations as "shithole countries",<sup about="#mwt2039" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-576" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-576"}}'><a href="#cite_note-576" id="mwDX4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDX8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDYA">[</span>574<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDYE">]</span></span></a></sup> remarks condemned as racist.<sup about="#mwt2042" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-577" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-577"}}'><a href="#cite_note-577" id="mwDYI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDYM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDYQ">[</span>575<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDYU">]</span></span></a></sup> In July 2019, a House of Representatives <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href='https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Racial_views_of_Donald_Trump#Democratic_congresswomen_should_"go_back"_to_their_countries' title="Racial views of Donald Trump" id="mwDYY">resolution condemned Trump for racist remarks</a> about four minority Democratic congresswomen.<sup about="#mwt2045" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-578" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-578"}}'><a href="#cite_note-578" id="mwDYc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDYg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDYk">[</span>576<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDYo">]</span></span></a></sup> His 2024 campaign made extensive use of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dehumanization" title="Dehumanization" id="mwDYs">dehumanizing language</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Racial_stereotypes" title="Racial stereotypes" class="mw-redirect" id="mwDYw">racial stereotypes</a>.<sup about="#mwt2054" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-579" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-579"}}'><a href="#cite_note-579" id="mwDY0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDY4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDY8">[</span>577<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDZA">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwDZE"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:President_Trump_Visits_St._John's_Episcopal_Church_(49964153176).jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwDZI"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:President_Trump_Visits_St._John's_Episcopal_Church_(49964153176).jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/93/President_Trump_Visits_St._John%27s_Episcopal_Church_%2849964153176%29.jpg/250px-President_Trump_Visits_St._John%27s_Episcopal_Church_%2849964153176%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/93/President_Trump_Visits_St._John%27s_Episcopal_Church_%2849964153176%29.jpg/500px-President_Trump_Visits_St._John%27s_Episcopal_Church_%2849964153176%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="2800" data-file-height="1867" data-file-type="bitmap" height="167" width="250" class="mw-file-element" id="mwDZM"/></a><figcaption id="mwDZQ">With a group of officials and advisors walking from the White House to St. John's Church, following the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump_photo_op_at_St._John's_Church#Clearing_Lafayette_Square_and_St._John's" title="Donald Trump photo op at St. John's Church" id="mwDZU">forced removal of protesters at Lafayette Square</a> in June 2020</figcaption></figure>
<p id="mwDZY">In June 2020, during the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_Floyd_protests" title="George Floyd protests" id="mwDZc">George Floyd protests</a>, federal law-enforcement officials used tear gas and other crowd control tactics to remove a largely peaceful crowd of lawful protesters from <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lafayette_Square,_Washington,_D.C." title="Lafayette Square, Washington, D.C." id="mwDZg">Lafayette Square</a>, outside the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_House" title="White House" id="mwDZk">White House</a>.<sup about="#mwt2057" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-wb_580-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"wb"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-wb-580"}}'><a href="#cite_note-wb-580" id="mwDZo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDZs"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDZw">[</span>578<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDZ0">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt2060" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-bumpline_581-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"bumpline"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-bumpline-581"}}'><a href="#cite_note-bumpline-581" id="mwDZ4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDZ8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDaA">[</span>579<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDaE">]</span></span></a></sup> Trump then <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump_photo_op_at_St._John's_Church" title="Donald Trump photo op at St. John's Church" id="mwDaI">posed with a Bible for a photo op</a> at the nearby <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St._John's_Episcopal_Church,_Lafayette_Square" title="St. John's Episcopal Church, Lafayette Square" class="mw-redirect" id="mwDaM">St. John's Episcopal Church</a>,<sup about="#mwt2061" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-wb_580-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"wb"}}'><a href="#cite_note-wb-580" id="mwDaQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDaU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDaY">[</span>578<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDac">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt2064" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-582" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-582"}}'><a href="#cite_note-582" id="mwDag"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDak"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDao">[</span>580<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDas">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt2067" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-583" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-583"}}'><a href="#cite_note-583" id="mwDaw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDa0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDa4">[</span>581<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDa8">]</span></span></a></sup> with religious leaders condemning both the treatment of protesters and the photo opportunity itself.<sup about="#mwt2070" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-584" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-584"}}'><a href="#cite_note-584" id="mwDbA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDbE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDbI">[</span>582<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDbM">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwDbQ">Trump has a history of belittling women when speaking to the media and on social media.<sup about="#mwt2071" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTERotheCollins2019_585-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTERotheCollins2019"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTERotheCollins2019-585"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Rothe"},"2":{"wt":"Collins"},"3":{"wt":"2019"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTERotheCollins2019-585" id="mwDbU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDbY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDbc">[</span>583<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDbg">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt2076" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Shear_586-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Shear"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Shear-586"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Shear-586" id="mwDbk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDbo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDbs">[</span>584<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDbw">]</span></span></a></sup> He made lewd comments, disparaged women's physical appearances, and referred to them using derogatory epithets.<sup about="#mwt2077" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Shear_586-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Shear"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Shear-586" id="mwDb0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDb4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDb8">[</span>584<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDcA">]</span></span></a></sup> In October 2016, a 2005 "<a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hot_mic" title="Hot mic" id="mwDcE">hot mic</a>" recording surfaced in which <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump_Access_Hollywood_tape" title="Donald Trump Access Hollywood tape" id="mwDcI">he bragged</a> about kissing and groping women without their consent, saying that, "when you're a star, they let you do it. You can do anything... Grab 'em by the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pussy#Female_genitalia" title="Pussy" id="mwDcM">pussy</a>."<sup about="#mwt2080" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-587" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-587"}}'><a href="#cite_note-587" id="mwDcQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDcU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDcY">[</span>585<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDcc">]</span></span></a></sup> He characterized the comments as "locker-room talk".<sup about="#mwt2083" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-588" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-588"}}'><a href="#cite_note-588" id="mwDcg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDck"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDco">[</span>586<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDcs">]</span></span></a></sup> After widespread public outrage, including among Republicans, Trump issued a video with a rare public apology and an attack on Hillary Clinton.<sup about="#mwt2086" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-589" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-589"}}'><a href="#cite_note-589" id="mwDcw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDc0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDc4">[</span>587<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDc8">]</span></span></a></sup></p> 

</section><section data-mw-section-id="50" id="mwDdA" aria-labelledby="Link_to_violence_and_hate_crimes"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Link_to_violence_and_hate_crimes">Link to violence and hate crimes</h3></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt2087" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwDdE" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"further","href":"./Template:Further"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Rhetoric of Donald Trump#Violence"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt2088" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt2087" id="mwDdI">Further information: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rhetoric_of_Donald_Trump#Violence" title="Rhetoric of Donald Trump">Rhetoric of Donald Trump §<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>Violence</a></div>
<p id="mwDdM">Trump has been identified as a key figure in increasing political violence in the U.S., both for and against him.<sup about="#mwt2097" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-590" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-590"}}'><a href="#cite_note-590" id="mwDdQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDdU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDdY">[</span>588<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDdc">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt2089" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTENacosShapiroBloch-Elkon2020_591-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTENacosShapiroBloch-Elkon2020"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTENacosShapiroBloch-Elkon2020-591"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Nacos"},"2":{"wt":"Shapiro"},"3":{"wt":"Bloch-Elkon"},"4":{"wt":"2020"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTENacosShapiroBloch-Elkon2020-591" id="mwDdg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDdk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDdo">[</span>589<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDds">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt2092" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEPiazzaVan_Doren2022_592-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEPiazzaVan Doren2022"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEPiazzaVan_Doren2022-592"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Piazza"},"2":{"wt":"Van Doren"},"3":{"wt":"2022"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEPiazzaVan_Doren2022-592" id="mwDdw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDd0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDd4">[</span>590<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDd8">]</span></span></a></sup> He is described as embracing extremism, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_conspiracy_theories_promoted_by_Donald_Trump" title="List of conspiracy theories promoted by Donald Trump" id="mwDeA">conspiracy theories</a> such as <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/QAnon" title="QAnon" id="mwDeE">QAnon</a>, and far-right <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_militia_movement" title="American militia movement" id="mwDeI">militia movements</a> more than any other modern American president.<sup about="#mwt2100" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-593" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-593"}}'><a href="#cite_note-593" id="mwDeM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDeQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDeU">[</span>591<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDeY">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt2103" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-594" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-594"}}'><a href="#cite_note-594" id="mwDec"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDeg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDek">[</span>592<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDeo">]</span></span></a></sup> He has repeatedly accused his political opponents of treason and called for their imprisonment or execution, leading to accusations of promoting violence.<sup about="#mwt2106" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-595" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-595"}}'><a href="#cite_note-595" id="mwDes"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDew"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDe0">[</span>593<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDe4">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt2109" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-596" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-596"}}'><a href="#cite_note-596" id="mwDe8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDfA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDfE">[</span>594<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDfI">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt2112" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-597" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-597"}}'><a href="#cite_note-597" id="mwDfM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDfQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDfU">[</span>595<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDfY">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwDfc">Research suggests that Trump's rhetoric is associated with an increased incidence of hate crimes,<sup about="#mwt2118" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-598" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-598"}}'><a href="#cite_note-598" id="mwDfg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDfk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDfo">[</span>596<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDfs">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt2121" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-599" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-599"}}'><a href="#cite_note-599" id="mwDfw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDf0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDf4">[</span>597<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDf8">]</span></span></a></sup> and that he has an emboldening effect on expressing prejudicial attitudes due to his normalization of explicit racial rhetoric.<sup about="#mwt2113" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEStephens-Dougan2021306_600-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEStephens-Dougan2021306"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEStephens-Dougan2021306-600"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Stephens-Dougan"},"2":{"wt":"2021"},"p":{"wt":"306"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEStephens-Dougan2021306-600" id="mwDgA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDgE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDgI">[</span>598<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDgM">]</span></span></a></sup> Researchers have also argued that Trump's "negative characterisations of, and false claims made about, those who became the targets" of the mob at the January<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwDgQ"> </span>6 riot was a case of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stochastic_terrorism" title="Stochastic terrorism" id="mwDgU">stochastic terrorism</a>.<sup about="#mwt2124" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-601" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-601"}}'><a href="#cite_note-601" id="mwDgY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDgc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDgg">[</span>599<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDgk">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt2127" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-602" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-602"}}'><a href="#cite_note-602" id="mwDgo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDgs"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDgw">[</span>600<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDg0">]</span></span></a></sup> Numerous defendants investigated or prosecuted for violent acts and hate crimes cited his rhetoric in arguing that they were not culpable or deserved leniency.<sup about="#mwt2130" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-603" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-603"}}'><a href="#cite_note-603" id="mwDg4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDg8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDhA">[</span>601<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDhE">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt2133" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-604" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-604"}}'><a href="#cite_note-604" id="mwDhI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDhM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDhQ">[</span>602<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDhU">]</span></span></a></sup> A nationwide review by <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ABC_News_(United_States)" title="ABC News (United States)" id="mwDhY">ABC News</a> in May 2020 identified at least 54 criminal cases, from August 2015 to April 2020, in which he was invoked in direct connection to violence or threats of violence against predominantly minorities, with the perpetrators involved being primarily white men.<sup about="#mwt2136" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-605" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-605"}}'><a href="#cite_note-605" id="mwDhc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDhg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDhk">[</span>603<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDho">]</span></span></a></sup> Trump's refusal to condemn the white supremacist <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Proud_Boys" title="Proud Boys" id="mwDhs">Proud Boys</a> during a 2020 presidential debate<sup about="#mwt2139" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-606" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-606"}}'><a href="#cite_note-606" id="mwDhw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDh0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDh4">[</span>604<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDh8">]</span></span></a></sup> and his comment, "Proud Boys, stand back and stand by", were said to have led to increased recruitment for the pro-Trump group.<sup about="#mwt2142" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-607" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-607"}}'><a href="#cite_note-607" id="mwDiA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDiE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDiI">[</span>605<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDiM">]</span></span></a></sup> Counterterrorism researchers described his normalization and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Historical_revisionism" title="Historical revisionism" id="mwDiQ">revisionist history</a> of the January<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwDiU"> </span>6 Capitol attack and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pardon_of_January_6_United_States_Capitol_attack_defendants" title="Pardon of January 6 United States Capitol attack defendants" id="mwDiY">grant of clemency to all January<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwDic"> </span>6 rioters</a> as encouraging future political violence.<sup about="#mwt2145" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-608" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-608"}}'><a href="#cite_note-608" id="mwDig"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDik"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDio">[</span>606<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDis">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt2148" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-609" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-609"}}'><a href="#cite_note-609" id="mwDiw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDi0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDi4">[</span>607<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDi8">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="51" id="mwDjA" aria-labelledby="False_or_misleading_statements_and_conspiracy_theories"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="False_or_misleading_statements_and_conspiracy_theories">False or misleading statements and conspiracy theories</h3></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt2149" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwDjE" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"False or misleading statements by Donald Trump"},"2":{"wt":"List of conspiracy theories promoted by Donald Trump"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt2150" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt2149" id="mwDjI">Main articles: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/False_or_misleading_statements_by_Donald_Trump" title="False or misleading statements by Donald Trump">False or misleading statements by Donald Trump</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_conspiracy_theories_promoted_by_Donald_Trump" title="List of conspiracy theories promoted by Donald Trump">List of conspiracy theories promoted by Donald Trump</a></div>

<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwDjM"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:2017-_Donald_Trump_veracity_-_composite_graph.png" class="mw-file-description" id="mwDjQ"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:2017-_Donald_Trump_veracity_-_composite_graph.png" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/2017-_Donald_Trump_veracity_-_composite_graph.png/500px-2017-_Donald_Trump_veracity_-_composite_graph.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/2017-_Donald_Trump_veracity_-_composite_graph.png/960px-2017-_Donald_Trump_veracity_-_composite_graph.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" alt="Chart depicting false or misleading claims made by Trump" data-file-width="2775" data-file-height="1750" data-file-type="bitmap" height="240" width="380" class="mw-file-element mw-file-upright" style="--mw-file-upright: 1.5" id="mwDjU"/></a><figcaption id="mwDjY"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fact-checkers" title="Fact-checkers" class="mw-redirect" id="mwDjc">Fact-checkers</a> from <i id="mwDjg">The Washington Post</i>,<sup about="#mwt2153" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-database_610-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"database"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-database-610"}}'><a href="#cite_note-database-610" id="mwDjk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDjo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDjs">[</span>608<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDjw">]</span></span></a></sup> the <i id="mwDj0">Toronto Star</i>,<sup about="#mwt2156" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-611" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-611"}}'><a href="#cite_note-611" id="mwDj4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDj8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDkA">[</span>609<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDkE">]</span></span></a></sup> and CNN<sup about="#mwt2159" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-612" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-612"}}'><a href="#cite_note-612" id="mwDkI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDkM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDkQ">[</span>610<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDkU">]</span></span></a></sup> compiled data on "false or misleading claims" (orange background) and "false claims" (violet foreground).</figcaption></figure>

<p id="mwDkY">Trump frequently makes false statements in public remarks,<sup about="#mwt2165" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-finnegan_613-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"finnegan"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-finnegan-613"}}'><a href="#cite_note-finnegan-613" id="mwDkc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDkg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDkk">[</span>611<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDko">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt2166" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-whoppers_138-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"whoppers"}}'><a href="#cite_note-whoppers-138" id="mwDks"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDkw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDk0">[</span>138<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDk4">]</span></span></a></sup> to an extent unprecedented in American politics.<sup about="#mwt2167" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-finnegan_613-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"finnegan"}}'><a href="#cite_note-finnegan-613" id="mwDk8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDlA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDlE">[</span>611<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDlI">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt2170" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-glasser_614-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"glasser"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-glasser-614"}}'><a href="#cite_note-glasser-614" id="mwDlM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDlQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDlU">[</span>612<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDlY">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt2173" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Konnikova_615-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Konnikova"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Konnikova-615"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Konnikova-615" id="mwDlc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDlg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDlk">[</span>613<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDlo">]</span></span></a></sup> His false and misleading statements were documented by <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fact-checker" title="Fact-checker" class="mw-redirect" id="mwDls">fact-checkers</a>, including at <i id="mwDlw">The Washington Post</i>, which tallied 30,573 false or misleading statements made by him during his first presidency,<sup about="#mwt2174" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-database_610-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"database"}}'><a href="#cite_note-database-610" id="mwDl0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDl4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDl8">[</span>608<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDmA">]</span></span></a></sup> increasing in frequency over time.<sup about="#mwt2177" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-TermUntruth_616-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"TermUntruth"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-TermUntruth-616"}}'><a href="#cite_note-TermUntruth-616" id="mwDmE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDmI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDmM">[</span>614<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDmQ">]</span></span></a></sup> His falsehoods are a distinctive part of his political identity<sup about="#mwt2178" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-glasser_614-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"glasser"}}'><a href="#cite_note-glasser-614" id="mwDmU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDmY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDmc">[</span>612<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDmg">]</span></span></a></sup> and have been described as <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Firehosing" title="Firehosing" class="mw-redirect" id="mwDmk">firehosing</a>.<sup about="#mwt2160" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEKakutani201894–104_617-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEKakutani201894–104"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEKakutani201894–104-617"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Kakutani"},"2":{"wt":"2018"},"pp":{"wt":"94–104"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEKakutani201894–104-617" id="mwDmo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDms"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDmw">[</span>615<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDm0">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwDm4">During his second term, Trump relied on false, misleading, and exaggerated claims to justify his executive actions and policies.<sup about="#mwt2181" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-618" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-618"}}'><a href="#cite_note-618" id="mwDm8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDnA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDnE">[</span>616<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDnI">]</span></span></a></sup> He continued to make untrue statements as frequently as before but with less variety while increasing the frequency of repeating a "core set" of falsehoods.<sup about="#mwt2184" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-619" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-619"}}'><a href="#cite_note-619" id="mwDnM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDnQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDnU">[</span>617<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDnY">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwDnc">Some of Trump's falsehoods were inconsequential,<sup about="#mwt2187" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-620" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-620"}}'><a href="#cite_note-620" id="mwDng"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDnk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDno">[</span>618<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDns">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt2190" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-621" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-621"}}'><a href="#cite_note-621" id="mwDnw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDn0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDn4">[</span>619<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDn8">]</span></span></a></sup> while others had more far-reaching effects, such as his unproven promotion of antimalarial drugs as a treatment for COVID-19,<sup about="#mwt2193" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-622" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-622"}}'><a href="#cite_note-622" id="mwDoA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDoE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDoI">[</span>620<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDoM">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt2196" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-623" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-623"}}'><a href="#cite_note-623" id="mwDoQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDoU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDoY">[</span>621<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDoc">]</span></span></a></sup> contributing to a U.S. shortage of these drugs and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Panic_buying" title="Panic buying" id="mwDog">panic buying</a> in Africa and South Asia.<sup about="#mwt2199" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-624" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-624"}}'><a href="#cite_note-624" id="mwDok"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDoo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDos">[</span>622<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDow">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt2202" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-625" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-625"}}'><a href="#cite_note-625" id="mwDo0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDo4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDo8">[</span>623<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDpA">]</span></span></a></sup> His attacks on mail-in ballots and other election practices weakened public faith in the integrity of the 2020 presidential election,<sup about="#mwt2205" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-626" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-626"}}'><a href="#cite_note-626" id="mwDpE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDpI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDpM">[</span>624<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDpQ">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt2208" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-627" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-627"}}'><a href="#cite_note-627" id="mwDpU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDpY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDpc">[</span>625<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDpg">]</span></span></a></sup> while his disinformation about the COVID-19 pandemic delayed and weakened the national response to it.<sup about="#mwt2211" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-NYT_4_11_20_628-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"NYT 4 11 20"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-NYT_4_11_20-628"}}'><a href="#cite_note-NYT_4_11_20-628" id="mwDpk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDpo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDps">[</span>626<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDpw">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt2214" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-USAT-Disinfo_629-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"USAT-Disinfo"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-USAT-Disinfo-629"}}'><a href="#cite_note-USAT-Disinfo-629" id="mwDp0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDp4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDp8">[</span>627<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDqA">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt2217" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-630" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-630"}}'><a href="#cite_note-630" id="mwDqE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDqI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDqM">[</span>628<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDqQ">]</span></span></a></sup> He habitually does not apologize for his falsehoods.<sup about="#mwt2220" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-631" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-631"}}'><a href="#cite_note-631" id="mwDqU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDqY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDqc">[</span>629<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDqg">]</span></span></a></sup> Until 2018, the media rarely referred to his falsehoods as lies, including when he repeated demonstrably false statements.<sup about="#mwt2223" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-632" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-632"}}'><a href="#cite_note-632" id="mwDqk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDqo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDqs">[</span>630<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDqw">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt2226" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-DBauder_633-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"DBauder"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-DBauder-633"}}'><a href="#cite_note-DBauder-633" id="mwDq0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDq4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDq8">[</span>631<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDrA">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt2229" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-634" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-634"}}'><a href="#cite_note-634" id="mwDrE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDrI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDrM">[</span>632<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDrQ">]</span></span></a></sup></p> 

<p id="mwDrU">Since before his first presidency, Trump has <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_conspiracy_theories_promoted_by_Donald_Trump" title="List of conspiracy theories promoted by Donald Trump" id="mwDrY">promoted numerous conspiracy theories</a>. In 2011, Trump became the leading proponent of the racist <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barack_Obama_citizenship_conspiracy_theories" title="Barack Obama citizenship conspiracy theories" id="mwDrc">"birther" conspiracy theory</a> that <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barack_Obama" title="Barack Obama" id="mwDrg">Barack Obama</a>, the first Black U.S. president, was not born in the United States,<sup about="#mwt2232" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-635" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-635"}}'><a href="#cite_note-635" id="mwDrk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDro"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDrs">[</span>633<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDrw">]</span></span></a></sup> and he claimed credit for pressuring the government to publish Obama's birth certificate, which he considered fraudulent.<sup about="#mwt2235" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-636" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-636"}}'><a href="#cite_note-636" id="mwDr0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDr4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDr8">[</span>634<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDsA">]</span></span></a></sup> In September 2016, he publicly acknowledged Barack Obama's birthplace and falsely claimed that the rumors had been started by <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hillary_Clinton" title="Hillary Clinton" id="mwDsE">Hillary Clinton</a> during her <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hillary_Clinton_2008_presidential_campaign" title="Hillary Clinton 2008 presidential campaign" id="mwDsI">2008 presidential campaign</a>.<!-- DO NOT CHANGE preceding sentence without prior consensus; see [[Talk:Donald Trump#C27|consensus 27]]. --><sup about="#mwt2238" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-637" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-637"}}'><a href="#cite_note-637" id="mwDsM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDsQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDsU">[</span>635<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDsY">]</span></span></a></sup> Trump often prefaced his conspiracies by claiming he heard them from someone else,<sup about="#mwt2239" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Haberman2016_638-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Haberman2016"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Haberman2016-638" id="mwDsc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDsg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDsk">[</span>636<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDso">]</span></span></a></sup> and promoted conspiracies such as <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Climate_change_denial" title="Climate change denial" id="mwDss">climate change denial</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conspiracy_theories_related_to_the_2019_Trump–Ukraine_scandal" title="Conspiracy theories related to the 2019 Trump–Ukraine scandal" id="mwDsw">alleged Ukrainian interference in U.S. elections</a>, and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vaccines_and_autism" title="Vaccines and autism" id="mwDs0">vaccines and autism</a>.<sup about="#mwt2242" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Haberman2016_638-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Haberman2016"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Haberman2016-638"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Haberman2016-638" id="mwDs4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDs8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDtA">[</span>636<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDtE">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt2245" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-639" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-639"}}'><a href="#cite_note-639" id="mwDtI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDtM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDtQ">[</span>637<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDtU">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt2248" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-640" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-640"}}'><a href="#cite_note-640" id="mwDtY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDtc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDtg">[</span>638<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDtk">]</span></span></a></sup> After the 2020 presidential election, he promoted conspiracy theories for his defeat that were characterized as "<a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Big_lie_(2020_United_States_presidential_election)" title="Big lie (2020 United States presidential election)" class="mw-redirect" id="mwDto">the big lie</a>".<sup about="#mwt2251" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-641" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-641"}}'><a href="#cite_note-641" id="mwDts"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDtw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDt0">[</span>639<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDt4">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt2254" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-642" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-642"}}'><a href="#cite_note-642" id="mwDt8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDuA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDuE">[</span>640<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDuI">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="52" id="mwDuM" aria-labelledby="Relationship_with_news_media"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Relationship_with_news_media">Relationship with news media</h3></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt2255" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwDuQ" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Donald Trump&apos;s conflict with the news media"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt2256" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt2255" id="mwDuU">Main article: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump's_conflict_with_the_news_media" title="Donald Trump's conflict with the news media">Donald Trump's conflict with the news media</a></div>

<p id="mwDuY">In the 2016 campaign, Trump benefited from a record amount of free media coverage,<sup about="#mwt2259" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Cillizza-160614_643-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Cillizza-160614"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Cillizza-160614-643"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Cillizza-160614-643" id="mwDuc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDug"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDuk">[</span>641<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDuo">]</span></span></a></sup> estimated at $2<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwDus"> </span>billion.<sup about="#mwt2262" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-644" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-644"}}'><a href="#cite_note-644" id="mwDuw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDu0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDu4">[</span>642<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDu8">]</span></span></a></sup> As a candidate and as president, he frequently accused the press of bias, calling it the "fake news media" and "the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Enemy_of_the_people" title="Enemy of the people" id="mwDvA">enemy of the people</a>".<sup about="#mwt2265" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-645" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-645"}}'><a href="#cite_note-645" id="mwDvE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDvI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDvM">[</span>643<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDvQ">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwDvU">The first Trump presidency reduced formal press briefings from about one hundred in 2017 to two by 2019 and revoked the press passes of two White House reporters, which were restored by the courts.<sup about="#mwt2268" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-646" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-646"}}'><a href="#cite_note-646" id="mwDvY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDvc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDvg">[</span>644<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDvk">]</span></span></a></sup> By 2024, Trump repeatedly voiced support for <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Criminalization_of_dissent" title="Criminalization of dissent" class="mw-redirect" id="mwDvo">outlawing political dissent</a> and criticism,<sup about="#mwt2271" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-647" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-647"}}'><a href="#cite_note-647" id="mwDvs"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDvw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDv0">[</span>645<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDv4">]</span></span></a></sup> and said that reporters should be prosecuted for not divulging confidential sources and media companies should possibly lose their broadcast licenses for unfavorable coverage of him.<sup about="#mwt2274" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-648" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-648"}}'><a href="#cite_note-648" id="mwDv8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDwA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDwE">[</span>646<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDwI">]</span></span></a></sup></p> 

<p id="mwDwM">In his second term, Trump's actions against the media were unprecedented in modern American history,<sup about="#mwt2277" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-649" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-649"}}'><a href="#cite_note-649" id="mwDwQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDwU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDwY">[</span>647<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDwc">]</span></span></a></sup> and historians described them as mirroring actions by authoritarian leaders to censor political opponents and negatively impacting the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Freedom_of_speech" title="Freedom of speech" id="mwDwg">freedom of speech</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Free_press" title="Free press" class="mw-redirect" id="mwDwk">free press</a>.<sup about="#mwt2280" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-650" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-650"}}'><a href="#cite_note-650" id="mwDwo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDws"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDww">[</span>648<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDw0">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt2283" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-651" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-651"}}'><a href="#cite_note-651" id="mwDw4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDw8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDxA">[</span>649<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDxE">]</span></span></a></sup> The campaign to police speech drew comparisons to <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cancel_culture" title="Cancel culture" id="mwDxI">cancel culture</a>, government censorship, and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/McCarthyism" title="McCarthyism" id="mwDxM">McCarthyism</a>.<sup about="#mwt2286" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-652" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-652"}}'><a href="#cite_note-652" id="mwDxQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDxU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDxY">[</span>650<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDxc">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt2289" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-653" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-653"}}'><a href="#cite_note-653" id="mwDxg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDxk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDxo">[</span>651<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDxs">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt2292" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-654" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-654"}}'><a href="#cite_note-654" id="mwDxw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDx0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDx4">[</span>652<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDx8">]</span></span></a></sup> The <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Federal_Communications_Commission" title="Federal Communications Commission" id="mwDyA">Federal Communications Commission</a> launched investigations into media outlets accused of bias against him.<sup about="#mwt2295" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-655" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-655"}}'><a href="#cite_note-655" id="mwDyE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDyI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDyM">[</span>653<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDyQ">]</span></span></a></sup> As a result of Trump's threats, media executives instructed journalists and their staff to <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Self-censor" title="Self-censor" class="mw-redirect" id="mwDyU">self-censor</a> and reduce criticism of Trump.<sup about="#mwt2298" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-656" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-656"}}'><a href="#cite_note-656" id="mwDyY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDyc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDyg">[</span>654<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDyk">]</span></span></a></sup> Many have characterized Trump as causing a significant decline in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Freedom_of_the_press" title="Freedom of the press" id="mwDyo">freedom of the press</a>, including journalist advocacy groups<sup about="#mwt2301" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FerragamoCFR2025_657-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"FerragamoCFR2025"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FerragamoCFR2025-657"}}'><a href="#cite_note-FerragamoCFR2025-657" id="mwDys"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDyw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDy0">[</span>655<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDy4">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt2304" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-RSFUnitedStates2025_658-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"RSFUnitedStates2025"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-RSFUnitedStates2025-658"}}'><a href="#cite_note-RSFUnitedStates2025-658" id="mwDy8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDzA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDzE">[</span>656<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDzI">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt2307" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-CPJPressFreedomUS_659-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"CPJPressFreedomUS"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-CPJPressFreedomUS-659"}}'><a href="#cite_note-CPJPressFreedomUS-659" id="mwDzM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDzQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDzU">[</span>657<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDzY">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt2310" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-660" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-660"}}'><a href="#cite_note-660" id="mwDzc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDzg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDzk">[</span>658<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDzo">]</span></span></a></sup> and academic sources.<sup about="#mwt2313" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-661" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-661"}}'><a href="#cite_note-661" id="mwDzs"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDzw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDz0">[</span>659<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDz4">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt2316" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-662" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-662"}}'><a href="#cite_note-662" id="mwDz8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwD0A"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwD0E">[</span>660<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwD0I">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt2319" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-663" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-663"}}'><a href="#cite_note-663" id="mwD0M"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwD0Q"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwD0U">[</span>661<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwD0Y">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="53" id="mwD0c" aria-labelledby="Social_media"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Social_media">Social media</h3></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt2320" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwD0g" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Social media use by Donald Trump"},"2":{"wt":"Use of X by Donald Trump"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt2321" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt2320" id="mwD0k">Main articles: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Social_media_use_by_Donald_Trump" title="Social media use by Donald Trump">Social media use by Donald Trump</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Use_of_X_by_Donald_Trump" title="Use of X by Donald Trump">Use of X by Donald Trump</a></div>

<p id="mwD0o">From 2009 until he was banned in January 2021, Trump posted on Twitter more than 57,000 times.<sup about="#mwt2324" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-gone_664-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"gone"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-gone-664"}}'><a href="#cite_note-gone-664" id="mwD0s"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwD0w"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwD00">[</span>662<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwD04">]</span></span></a></sup> After years of criticism for allowing Trump to post misinformation and falsehoods, Twitter began to tag some of his tweets with fact-checks in May 2020.<sup about="#mwt2327" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-665" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-665"}}'><a href="#cite_note-665" id="mwD08"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwD1A"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwD1E">[</span>663<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwD1I">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt2328" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-gone_664-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"gone"}}'><a href="#cite_note-gone-664" id="mwD1M"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwD1Q"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwD1U">[</span>662<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwD1Y">]</span></span></a></sup> He was banned from Facebook, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Instagram" title="Instagram" id="mwD1c">Instagram</a>, Twitter, and other platforms after the January<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwD1g"> </span>6 attack.<sup about="#mwt2331" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-666" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-666"}}'><a href="#cite_note-666" id="mwD1k"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwD1o"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwD1s">[</span>664<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwD1w">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt2334" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-667" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-667"}}'><a href="#cite_note-667" id="mwD10"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwD14"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwD18">[</span>665<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwD2A">]</span></span></a></sup> The loss of his social media presence diminished his ability to shape events<sup about="#mwt2337" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-668" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-668"}}'><a href="#cite_note-668" id="mwD2E"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwD2I"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwD2M">[</span>666<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwD2Q">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt2340" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-669" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-669"}}'><a href="#cite_note-669" id="mwD2U"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwD2Y"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwD2c">[</span>667<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwD2g">]</span></span></a></sup> and correlated with a dramatic decrease in the volume of misinformation on Twitter.<sup about="#mwt2343" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-670" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-670"}}'><a href="#cite_note-670" id="mwD2k"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwD2o"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwD2s">[</span>668<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwD2w">]</span></span></a></sup> In February 2022, he launched social media platform <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Truth_Social" title="Truth Social" id="mwD20">Truth Social</a>, where he attracted a fraction of his Twitter following.<sup about="#mwt2346" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-671" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-671"}}'><a href="#cite_note-671" id="mwD24"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwD28"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwD3A">[</span>669<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwD3E">]</span></span></a></sup> Twitter reinstated his account in November 2022.<sup about="#mwt2349" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-672" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-672"}}'><a href="#cite_note-672" id="mwD3I"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwD3M"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwD3Q">[</span>670<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwD3U">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt2352" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-673" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-673"}}'><a href="#cite_note-673" id="mwD3Y"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwD3c"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwD3g">[</span>671<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwD3k">]</span></span></a></sup> The two-year ban at <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meta_Platforms" title="Meta Platforms" id="mwD3o">Meta Platforms</a> lapsed in January 2023, allowing him to return to Facebook and Instagram.<sup about="#mwt2355" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-674" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-674"}}'><a href="#cite_note-674" id="mwD3s"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwD3w"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwD30">[</span>672<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwD34">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section></section><section data-mw-section-id="54" id="mwD38" aria-labelledby="Assessments"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id="Assessments">Assessments</h2></div>

<section data-mw-section-id="55" id="mwD4A" aria-labelledby="Public_image"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Public_image">Public image</h3></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt2356" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwD4E" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Public image of Donald Trump"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt2357" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt2356" id="mwD4I">Main article: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Public_image_of_Donald_Trump" title="Public image of Donald Trump">Public image of Donald Trump</a></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt2358" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwD4M" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"further info","href":"./Template:Further_info"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Opinion polling on the first Trump presidency{{!}}Opinion polling on the first"},"2":{"wt":"Opinion polling on the second Trump presidency{{!}}second Trump presidencies"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt2359" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt2358" id="mwD4Q">Further information: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Opinion_polling_on_the_first_Trump_presidency" title="Opinion polling on the first Trump presidency">Opinion polling on the first</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Opinion_polling_on_the_second_Trump_presidency" title="Opinion polling on the second Trump presidency">second Trump presidencies</a></div>
<p id="mwD4U">In Trump's first term, from 2017 to 2021, international approval ratings of U.S. leadership dropped from about 22<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwD4Y"> </span>percent in a <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gallup,_Inc." title="Gallup, Inc." id="mwD4c">Gallup</a> poll<sup about="#mwt2362" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-675" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-675"}}'><a href="#cite_note-675" id="mwD4g"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwD4k"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwD4o">[</span>673<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwD4s">]</span></span></a></sup> of 134 countries to 16<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwD4w"> </span>percent in a <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pew_Research" title="Pew Research" class="mw-redirect" id="mwD40">Pew Research</a> poll<sup about="#mwt2365" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-676" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-676"}}'><a href="#cite_note-676" id="mwD44"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwD48"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwD5A">[</span>674<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwD5E">]</span></span></a></sup> of 13 countries. In 2017, estimation of U.S. leadership declined most among allies.<sup about="#mwt2368" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-677" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-677"}}'><a href="#cite_note-677" id="mwD5I"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwD5M"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwD5Q">[</span>675<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwD5U">]</span></span></a></sup> Domestically, Trump had chiefly partisan support: 88<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwD5Y"> </span>percent among Republicans and 7<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwD5c"> </span>percent among Democrats.<sup about="#mwt2371" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Jones_678-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Jones"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Jones-678"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Jones-678" id="mwD5g"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwD5k"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwD5o">[</span>676<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwD5s">]</span></span></a></sup> In a 2021 Gallup poll, he was the only president never to reach a 50<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwD5w"> </span>percent approval rating, and he was the first not to be named most admired in his first year in office.<sup about="#mwt2374" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-679" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-679"}}'><a href="#cite_note-679" id="mwD50"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwD54"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwD58">[</span>677<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwD6A">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="56" id="mwD6E" aria-labelledby="Scholarly_rankings"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Scholarly_rankings">Scholarly rankings</h3></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt2375" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwD6I" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"further","href":"./Template:Further"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Historical rankings of presidents of the United States"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt2376" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt2375" id="mwD6M">Further information: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Historical_rankings_of_presidents_of_the_United_States" title="Historical rankings of presidents of the United States">Historical rankings of presidents of the United States</a></div>
<p id="mwD6Q">After Trump's first term, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Historical_rankings_of_presidents_of_the_United_States#2021_C-SPAN" title="Historical rankings of presidents of the United States" id="mwD6U">historians ranked Trump</a> as the fourth-worst president in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/C-SPAN" title="C-SPAN" id="mwD6Y">C-SPAN</a>'s 2021 survey of presidential historians.<sup about="#mwt2379" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-680" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-680"}}'><a href="#cite_note-680" id="mwD6c"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwD6g"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwD6k">[</span>678<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwD6o">]</span></span></a></sup> He rated lowest in the leadership characteristics categories for moral authority and administrative skills.<sup about="#mwt2382" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-681" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-681"}}'><a href="#cite_note-681" id="mwD6s"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwD6w"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwD60">[</span>679<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwD64">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt2385" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-682" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-682"}}'><a href="#cite_note-682" id="mwD68"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwD7A"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwD7E">[</span>680<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwD7I">]</span></span></a></sup> The <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Siena_College_Research_Institute" title="Siena College Research Institute" class="mw-redirect" id="mwD7M">Siena College Research Institute</a>'s 2022 survey <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Historical_rankings_of_presidents_of_the_United_States#2022_Siena_College" title="Historical rankings of presidents of the United States" id="mwD7Q">ranked him</a> third-worst. He was ranked near the bottom in all categories except for luck, willingness to take risks, and party leadership, and ranked last in several categories.<sup about="#mwt2388" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-scri_22_683-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"scri_22"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-scri_22-683"}}'><a href="#cite_note-scri_22-683" id="mwD7U"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwD7Y"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwD7c">[</span>681<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwD7g">]</span></span></a></sup> In 2018 and 2024, the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_Political_Science_Association" title="American Political Science Association" id="mwD7k">American Political Science Association</a> ranked him the worst president.<sup about="#mwt2391" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-684" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-684"}}'><a href="#cite_note-684" id="mwD7o"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwD7s"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwD7w">[</span>682<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwD70">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt2394" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-685" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-685"}}'><a href="#cite_note-685" id="mwD74"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwD78"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwD8A">[</span>683<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwD8E">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section></section><section data-mw-section-id="57" id="mwD8I" aria-labelledby="Personal_life"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id="Personal_life">Personal life</h2></div>
<section data-mw-section-id="58" id="mwD8M" aria-labelledby="Family"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Family">Family</h3></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt2395" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwD8Q" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Trump family"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt2396" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt2395" id="mwD8U">Main article: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trump_family" title="Trump family">Trump family</a></div>
<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwD8Y"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:State_Visit_of_King_Fahd_of_Saudi_Arabia_State_Dinner_Receiving_Line_with_Ivana_Trump_and_Donald_Trump_in_East_Room_-_DPLA_-_1a44d05819eab8ba3dcf7a6883ad92ba.jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwD8c"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:State_Visit_of_King_Fahd_of_Saudi_Arabia_State_Dinner_Receiving_Line_with_Ivana_Trump_and_Donald_Trump_in_East_Room_-_DPLA_-_1a44d05819eab8ba3dcf7a6883ad92ba.jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/State_Visit_of_King_Fahd_of_Saudi_Arabia_State_Dinner_Receiving_Line_with_Ivana_Trump_and_Donald_Trump_in_East_Room_-_DPLA_-_1a44d05819eab8ba3dcf7a6883ad92ba.jpg/250px-thumbnail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/State_Visit_of_King_Fahd_of_Saudi_Arabia_State_Dinner_Receiving_Line_with_Ivana_Trump_and_Donald_Trump_in_East_Room_-_DPLA_-_1a44d05819eab8ba3dcf7a6883ad92ba.jpg/500px-thumbnail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" alt="Ivana Trump and King Fahd shake hands, with Ronald Reagan standing next to them smiling" data-file-width="4000" data-file-height="2885" data-file-type="bitmap" height="180" width="250" class="mw-file-element" id="mwD8g"/></a><figcaption id="mwD8k">Trump and wife Ivana at 1985 state dinner for King <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fahd_of_Saudi_Arabia" title="Fahd of Saudi Arabia" id="mwD8o">Fahd of Saudi Arabia</a> with President <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ronald_Reagan" title="Ronald Reagan" id="mwD8s">Ronald Reagan</a> and First Lady <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nancy_Reagan" title="Nancy Reagan" id="mwD8w">Nancy Reagan</a></figcaption></figure>
<p id="mwD80">In 1977, Trump married <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ivana_Zelníčková" title="Ivana Zelníčková" class="mw-redirect" id="mwD84">Ivana Zelníčková</a>.<sup about="#mwt2397" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBlair2015300_686-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEBlair2015300"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEBlair2015300-686"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Blair"},"2":{"wt":"2015"},"p":{"wt":"300"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBlair2015300-686" id="mwD88"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwD9A"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwD9E">[</span>684<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwD9I">]</span></span></a></sup> They had three children: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Jr." title="Donald Jr." class="mw-redirect" id="mwD9M">Donald Jr.</a> (b. 1977), <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ivanka_Trump" title="Ivanka Trump" id="mwD9Q">Ivanka</a> (b. 1981), and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eric_Trump" title="Eric Trump" id="mwD9U">Eric</a> (b. 1984). Ivana played a crucial role in generating the "fawning press coverage" that shaped Trump's public image as a real estate mogul.<sup about="#mwt2405" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-687" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-687"}}'><a href="#cite_note-687" id="mwD9Y"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwD9c"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwD9g">[</span>685<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwD9k">]</span></span></a></sup> The couple divorced in 1990, following his affair with model and actress <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marla_Maples" title="Marla Maples" id="mwD9o">Marla Maples</a>.<sup about="#mwt2408" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-688" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-688"}}'><a href="#cite_note-688" id="mwD9s"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwD9w"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwD90">[</span>686<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwD94">]</span></span></a></sup> He and Maples married in 1993 and had one daughter, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tiffany_Trump" title="Tiffany Trump" id="mwD98">Tiffany</a> (b. 1993), whom Maples raised in California. They divorced in 1999.<sup about="#mwt2411" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-689" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-689"}}'><a href="#cite_note-689" id="mwD-A"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwD-E"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwD-I">[</span>687<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwD-M">]</span></span></a></sup> In 2005, he married Slovenian model <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Melania_Knauss" title="Melania Knauss" class="mw-redirect" id="mwD-Q">Melania Knauss</a>.<sup about="#mwt2400" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher2017266_690-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEKranishFisher2017266"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher2017266-690"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Kranish"},"2":{"wt":"Fisher"},"3":{"wt":"2017"},"p":{"wt":"266"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher2017266-690" id="mwD-U"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwD-Y"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwD-c">[</span>688<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwD-g">]</span></span></a></sup> Their son <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barron_Trump" title="Barron Trump" id="mwD-k">Barron</a> was born in 2006.<sup about="#mwt2414" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-691" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-691"}}'><a href="#cite_note-691" id="mwD-o"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwD-s"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwD-w">[</span>689<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwD-0">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="59" id="mwD-4" aria-labelledby="Sexual_misconduct_allegations_and_E._Jean_Carroll_lawsuits"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Sexual_misconduct_allegations_and_E._Jean_Carroll_lawsuits">Sexual misconduct allegations and E. Jean Carroll lawsuits</h3></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt2415" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwD-8" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Donald Trump sexual misconduct allegations"},"2":{"wt":"E. Jean Carroll v. Donald J. Trump"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt2416" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt2415" id="mwD_A">Main articles: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump_sexual_misconduct_allegations" title="Donald Trump sexual misconduct allegations">Donald Trump sexual misconduct allegations</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/E._Jean_Carroll_v._Donald_J._Trump" title="E. Jean Carroll v. Donald J. Trump">E. Jean Carroll v. Donald J. Trump</a></div>

<p id="mwD_E">In 2019, journalist <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/E._Jean_Carroll" title="E. Jean Carroll" id="mwD_I">E. Jean Carroll</a> accused Trump of raping her in the 1990s and sued him for defamation over his denial.<sup about="#mwt2419" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-692" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-692"}}'><a href="#cite_note-692" id="mwD_M"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwD_Q"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwD_U">[</span>690<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwD_Y">]</span></span></a></sup> Carroll sued him again in 2022 for <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battery_(crime)" title="Battery (crime)" id="mwD_c">battery</a> and further defamation.<sup about="#mwt2422" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-693" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-693"}}'><a href="#cite_note-693" id="mwD_g"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwD_k"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwD_o">[</span>691<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwD_s">]</span></span></a></sup> He was <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Legal_liability" title="Legal liability" id="mwD_w">found liable</a> for sexual abuse and defamation and ordered to pay $5<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwD_0"> </span>million in one case<sup about="#mwt2425" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-694" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-694"}}'><a href="#cite_note-694" id="mwD_4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwD_8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEAA">[</span>692<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEAE">]</span></span></a></sup> and $83.3<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwEAI"> </span>million in the other.<sup about="#mwt2428" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-uphold_695-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"uphold"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-uphold-695"}}'><a href="#cite_note-uphold-695" id="mwEAM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwEAQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEAU">[</span>693<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEAY">]</span></span></a></sup> Federal appeals courts upheld both findings and awards in December 2024 and September 2025, respectively.<sup about="#mwt2431" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-696" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-696"}}'><a href="#cite_note-696" id="mwEAc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwEAg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEAk">[</span>694<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEAo">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt2432" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-uphold_695-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"uphold"}}'><a href="#cite_note-uphold-695" id="mwEAs"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwEAw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEA0">[</span>693<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEA4">]</span></span></a></sup> The U.S. Supreme Court rejected Trump's appeal in the first case.<sup about="#mwt2435" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-697" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-697"}}'><a href="#cite_note-697" id="mwEA8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwEBA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEBE">[</span>695<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEBI">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwEBM">As of October 2024, since the 1970s, at least 28 women<sup about="#mwt2438" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-NewRepublic10.31.24_698-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"NewRepublic10.31.24"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-NewRepublic10.31.24-698"}}'><a href="#cite_note-NewRepublic10.31.24-698" id="mwEBQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwEBU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEBY">[</span>696<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEBc">]</span></span></a></sup> have accused Trump of various acts of sexual misconduct, including rape, sex with minors, sexual assault, physical abuse, kissing and groping without consent, looking under women's skirts, and walking in on naked pageant contestants.<sup about="#mwt2441" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-no26_699-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"no26"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-no26-699"}}'><a href="#cite_note-no26-699" id="mwEBg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwEBk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEBo">[</span>697<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEBs">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="60" id="mwEBw" aria-labelledby="Relationship_with_Jeffrey_Epstein"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Relationship_with_Jeffrey_Epstein">Relationship with Jeffrey Epstein</h3></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt2442" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwEB0" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Relationship of Donald Trump and Jeffrey Epstein"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt2443" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt2442" id="mwEB4">Main article: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Relationship_of_Donald_Trump_and_Jeffrey_Epstein" title="Relationship of Donald Trump and Jeffrey Epstein">Relationship of Donald Trump and Jeffrey Epstein</a></div>

<p id="mwEB8">Trump had a 15-year friendship with <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jeffrey_Epstein" title="Jeffrey Epstein" id="mwECA">Jeffrey Epstein</a>. Persons who knew them at the time said they frequently "hit on" and competed for women.<sup about="#mwt2446" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-700" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-700"}}'><a href="#cite_note-700" id="mwECE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwECI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwECM">[</span>698<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwECQ">]</span></span></a></sup> Media attention and public pressure mounted in 2025, when his administration did not release <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Epstein_files" title="Epstein files" id="mwECU">files relating to Epstein</a>, despite Trump's promise to do so during the 2024 campaign.<sup about="#mwt2449" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-701" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-701"}}'><a href="#cite_note-701" id="mwECY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwECc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwECg">[</span>699<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwECk">]</span></span></a></sup> Some of the files were released in December 2025, mostly heavily redacted.<sup about="#mwt2452" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-702" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-702"}}'><a href="#cite_note-702" id="mwECo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwECs"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwECw">[</span>700<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEC0">]</span></span></a></sup> Roughly three million additional pages released in 2026 included approximately 38,000 references to Trump, his wife, Mar-a-Lago, and other Trump-related terms. References to Trump included "salacious information" and uncorroborated statements about him from witnesses in transcripts made since the early 2000s.<sup about="#mwt2455" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-703" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-703"}}'><a href="#cite_note-703" id="mwEC4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwEC8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEDA">[</span>701<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEDE">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="61" id="mwEDI" aria-labelledby="Wealth"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Wealth">Wealth</h3></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt2456" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwEDM" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Wealth of Donald Trump"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt2457" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt2456" id="mwEDQ">Main article: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wealth_of_Donald_Trump" title="Wealth of Donald Trump">Wealth of Donald Trump</a></div>

<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwEDU"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:1982-_Net_worth_of_Donald_Trump.svg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwEDY"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:1982-_Net_worth_of_Donald_Trump.svg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/1982-_Net_worth_of_Donald_Trump.svg/330px-1982-_Net_worth_of_Donald_Trump.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/1982-_Net_worth_of_Donald_Trump.svg/960px-1982-_Net_worth_of_Donald_Trump.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="1200" data-file-height="675" data-file-type="drawing" height="169" width="300" class="mw-file-element mw-file-upright" style="--mw-file-upright: 1.2" id="mwEDc"/></a><figcaption id="mwEDg">Net worth over time, as estimated by <i id="mwEDk"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forbes" title="Forbes" id="mwEDo">Forbes</a></i><sup about="#mwt2460" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Forbes1982-_704-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Forbes1982-"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Forbes1982--704"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Forbes1982--704" id="mwEDs"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwEDw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwED0">[</span>702<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwED4">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt2463" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Forbes2001-_705-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Forbes2001-"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Forbes2001--705"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Forbes2001--705" id="mwED8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwEEA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEEE">[</span>703<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEEI">]</span></span></a></sup></figcaption></figure>
<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwEEM"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:2017-_Personal_revenue_of_Donald_Trump.svg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwEEQ"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:2017-_Personal_revenue_of_Donald_Trump.svg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/2017-_Personal_revenue_of_Donald_Trump.svg/330px-2017-_Personal_revenue_of_Donald_Trump.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/2017-_Personal_revenue_of_Donald_Trump.svg/960px-2017-_Personal_revenue_of_Donald_Trump.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="1200" data-file-height="675" data-file-type="drawing" height="169" width="300" class="mw-file-element mw-file-upright" style="--mw-file-upright: 1.2" id="mwEEU"/></a><figcaption id="mwEEY">Graph based on <i id="mwEEc"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_York_Times" title="New York Times" class="mw-redirect" id="mwEEg">New York Times</a></i> analysis<sup about="#mwt2464" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-richer_706-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"richer"}}'><a href="#cite_note-richer-706" id="mwEEk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwEEo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEEs">[</span>704<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEEw">]</span></span></a></sup></figcaption></figure><p id="mwEE0"> 
Trump has said he began his career with "a small loan of a million dollars" from his father and that he had to pay it back with interest.<sup about="#mwt2471" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-707" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-707"}}'><a href="#cite_note-707" id="mwEE4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwEE8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEFA">[</span>705<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEFE">]</span></span></a></sup> He borrowed at least $60<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwEFI"> </span>million from his father, did not repay many of the loans, and received $413<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwEFM"> </span>million (2018 equivalent, adjusted for inflation) from his father's real estate company.<sup about="#mwt2474" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-708" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-708"}}'><a href="#cite_note-708" id="mwEFQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwEFU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEFY">[</span>706<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEFc">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt2475" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Tax_Schemes_42-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Tax_Schemes"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Tax_Schemes-42" id="mwEFg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwEFk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEFo">[</span>42<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEFs">]</span></span></a></sup> Posing as a Trump Organization official named "<a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Barron_(pseudonym)" title="John Barron (pseudonym)" class="mw-redirect" id="mwEFw">John Barron</a>", Trump called journalist Jonathan Greenberg in 1984, trying to get a higher ranking on the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forbes_400" title="Forbes 400" id="mwEF0"><i id="mwEF4">Forbes</i> 400</a> list of wealthiest Americans.<sup about="#mwt2478" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-709" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-709"}}'><a href="#cite_note-709" id="mwEF8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwEGA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEGE">[</span>707<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEGI">]</span></span></a></sup></p><div class="paragraphbreak" style="margin-top:0.5em" about="#mwt2465" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwEGM" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"pb","href":"./Template:Pb"},"params":{},"i":0}}]}'></div><p id="mwEGQ">Trump self-reported his net worth over a wide range, from minus<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwEGU"> </span>$900<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwEGY"> </span>million in 1990,<sup about="#mwt2481" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-broke_710-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"broke"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-broke-710"}}'><a href="#cite_note-broke-710" id="mwEGc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwEGg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEGk">[</span>708<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEGo">]</span></span></a></sup> to $10<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwEGs"> </span>billion in 2015.<sup about="#mwt2466" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEJohnston202120_711-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEJohnston202120"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEJohnston202120-711"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Johnston"},"2":{"wt":"2021"},"p":{"wt":"20"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEJohnston202120-711" id="mwEGw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwEG0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEG4">[</span>709<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEG8">]</span></span></a></sup> After his business bankruptcies in the 1990s, his lenders forgave more than 80% of his personal debt and allowed him to keep some of his real estate.<sup about="#mwt2482" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-broke_710-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"broke"}}'><a href="#cite_note-broke-710" id="mwEHA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwEHE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEHI">[</span>708<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEHM">]</span></span></a></sup> In 2015, <i id="mwEHQ"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forbes" title="Forbes" id="mwEHU">Forbes</a></i> estimated his net worth at $4.5<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwEHY"> </span>billion, based on interviews with more than 80 sources.<sup about="#mwt2485" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-712" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-712"}}'><a href="#cite_note-712" id="mwEHc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwEHg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEHk">[</span>710<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEHo">]</span></span></a></sup> In 2026, the magazine set that number at $6.5<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwEHs"> </span>billion and ranked him the 645th wealthiest person in the world.<!-- UPDATE ANNUALLY (around early April) per [[Talk:Donald Trump#C5|consensus 5]]. --><sup about="#mwt2488" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-713" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-713"}}'><a href="#cite_note-713" id="mwEHw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwEH0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEH4">[</span>711<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEH8">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwEIA">Based on the <i id="mwEIE">New York Times</i><span class="nowrap" style="padding-left:0.1em;" about="#mwt2489" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwEII" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"&apos;s","href":"./Template:&apos;s"},"params":{},"i":0}}]}'><span typeof="mw:Entity">'</span>s</span> reporting of Trump's financial disclosures during his presidential terms, the $594<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwEIM"> </span>million in revenue received by Trump's businesses in 2017 was generated mostly from his domestic golf and real estate portfolio. In 2025, the revenue had increased to $2.24<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwEIQ"> </span>billion, with more than half derived from <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cryptocurrency" title="Cryptocurrency" id="mwEIU">cryptocurrency</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meme_coins" title="Meme coins" class="mw-redirect" id="mwEIY">meme coins</a>, while the domestic golf and real estate portfolio's share was down to about a quarter of the total and had increased by a modest 4<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwEIc"> </span>percent since 2017.<sup about="#mwt2492" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-richer_706-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"richer"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-richer-706"}}'><a href="#cite_note-richer-706" id="mwEIg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwEIk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEIo">[</span>704<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEIs">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="62" id="mwEIw" aria-labelledby="Health"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Health">Health</h3></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt2493" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwEI0" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Age and health concerns about Donald Trump"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt2494" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt2493" id="mwEI4">Main article: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Age_and_health_concerns_about_Donald_Trump" title="Age and health concerns about Donald Trump">Age and health concerns about Donald Trump</a></div>

<p id="mwEI8">Trump says he has never drunk alcohol, smoked cigarettes, or used drugs,<!-- DO NOT CHANGE preceding sentence without prior consensus; see [[Talk:Donald Trump#C67|consensus 67]]. --><sup about="#mwt2503" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-714" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-714"}}'><a href="#cite_note-714" id="mwEJA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwEJE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEJI">[</span>712<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEJM">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt2506" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-715" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-715"}}'><a href="#cite_note-715" id="mwEJQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwEJU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEJY">[</span>713<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEJc">]</span></span></a></sup> and that he sleeps about four or five hours a night.<!-- DO NOT CHANGE preceding sentence without prior consensus; see [[Talk:Donald Trump#C67|consensus 67]]. --><sup about="#mwt2509" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-716" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-716"}}'><a href="#cite_note-716" id="mwEJg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwEJk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEJo">[</span>714<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEJs">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt2495" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEAlmondDu2020_717-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEAlmondDu2020"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEAlmondDu2020-717"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Almond"},"2":{"wt":"Du"},"3":{"wt":"2020"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEAlmondDu2020-717" id="mwEJw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwEJ0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEJ4">[</span>715<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEJ8">]</span></span></a></sup> He has called golfing his "primary form of exercise", but usually does not walk the course.<sup about="#mwt2512" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-718" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-718"}}'><a href="#cite_note-718" id="mwEKA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwEKE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEKI">[</span>716<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEKM">]</span></span></a></sup> He considers exercise a waste of energy, believing the body is "like a battery, with a finite amount of energy" which is depleted by exercise.<sup about="#mwt2515" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-719" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-719"}}'><a href="#cite_note-719" id="mwEKQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwEKU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEKY">[</span>717<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEKc">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt2498" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEO'DonnellRutherford1991133_720-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEO&apos;DonnellRutherford1991133"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEO&apos;DonnellRutherford1991133-720"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"O&apos;Donnell"},"2":{"wt":"Rutherford"},"3":{"wt":"1991"},"p":{"wt":"133"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEO'DonnellRutherford1991133-720" id="mwEKg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwEKk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEKo">[</span>718<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEKs">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwEKw">In 2015, his campaign released a letter from his longtime personal physician, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harold_Bornstein" title="Harold Bornstein" id="mwEK0">Harold Bornstein</a>, stating that he would "be the healthiest individual ever elected to the presidency".<sup about="#mwt2518" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-dictation_721-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"dictation"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-dictation-721"}}'><a href="#cite_note-dictation-721" id="mwEK4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwEK8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwELA">[</span>719<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwELE">]</span></span></a></sup> In 2018, Bornstein said Trump had dictated the contents of the letter and that three of Trump's agents had seized his medical records in a February 2017 raid on Bornstein's office.<sup about="#mwt2519" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-dictation_721-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"dictation"}}'><a href="#cite_note-dictation-721" id="mwELI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwELM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwELQ">[</span>719<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwELU">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt2522" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-722" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-722"}}'><a href="#cite_note-722" id="mwELY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwELc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwELg">[</span>720<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwELk">]</span></span></a></sup> Beginning in 2025, Trump appeared to fall asleep during meetings, raising concerns over his stamina.<sup about="#mwt2525" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-723" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-723"}}'><a href="#cite_note-723" id="mwELo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwELs"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwELw">[</span>721<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEL0">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt2528" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-724" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-724"}}'><a href="#cite_note-724" id="mwEL4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwEL8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEMA">[</span>722<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEME">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="63" id="mwEMI" aria-labelledby="Religion"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Religion">Religion</h3></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt2529" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwEMM" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Donald Trump and religion"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt2530" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt2529" id="mwEMQ">Main article: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump_and_religion" title="Donald Trump and religion">Donald Trump and religion</a></div>
<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb mw:ExpandedAttrs" about="#mwt2531" id="mwEMY" data-mw='{"attribs":[["alt",{"html":"alt=Trump in a white robe and red stole before an American flag, with glowing hands healing a man in a hospital bed. Four people, including a nurse and a soldier, look on while Eagles and fighter planes fly overhead, and in the background fireworks erupt over the &lt;a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"./Statue_of_Liberty\" title=\"Statue of Liberty\" id=\"mwEMU\">Statue of Liberty&lt;/a>, and nebulous soldiers and beams of light emanate from clouds.","txt":"Trump in a white robe and red stole before an American flag, with glowing hands healing a man in a hospital bed. Four people, including a nurse and a soldier, look on while Eagles and fighter planes fly overhead, and in the background fireworks erupt over the Statue of Liberty, and nebulous soldiers and beams of light emanate from clouds."}]]}'><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:AI-Generated_Image_depicting_Donald_Trump_as_Jesus_Christ.jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwEMc"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:AI-Generated_Image_depicting_Donald_Trump_as_Jesus_Christ.jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/AI-Generated_Image_depicting_Donald_Trump_as_Jesus_Christ.jpg/250px-AI-Generated_Image_depicting_Donald_Trump_as_Jesus_Christ.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/AI-Generated_Image_depicting_Donald_Trump_as_Jesus_Christ.jpg/500px-AI-Generated_Image_depicting_Donald_Trump_as_Jesus_Christ.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" alt="Trump in a white robe and red stole before an American flag, with glowing hands healing a man in a hospital bed. Four people, including a nurse and a soldier, look on while Eagles and fighter planes fly overhead, and in the background fireworks erupt over the Statue of Liberty, and nebulous soldiers and beams of light emanate from clouds." data-file-width="848" data-file-height="1216" data-file-type="bitmap" height="272" width="190" class="mw-file-element mw-file-upright" style="--mw-file-upright: 0.75" id="mwEMg"/></a><figcaption id="mwEMk">April 2026 post</figcaption></figure>
<p id="mwEMo">Trump said he was a <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Presbyterian" title="Presbyterian" class="mw-redirect" id="mwEMs">Presbyterian</a> and a <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Protestant" title="Protestant" class="mw-redirect" id="mwEMw">Protestant</a> in 2016.<sup about="#mwt2534" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-725" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-725"}}'><a href="#cite_note-725" id="mwEM0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwEM4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEM8">[</span>723<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwENA">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt2537" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-726" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-726"}}'><a href="#cite_note-726" id="mwENE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwENI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwENM">[</span>724<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwENQ">]</span></span></a></sup> In 2020, he said he was a <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nondenominational_Christian" title="Nondenominational Christian" class="mw-redirect" id="mwENU">nondenominational Christian</a>.<sup about="#mwt2540" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-727" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-727"}}'><a href="#cite_note-727" id="mwENY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwENc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwENg">[</span>725<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwENk">]</span></span></a></sup> In his first term, Trump appointed his personal pastor<sup about="#mwt2543" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-728" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-728"}}'><a href="#cite_note-728" id="mwENo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwENs"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwENw">[</span>726<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEN0">]</span></span></a></sup> and spiritual advisor,<sup about="#mwt2546" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-729" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-729"}}'><a href="#cite_note-729" id="mwEN4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwEN8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEOA">[</span>727<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEOE">]</span></span></a></sup> televangelist <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paula_White-Cain" title="Paula White-Cain" id="mwEOI">Paula White-Cain</a>, to the White House <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Office_of_Public_Liaison" title="Office of Public Liaison" class="mw-redirect" id="mwEOM">Office of Public Liaison</a>.<sup about="#mwt2549" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-730" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-730"}}'><a href="#cite_note-730" id="mwEOQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwEOU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEOY">[</span>728<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEOc">]</span></span></a></sup> During his second term, he appointed her as senior advisor of the newly created <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_House_Faith_Office" title="White House Faith Office" class="mw-redirect" id="mwEOg">White House Faith Office</a>.<sup about="#mwt2552" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-731" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-731"}}'><a href="#cite_note-731" id="mwEOk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwEOo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEOs">[</span>729<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEOw">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt2555" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-732" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-732"}}'><a href="#cite_note-732" id="mwEO0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwEO4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEO8">[</span>730<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEPA">]</span></span></a></sup></p> 

<p id="mwEPE">A survey during his first presidency showed that 63<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwEPI"> </span>percent of Americans did not believe that he was religious and that 44<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwEPM"> </span>percent believed he was a Christian.<sup about="#mwt2558" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-733" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-733"}}'><a href="#cite_note-733" id="mwEPQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwEPU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEPY">[</span>731<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEPc">]</span></span></a></sup> 70<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwEPg"> </span>percent of respondents to an April 2026 survey said that he was "not too religious" or "not at all religious".<sup about="#mwt2561" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-734" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-734"}}'><a href="#cite_note-734" id="mwEPk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwEPo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEPs">[</span>732<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEPw">]</span></span></a></sup> Some of Trump's comments on the Bible or Christian practice have led critical observers to suggest that his knowledge of Christianity is superficial or erroneous, and few biographers have described him as deeply or even particularly religious.<sup about="#mwt2564" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-LATimes17_735-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"LATimes17"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-LATimes17-735"}}'><a href="#cite_note-LATimes17-735" id="mwEP0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwEP4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEP8">[</span>733<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEQA">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt2567" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-NPR16_736-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"NPR16"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-NPR16-736"}}'><a href="#cite_note-NPR16-736" id="mwEQE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwEQI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEQM">[</span>734<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEQQ">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwEQU">In April 2026, Trump posted an <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AI_art" title="AI art" id="mwEQY">AI-generated</a> image depicting him as Jesus Christ. The post was deleted following criticism. He later said he had believed the image portrayed him as a doctor.<sup about="#mwt2570" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-737" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-737"}}'><a href="#cite_note-737" id="mwEQc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwEQg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEQk">[</span>735<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEQo">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section></section><section data-mw-section-id="64" id="mwEQs" aria-labelledby="See_also"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id="See_also">See also</h2></div>
<ul id="mwEQw"><li id="mwEQ0"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_awards_and_honors_received_by_Donald_Trump" title="List of awards and honors received by Donald Trump" id="mwEQ4">List of awards and honors received by Donald Trump</a></li>
<li id="mwEQ8"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pseudonyms_used_by_Donald_Trump" title="Pseudonyms used by Donald Trump" id="mwERA">Pseudonyms used by Donald Trump</a></li></ul>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="65" id="mwERE" aria-labelledby="Notes"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id="Notes">Notes</h2></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt2571" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwERI" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"notelist","href":"./Template:Notelist"},"params":{},"i":0}}]}'><style data-mw-deduplicate="TemplateStyles:r1327269900" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" about="#mwt2572" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Reflist/styles.css"}}'>.mw-parser-output .reflist-columns-2{column-width:30em}.mw-parser-output .reflist-columns-3{column-width:25em}body.skin-vector-2022 .mw-parser-output .reflist-columns-2{column-width:27em}body.skin-vector-2022 .mw-parser-output .reflist-columns-3{column-width:22.5em}.mw-parser-output .references[data-mw-group=upper-alpha]{list-style-type:upper-alpha}.mw-parser-output .references[data-mw-group=upper-roman]{list-style-type:upper-roman}.mw-parser-output .references[data-mw-group=lower-alpha]{list-style-type:lower-alpha}.mw-parser-output .references[data-mw-group=lower-greek]{list-style-type:lower-greek}.mw-parser-output .references[data-mw-group=lower-roman]{list-style-type:lower-roman}.mw-parser-output div.reflist-liststyle-upper-alpha .references{list-style-type:upper-alpha}.mw-parser-output div.reflist-liststyle-upper-roman .references{list-style-type:upper-roman}.mw-parser-output div.reflist-liststyle-lower-alpha .references{list-style-type:lower-alpha}.mw-parser-output div.reflist-liststyle-lower-greek .references{list-style-type:lower-greek}.mw-parser-output div.reflist-liststyle-lower-roman .references{list-style-type:lower-roman}</style></span><div about="#mwt2571" id="mwERM">
<div class="mw-references-wrap" typeof="mw:Extension/references" about="#mwt2573" id="mwERQ" data-mw='{"name":"references","attrs":{"group":"lower-alpha","responsive":"1"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'><ol class="mw-references references" data-mw-group="lower-alpha" id="mwERU"><li about="#cite_note-electoral-college-162" id="cite_note-electoral-college-162" data-mw-footnote-number="a"><span class="mw-cite-backlink" id="mwERY"><a href="#cite_ref-electoral-college_162-0" data-mw-group="lower-alpha" rel="mw:referencedBy" id="mwERc"><span class="mw-linkback-text" id="mwERg">↑</span></a></span> <span id="mw-reference-text-cite_note-electoral-college-162" class="mw-reference-text reference-text" data-mw-group="lower-alpha">Presidential elections in the U.S. are decided by the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Electoral_College" title="United States Electoral College" id="mwERk">Electoral College</a>. Each state names a number of electors equal to its representation in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Congress" title="United States Congress" id="mwERo">Congress</a> and (in most states) all electors vote for the winner of their state's popular vote.</span></li>
<li about="#cite_note-165" id="cite_note-165" data-mw-footnote-number="b"><span class="mw-cite-backlink" id="mwERs"><a href="#cite_ref-165" data-mw-group="lower-alpha" rel="mw:referencedBy" id="mwERw"><span class="mw-linkback-text" id="mwER0">↑</span></a></span> <span id="mw-reference-text-cite_note-165" class="mw-reference-text reference-text" data-mw-group="lower-alpha">A Republican president combined with Republican control of both chambers of Congress</span></li>
</ol></div></div>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="66" id="mwER4" aria-labelledby="References"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id="References">References</h2></div>
